1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Life on the FarmEarly 1800s , Meadville, Pennsylvania 
2 CONT
2 CONT After Phineas Dunham married Zerniah Dunham, daughter of his cousin David “Squire” Dunham, they settled near their family place in Piscataway, New Jersey, where their first six children were born. In 1802 they emigrated to the wil
2 CONC ds of Western Pennsylvania and located on his new farm about twelve miles north of Meadville. The six week journey was made in wagons.  As the roads were scarcely more than Indian trails, it is a miracle that they had only one acc
2 CONC ident on the way when the wagon overturned but was not seriously damaged. Their domestic animals were driven with them. Each morning a bucket containing milk was hung under the wagon as they rode and by night the butter was churne
2 CONC d ready for the evening meal.   After many long days and nights full of fear, they arrived at their new home. The purchased farm and its surroundings were so wild and desolate, that Phineas was completely discouraged. Turning to h
2 CONC is wife, he said, "Zerniah, we cannot live here, we will not unpack, and we will go back home again."  " No, Phineas," said his energetic wife, "You brought me here, and here I will stay."   After remaining there three or four yea
2 CONC rs, they moved to a farm just north of Meadville, a beautiful prairie, long known as "Dunham's Flats." They built the house with heavy hewn logs, fashioned after the New Jersey homesteads.  It was one-and-a-half stories high, with
2 CONC  a porch along the entire front covered with vines and bordered with flowers. A wide hall with a plain staircase divided the house and terminated in an outdoor kitchen. On the right it opened into an immense kitchen, whose sand-po
2 CONC lished floor and general order made it perfect for entertaining of company. On each side of the huge fireplace was a large pantry. A large iron crane hung in the fireplace, with its pots and kettles, and in front stood the "Reflec
2 CONC tor," a large tin box with three sides, and open front with two shelves to bake cakes, pies and puddings.  The bread was baked in an iron "Dutch oven," or in the large brick oven outside.   No one, who has ever visited one of thes
2 CONC e old-time homesteads. can forget the cheer and beauty of the light and heat from these large fireplaces, particularly, in the evening, when the pine knots were thrown on, the beautiful play of the light on the brass candlesticks 
2 CONC on the mantel, the brass warming pan on the wall, and pewter kitchen ware polished and shining distributed around, while from the joists hung the guns, game bags, shot pouches and other implements for hunting or defense.   The pri
2 CONC vate sitting and bedroom of the heads of the family opened off the kitchen. On the opposite side of the hall was "the room" kept for formal hospitality, the corner cupboard filled with dainty china, the samplers hung beside the fa
2 CONC mily silhouettes on the wall, the old-fashioned furniture, and the floor covered with a handsome striped carpet of home manufacture.  Adjoining "the room" were two guest chambers, the family bedrooms were upstairs. In that home fu
2 CONC ll of plenty and happiness the Dunham family grew to manhood and womanhood.  The sons worked with their father tilling the ground and preparing the wool and flax for feminine hands. The daughters helped their mother in the manifol
2 CONC d duties of the household, which included the carding and spinning of all yarn, which was afterwards woven into the cloth and flannel for clothing and the linen for sheets and tablecloths. Not all was work and much recreation adde
2 CONC d enjoyment to their lives. Zerniah was a notable housewife and her delightful hospitality was known afar. Her daughters were handsome, her sons were gallant, and an invitation from Mrs. Dunham was considered a compliment highly p
2 CONC rized by the recipient. She lived to be almost one hundred years of age, and died July 16, 1864. Her death was regretted by all who knew her. Her husband died Feb. I0, 1844 at age 80. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Adapted from Dunham Genealogy, English and American Branches of the Dunham Family. Isaac Watson Dunham. Hartford, Conn. 1907 
