2 CONT Rebecca Hunter (Bearse) 1657-1746
2 CONT
2 CONT James Bearse Sr. 1660-1728
2 CONT
2 CONT Came to America in early 1630's with his wife Joan
2 CONT
2 CONT Emigration: To Roxbury, Mass. 1633; to Hartford, Conn., by 1639; to Middletown, Conn. before November 1652.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sgt in the militia - fought Pequot Indians in 1637
2 CONT
2 CONT Represented Middletown in colonial legislature
2 CONT
2 CONT Constable in Middletown
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Christened by the brother of Fairsted's incumbent rector, the Rev. Henry Robinson on 25 May 1609 in Terling, Essex, England. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William was one of sixteen Sargeant-At-Arms assigned as body-guards to protect King Charles I. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was dispatched to 'The New World' (New England Colony) in the 1600's. (All three brothers were dispatched to different areas. William never saw his brother, Charles, again.). William arrived with the Winthrop Fleet aboard the f
2 CONC lagship 'Arbella' to help in the settlement of the Massachusetts Bay Colony at Roxbury (Boston) in 1633. He came from England to Boston with his first wife, Joan. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He became a member of Rev. John Eliot's ('the Indian Missionary') church. In the mid 1630's, after Joan was murdered by the Pequots, he spent a winter on Cape Cod where he presumably met 'Little Dove'. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William went as a Cavalier, assigned to protect the Puritan Colonist from marauding Pequot Indians. William served with Capt. John Underhill after joining Capt. Masons' force at Saybrook, CT. for their mass attack on Pequot Indian
2 CONC s in their Fort at Mystic, in May of 1637. He fought alongside Chief John Hyanno. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1638 he negotiated with John Hyanno for the purchase of Indian lands for the Stratford Colony in CT. After the Pequot Expedition he settled in Hartford (CT.). For his service against the Pequot Indians, he was awarded a lot in 
2 CONC "Souldier's Field" in 1639. This land was located at what is now known today as the north end of Village St. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On or about 1650/1651 he removed with the first settlers to Middletown (CT.). This land originally belonged to the Narragansetts. He was the founder of Middletown & a representative in 1654, 1664 & 1665. His land was located at wh
2 CONC at is known today as the corner of Main & Washington St. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His descendants have been certified as 'Patriots' by the National Society of the Sons of the American Revolution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: 
2 CONT *Cousin Gale Thomas Cornwell 48100 Desert Grove Dr. #12, Indio, CA. 92201  (Gale was a 1st Lieut. in WWII. I have a sketch he completed during the end of the war (June 1945; age 19) in Linz, Austria. Gale is a native of Redwood, T
2 CONC own of Alexandria, N.Y.).
