2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p13/4c/52/e1/d2/5344485f0d7c5baf/william_cornwall_great_migration_profile_original.pdf?hash=99863a3263f8683b3e61598ef2cb1ddf578363fcfb6c6eb589cba26a024186f5.1795420799
2 FORM pdf
2 TITL william cornwall Great Migration Profile.pdf
1 NOTE William Cornwall was born of respectable parents of the middle class and probably served for a time as one of the little corps of sixteen sergeants-at-arms who were in attendance on King Charles I.  He became a Puritan, and emigra
2 CONC ted to New England in company with one brother who went on to Long Island.  It is thought he came from Hertfordshire since many of the early settlers of Hartford, CT were from there and his probable brother, Thomas, who married in
2 CONC  New York in 1642 was from Hertfordshire.  
2 CONT
2 CONT The first record we have of him is found in the Reverend John Eliot's list of the members of his church in Roxbury, Norfolk, MA. in 1633.  In May, 1637, he was one of 77 soldiers who attacked and all but extinguished the Pequot In
2 CONC dians in their fort at Mystic.  He soon settled in Hartford after this event.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1638 he accompanied Mr. Hopkins and Mr. Goodwin in an expedition sent by the Colony of Connecticut to consummate the purchase of Stratford from the Indians.  In 1639, when the land records of Hartford begin, William Cornwall ha
2 CONC d a house lot of 8 acres in the village, "no. 54, west of South street, south from the Lane."  
2 CONT
2 CONT Although an early settler of Hartford, he was not one of the original proprietors of the town.  We find his name third in a list of 42 householders who, in 1640, held their rights of pasturage on the Common and of drawing wood and
2 CONC  water "by the courtise of the town."
2 CONT
2 CONT In the earliest record of his land in Hartford, dated February 1639, he is styled "William Cornwall, Sergeant-at-Arms."  
2 CONT
2 CONT He removed before March 5, 1648, from the village of Hartford to the east side of the Connecticut River at Hocanum.  On that date, he bound his second son, William, who was 7 years old, to Susanna Hooker of Hartford, widow of the 
2 CONC Reverend Thomas Hooker, with provision for his education.  The original indenture in the handwriting of Governor Edward Hopkins is still in existence.
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 6, 1649, he was sued by Thomas Osmore and judgment and a fine was rendered against him.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1650 or 1651 he removed with the first settlers to Middletown where his house lot in the center of town consisted of 5 acres with ten acres on the opposite side of the street.  His lands in Middletown were first formerly record
2 CONC ed February 30, 1657, though a grant to his is alluded to as early as March 15, 1652.  The total amount recorded to him is 903 acres.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1666 he received a grant of land in East Hartford of 24 acres.   In 1667 the General Court at Hartford "freed William Cornwell's head from the paying of rates." On October 3, 1668 he joined the recently organized church in Midd
2 CONC letown. In 1670, he was assessed in Middletown on £160, which was the 5th largest assessment in the list of 52 householders.  In 1673, he was rated at £231.  His will and inventory are on file in the Hartford Probate office.
