1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Roger Minott Sherman'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Although there were other newspaper articles concerning Roger Minott Sherman published in the New York Times, 1876-1905, here are some of the most interesting:
2 CONT     *  *  *
2 CONT New York Times, February 18, 1876, report from St. Louis regarding the "Trial of General Babcock." Several generals and other prominent men were involved. Roger Sherman's involvement is briefly described.
2 CONT     *  *  *
2 CONT New York Times, February 25, 1876
2 CONT
2 CONT ROGER M. SHERMAN.; A LETTER FROM UNITED STATES DISTRICT ATTORNEY BLISS. 
2 CONT
2 CONT United States District Attorney Bliss has sent the following letter fully explaining the object of the attendance of Assistant United States District Attorney Roger M. Sherman at the Babcock trial [see notes regarding Gen. Orville
2 CONC  E. Babcock below this article]:
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir: The time seems to have now come when I can with propriety say something in behalf of a deserving subordinate.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. R. M. Sherman has been charged by the press with being a "spy of Babcock," and having betrayed the interests of the Government. No proof was ever presented to sustain the charges, but in these days proof is not needed where th
2 CONC e charge is against a public officer. The procedure is to make the charge, and then call upon the officer to prove his innocence. If he does not speedily answer, he is condemned for his silence. If he does answer, he must use due 
2 CONC meekness or his guilt is established. If he has the requisite meekness, and proves himself not guilty, the press condescendingly admits that it "may have been misinformed, though the circumstances are suspicious."
2 CONT
2 CONT Let me state the facts in Mr. Sherman's case. As a result of the whiskey prosecutions in the West, a large quantity of distilled spirits was, by order from the Commissioner of Internal Revenue, seized in this City. Judging from th
2 CONC e stamps, either it was "crooked," or other spirits used at the West with the same stamps was so. I made frequent applications for information to the District Attorneys at St. Louis and elsewhere, and the Commissioner of Internal 
2 CONC Revenue directed the revenue officers to aid me. Still, everyone out there seemed so absorbed in his own matters that I could get no information. When I saw that the guilty men were beginning to tell all they knew — or more — I be
2 CONC lieved that if I had a competent man on the spot he could get much valuable information for the Government to be used here. I therefore applied to the Attorney General for leave to send Mr. Sherman, who had charge of internal reve
2 CONC nue matters in my office, to various places in the West. The permission was granted, and in due course the records of the civil and criminal courts here will, I believe, show how faithfully and successfully he has thus far perform
2 CONC ed his mission.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among other papers obtained by him — from distillers, and not from Mr. Dyer or any officer of the Government — were some letters of one Hoge, an absconding revenue agent. They showed that he gave information from Washington to the
2 CONC  Whiskey Ring at the West, and were therefore inconsistent with the theory of the prosecution against Gen. Babcock, that it was he who was relied upon for the information. Now, having these papers, what was it the duty of Mr. Sher
2 CONC man, either as a citizen or an officer, to do? Was it to turn them over to the prosecution and enable them to be suppressed, if a desire to do existed? It seems to me not. The people of this country do not want any man convicted b
2 CONC y the suppression of testimony. They want the whole truth brought out, no matter which side it helps or hurts.  Hoge's letter could be of no use to the prosecution against Gen. Babcock, while the feeling manifested by District Att
2 CONC orney Dyer, both toward Gen. Babcock and Mr. Sherman, seemed to show that no magnanimity was to be expected from him. Mr. Sherman, therefore, brought the letters to the notice of Gen. Babcock's counsel, but he at the same time too
2 CONC k care that Mr. Dyer should be allowed any use that he wanted to make of them, provided he did not suppress them. This did not suit. Mr. Dyer notified the Attorney General that Sherman was a spy. One of his assistants gave such a 
2 CONC statement to the press, requesting them to say they got it from another person, and when the manuscript was found in the Assistant's handwriting he was compelled to hear himself publicly branded as a liar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Dyer subpoenaed Mr. Sherman as a witness' then, though challenged, did not dare to call him, and finally wrote Mr. Sherman a letter of apology, and telegraphed a halting retraction to the Attorney General.
2 CONT
2 CONT Such are the facts, as I believe them to exist. If so, I submit that Mr. Sherman has not only done nothing censurable, but has done that for which every honorable man ought to commend him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Your obedient servant,
2 CONT
2 CONT GEORGE BLISS, United States Attorney.
2 CONT _____________
2 CONT
2 CONT Wikipedia's article re Orville E. Babcock is at http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Orville_Babcock
2 CONT
2 CONT It provides context for the NY Times article above, telling Gen. Babcock's life story, including his impressive achievements during the Civil War, service to Gen. and Pres. U.S, Grant, and the court case of 1876, in which Babcock 
2 CONC was indicted as a member of the "Whiskey Ring," and found innocent.
2 CONT
2 CONT A New York Times article, June 4, 1884, reported Gen. Babcock's death in a boating accident near Jacksonville, Florida.
2 CONT _________
2 CONT
2 CONT     *  *  *
2 CONT The New York Times, August 26, 1880
2 CONT
2 CONT THE YACHT INTREPID LOST.
2 CONT
2 CONT The sloop-yacht Intrepid, while on her way from Newport for New York, was struck by a squall off Hart Island, at 1:30 o'clock yesterday afternoon upset and sunk in 20 fathoms of water. Everything was lost, the crew themselves bare
2 CONC ly escaping. The yacht was of 9 tons burden, and owned by Mr. Roger M. Sherman, the lawyer of this City.      
2 CONT     *  *  *
2 CONT The New York Times, October 13, 1883
2 CONT
2 CONT THE SURETIES IN A DIVORCE SUIT.; A CASE IN WHICH EX-GOV. BAGLEY'S DAUGHTER IS INTERESTED.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Detroit, Mich., on Nov. 13, 1876, Miss Florence Bagley, daughter of ex-Gov. Bagley, of that State, was married to Roger M. Sherman, of this City [New York], who was then an Assistant United States District Attorney. About a yea
2 CONC r and a half ago Mr. and Mrs. Sherman separated, and the latter went back to Michigan. Soon after their separation Mr. Sherman began a suit in Connecticut for a divorce and for a decree awarding him the custody of his two children
2 CONC . Renewed attention is drawn in this City to this litigation by some statement made yesterday by Charles W. Brooke, counsel for Mr. Sherman, before Judge O'Gorman, in Superior Court, Chambers. Some time ago Robert Robertson began 
2 CONC a suit to obtain $320 from Mr. Sherman, asserting that he was entitled to that sum as compensation for having taken lucrative business to Mr. Sherman's office. In that suit an attachment was granted against Mr. Sherman's property,
2 CONC  and was sustained on the ground that he was not a resident of this State. The sureties given on the bond by Robertson at the time he procured the attachment were directed to appear before Judge O'Gorman yesterday to justify. The 
2 CONC parties having appeared in court, and Judge O'Gorman being too busy to proceed personally with the matter, Mr. Brooke asked for an adjournment. He said he wished the sureties to be examined in open court, for the reason that he be
2 CONC lieved the suit against Mr. Sherman to be without merits, and to have been begun with the mere intent to procure, if possible, an adjudication to the effect that Mr. Sherman was not a resident of Connecticut, where he is suing his
2 CONC  wife. Such an adjudication, he said, would be used to influence the course of the divorce suit to the disadvantage of Mr. Sherman. As to the bondsmen presented by the plaintiff, Robert Robertson, one of them, John Robertson, was 
2 CONC a person who had left Scotland as an insolvent debtor, and who was now a clerk at a small salary. He had admitted that Mrs. Florence Bagley Sherman had indemnified him against any loss as a surety by giving him her personal bond f
2 CONC or $30,000. Judge O'Gorman adjourned the examination of the sureties until next Thursday.   
2 CONT
2 CONT     *  *  *
2 CONT The New York Times, March 29, 1884
2 CONT
2 CONT MR. SHERMAN'S SUIT FOR DIVORCE; HIS VIEW OF THE CHARACTER OF MRS. GOV. BAGLEY.
2 CONT
2 CONT For about two years Roger M. Sherman, a well-known young lawyer of this city, has been trying to procure a decree separating him from his wife, Florence B. Sherman, the eldest daughter of the late Gov. John J. Bagley, of Michigan,
2 CONC  and awarding him the custody of their two children, a boy and a girl. The couple were married in 1876 in Detroit, Mich., with great pomp, and they came to this city to live, where Sherman was an Assistant United States District A
2 CONC ttorney. In 1881 Mrs. Sherman went to Detroit to her mother's home with her children, and has since remained there. The next year her husband brought a suit in Connecticut for an absolute divorce on the ground that she was a habit
2 CONC ual drunkard, but the complaint was dismissed for lack of jurisdiction. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Sherman waited until January last and then began a suit in the Supreme Court here for a limited divorce on the ground that his wife had abandoned him. Last week Mr. Sherman obtained from Judge Lawrence an order directing his w
2 CONC ife to show cause why she should not be ordered to bring her children within the physical jurisdiction of the court and obey any order it might make respecting their disposition pending the litigation. The order to show cause was 
2 CONC based on a voluminous affidavit, made by Mr. Sherman, and on sworn declarations of other persons, to the effect that Mrs. Sherman was addicted to the use of liquor. Mr. Sherman averred that his wife was not only a drunkard but tha
2 CONC t she had told him that she hated him, and that she loved another man whom she had known in her girlhood. She had discussed Zola's novel "Nana" with male visitors at her home in this city while he was absent, and had spoken in an 
2 CONC indelicate manner of marital relations. He had been deceived and lured into his marriage with her, notwithstanding that he loved her. He considered that her mother was the main actor in the fraud practiced upon him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Bagley, Mrs. Sherman thought, was responsible for her daughter's misconduct, and his own domestic sorrows. By her own statements to him Mrs. Bagley had given her children liquor in their childhood, and thus aroused the appeti
2 CONC te of which Mrs. Sherman is possessed. Mrs. Bagley wished to manage his household, to have her name conferred on his daughter, and to nourish his children, as she had nourished her own, with liquor, but he would not allow her to. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Sherman formed a dislike for Mrs. Bagley on account of her peculiar advanced notions of religion and morality, her frivolity, and the deception which he knew she had practiced on her husband. He therefore was unwilling to have
2 CONC  her exert any influence over his children. His dislike became apparent to her, and she therefore chose to make him unhappy through the extraordinary control she had over his wife. Mr. Sherman avers that his wife and mother-in-law
2 CONC  are unfit to have the custody of his children at all, and he asks to have them taken out of their hands until this suit is determined.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the motion was called for hearing by Judge Lawrence, in Supreme Court, Chambers, yesterday morning, Mr. Charles W. Brooke, counsel for Mr. Sherman, said that neither side was ready, and he asked for an adjournment until April
2 CONC  9. Mr. D. B. Ogden, who appeared for Mrs. Sherman, said that he wished the matter adjourned until April 11, and also wished to give notice of a motion then to be made to strike out parts of Mr. Sherman's affidavit as scandalous. 
2 CONC Mr. Brooke objected to a delay beyond April 9. He said that since Mr. Sherman began his suit Mrs. Sherman had begun one against him in Detroit, the process in which was returnable on April 10. Mr. Sherman had not appeared in the M
2 CONC ichigan action, and it might happen that if the hearing should be adjourned until April 11 the court here would then be confronted with some sort of order, taken by default in Michigan, apparently depriving it of jurisdiction and 
2 CONC raising questions that would complicate the case.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Judge Lawrence remarked that if a delay of six weeks had been asked it would not have been so suspicious as was, under the circumstances, the request for adjournment until the day after the Michigan process. He adjourned until Ap
2 CONC ril 9 the hearing of the motion to strike out parts of Mr. Sherman's affidavit, as well as that for the production of the children in court.
2 CONT     *  *  *
2 CONT The New York Times, May 15, 1884
2 CONT
2 CONT MRS. SHERMAN GETS A DIVORCE.
2 CONT
2 CONT DETROIT, Mich., May 14.--The matrimonial troubles of Mr. and Mrs. Roger Sherman have long been a source of regret to their Detroit friends. When Mr. Sherman, then Assistant District Attorney for New York, came here eight years ago
2 CONC  and wedded the late Gov. Bagley's eldest daughter, Florence, the alliance promised to be an exceptionally happy one. But grave differences sprang up and the divorce court was sought. Mr. Sherman sued for divorce in the East alleg
2 CONC ing drunkenness and other offenses on the part of his wife. Reports of the trial were telegraphed all over the country, and the case excited considerable comment, because of the social prominence of the parties. A few weeks ago an
2 CONC  interview with Mr. Sherman was published in a New York paper in which he made a bitter attack upon Mrs. Bagley. Pending the issue of the Eastern case Mrs. Bagley quietly instituted a divorce suit in the Wayne County Circuit Court
2 CONC , in this city. The bill was filed in December last and was hidden away from public gaze, while the papers in ordinary cases are open to anybody's inspection. The allegations were adultery and desertion. Employees of the Ebbit Hou
2 CONC se in Washington, testified to Mr. Sherman's visit there under the name of Shepherd with a woman whom he registered as his wife. It was also alleged in the wife's bill that Mr. Sherman had been guilty of like offenses with other w
2 CONC omen in and about New-York and upon his yacht at Newport. Mrs. Sherman yesterday testified that the defendant had not contributed to her support since the separation. Judge Chambers granted a divorce and awarded Mrs. Sherman custo
2 CONC dy of her two children.
2 CONT     *  *  *
2 CONT The New York Times, May 26, 1885
2 CONT
2 CONT COMPLICATING A DIVORCE SUIT.; A QUESTION OF ALLEGED PERJURY IN THE SHERMAN LITIGATION.
2 CONT
2 CONT In February last the suit of Kate M. Sherman for an absolute divorce from Walter R. Sherman was tried before Judge Beach in the Supreme Court. Judge Beach gave her a decree of divorce and awarded her the custody of her son, Walter
2 CONC  M. Sherman, 9 years of age. One of the main witnesses against Sherman was Robert Robertson, formerly a partner of his brother, Roger M. Sherman. Soon after the trial Robertson was arrested on a charge of perjury, and was committe
2 CONC d by a Police Justice for trial, Roger M. Sherman and Charles W. Brooke having appeared against him as the representatives of the District Attorney. Charles W. Brooke is counsel for Roger M. Sherman in his divorce litigation with 
2 CONC Florence M. Sherman, a daughter of ex-Gov. John J. Bagley, of Michigan. Robertson having been stigmatized as a perjurer, Messrs. Sherman and Brooke, as counsels for Walter R. Sherman, moved to have the decree of divorce against hi
2 CONC m vacated and for a new trial.
2 CONT
2 CONT The motion was heard by Judge Van Vorst yesterday. In their affidavits the Shermans declared that one James Annette swore falsely on the trial as well as Robertson. Walter Sherman swore that the charges of infidelity against him w
2 CONC ere untrue, and he was supported by his brother in the averment that his wife had treated him badly. One of the instances of ill-treatment was that she struck him in the face with a glass vase, which cut him deeply when it shivere
2 CONC d to bits.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Sherman made a long affidavit in response to the moving papers. She averred that her husband had inserted matters of malicious slander in his affidavits respecting her, and that his brother had joined him in slandering her un
2 CONC der cover of his privilege speaking in court, as well as in an affidavit. She asserted that Robertson and Annette had told the truth, and that the proceedings in the Police Court against Robertson were a mockery. Next she told how
2 CONC  she had eloped from Ithaca, when she was only 15 years old, with Walter R. Sherman, who had been a student at Cornell. They were married here, and very soon after their marriage he began to ill-treat her. She was compelled to lea
2 CONC ve him, but she returned to him at his earnest solicitation. Finally, in 1882, he left her, she said, because the proof of his infidelity was at hand. He took her child from her, and afterward put him in boarding schools, but fail
2 CONC ed to pay for his tuition, and she had to pay for it out of her small store. In February last she got possession of the boy after the burning of a school he was in at Chappaqua, and she has since retained him under the decree of t
2 CONC he court. Affidavits made by Robertson and Annette, repeating the testimony given by them on the trial, and insisting that Walter R. Sherman was utterly immoral, were also read by ex-Judge Richard Busteed [see the short bio of Ric
2 CONC hard Busteed below], who appeared for Mrs. Sherman. At the conclusion of the reading of the affidavits of Robertson and Annette Mr. Brooke moved that they be stricken out as scandalous, because they contained matter reflecting on 
2 CONC Mr. Roger M. Sherman. Judge Van Vorst reserved his decision on that question and will hear more on the motion for a new trial on Thursday.
2 CONT
2 CONT     *  *  *
2 CONT The New York Times, May 29, 1885, page 8
2 CONT
2 CONT SHARP TALK IN COURT.; LIVELY PROCEEDINGS IN THE SHERMAN DIVORCE CASE.
2 CONT
2 CONT Liveliness was the feature of the proceedings before Judge Van Vorst, in Supreme Court, Special Term, yesterday, on the motion for a new trial of the suit of Kate M. Sherman for an absolute divorce from Walter R. Sherman. Gen. Ric
2 CONC hard Busteed [see the short bio of Richard Busteed below] had recalled some of the fire of his youth, and, as the champion of Mrs. Sherman, revealed the qualities that made "Dick" Busteed famous at the bar 30 years ago. In his arg
2 CONC ument against a new trial poetical quotations jostled law points, and occasionally he stopped to shake the finger of scorn at Roger M. Sherman, who appears, with Charles W. Brooke, as counsel for his brother Walter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  The proceedings were opened with the striking-out by Judge Van Vorst of some passages in the affidavits presented last Monday on behalf of Mrs. Sherman that scandalized Roger M. Sherman. Then an affidavit made by Walter R. Sherma
2 CONC n was read by Mr. Brooke. In it Walter denied that he had treated his wife with violence more than once. That one time he pushed her violently because she was playing a tattoo on his face with her boot heel. He denied that the all
2 CONC egations of infidelity made against him were true, renewed his former charges of unfaithfulness against his wife, and declared that his sister-in-law, Annie Lavelle, had sworn falsely.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Then Gen. Busteed referred to Walter R. Sherman as a "travesty of a dude," to whom it was more natural to lie than to tell the truth, and who had fed recently on a diet of perjury administered to him recently by his brother and c
2 CONC ounsel, Roger M. Sherman.
2 CONT
2 CONT ""Beast and blackguard!" hissed Roger M.
2 CONT
2 CONT "You are both beast and blackguard," retorted Busteed, "and a ruffian and a liar to boot."
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger answered in kind, and added that it was well for the former Brigadier General of Volunteers that he was talking under the protection of the court. Judge Van Vorst having chided both, Gen. Busteed went on to glorify his fair 
2 CONC client, and dropped into poetry to such an extent as to rouse the disgust of Roger M. Sherman, who said:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Pshaw! I used to do that when I was but 16 years old."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Oh, wretch, without a fear, without a thought
2 CONT
2 CONT Save joy above the ruin thou hast wrought!"
2 CONT
2 CONT This was the metrical answer of the orator, with his fist upraised toward his calm interrupter.
2 CONT
2 CONT And thus the discussion went on until Judge Van Vorst wearily said that the papers could be handed to him for consideration.
2 CONT
2 CONT     *  *  *
2 CONT THE NEW YORK TIMES, December 12, 1899
2 CONT
2 CONT ON DEATH BY ELECTRICITY; Condemned Men, R.M. Sherman Says, Undergo Agony. || THEIR VERY FEAR THE CAUSE || Society of Medical Jurisprudence Stirred by a Paper on the Science of Capital Punishment.
2 CONT
2 CONT Roger M. Sherman read a paper on "The Law and Science of Capital Punishment by Electricity" before the Society of Medical Jurisprudence at its annual meeting in the Academy of Medicine last night. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Sherman held that the present method of executing murderers in this State was likely to produce great physical suffering to the man thereby put to death.
2 CONT
2 CONT His view was that the condemned man is in a perspiration when the current is turned on, and that this condition results in the turning of his body into a voltaic arc, the outer portion of which must be thoroughly dired up before t
2 CONC he inner portion is penetrated by the deadly current, intense heat being generated meanwhile.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Sherman quoted a celebrated French treatise on signs of death, which cited the case of a man's heart action being suspended four minutes by an electric shock without death following, and another of a man and animal surviving a
2 CONC  shock of 200,000 volts. he said, in part:
2 CONT
2 CONT  "The prisoner arriving at the chair is probably, if not always necessarily, in a sensible perspiration, a cold sweat. I believe the medical witnesses, who know how little electricity they wish to receive, and who doubtless exerci
2 CONC se a wise caution in proceeding with the autopsy, can hardly have assisted at such an event without themselves having taken a Turkish bath. How much more the victim!
2 CONT
2 CONT "This surface condition of moisture must be a factor for consideration. The moistened sponge to facilitate the current comes in contact with a moistened skin. Is there any reason to suppose that the general law which, especially i
2 CONC n the case of electricity, seeks the path of least resistance will not convert the surface of the victim into a voltaic arc?
2 CONT
2 CONT Will the electric force rather penetrate through that non-conductor, the bony parts of the skull? ow will it enter the blood vessels, until it shall have exhausted the receptivity of the surface moisture and generated in its place
2 CONC  a heat, necessarily increasing as resistance increases?
2 CONT
2 CONT "The voltaic arc which generates heat used for fusing the most refractory substances, which, when made with carbon, gives us our incandescent light, is hardly the condition to create upon the surface of the human body as a mode of
2 CONC  inflicting instantaneous and painless death, and in the name of humanity."
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Sherman adverted to the fact that in cases of execution in this State the current has had to be turned on from two to four times to kill the subject. He continued:
2 CONT
2 CONT "I suppose it to be certain that the motor nerves may be paralyzed when the coordinate sensory nerves may experience sensation. Is there any doubt that paralytics are frequently in that condition? Is there any rational doubt that 
2 CONC a man incapable of motion or speech from paralysis may yet be conscious of his surroundings. This powerful force holds the motor nerves in its grip, everyone tense with an involuntary paralysis and incapable of motion. Do the nerv
2 CONC e messengers of pain, given to warn men of danger, and mercifully dulled when the limit of endurance is reached, continue for a time their functions?"
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Sherman concluded with the suggestion that it were better for society "to set the example to the murderer" than "demand that he shall begin with a reverence for life."
2 CONT
2 CONT President S. B. Livingston, who acted as Chairman, said, in inviting discussion that the society had been instrumental in securing the introduction of electrocution in this State.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. H. J. Garrigues proposed the guillotine as a good substitute for electricity. "Electricity," said he, "is not instantaneous or precise. But if you have a well-made and well-oiled guillotine it is absolutely certain that the ma
2 CONC n executed will die at once."
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the speakers asked a question about the immediate effect of a shock which was heavy enough to prove fatal, and Mr. Sherman contended that that effect would not necessarily be unconsciousness. He cited the case of a lineman 
2 CONC who was held by a live wire, and was able to give directions for his release. "The time will yet come," said he, "with our present system of dynamos, when some victim will be found in whose case the dynamo must be supplemented by 
2 CONC an axe."
2 CONT ---------------------------
2 CONT Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Busteed article downloaded 2010 from Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard Busteed (February 16, 1822 – September 14, 1898) was an attorney and soldier who served in the Union Army during the American Civil War. [photo of him in uniform] He was a lawyer before and after the war, and also served a
2 CONC s the U.S. District Judge of Alabama from 1863 – 1874. He became highly controversial in that position, and resigned to avoid likely impeachment  by the U.S. House of Representatives.
2 CONT
