1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Still
2 CONT ==Birth==
2 CONT John Still was born around 1777. He was the son of https://www.geni.com/people/John-Still/6000000002844423175 and https://www.geni.com/people/Phebe-Wade-Still/6000000002844439138.
2 CONT ==Immigration==
2 CONT Moved to Hatley from Kingston, Vermont with extended family - mother  https://www.geni.com/people/Phebe-Wade-Still/6000000002844439138, brother https://www.geni.com/people/Durant-Still/6000000002844419268 and his wife https://www.
2 CONC geni.com/people/Lucy-Still/6000000002844510241, their offspring, and possibly John's wife Hannah.
2 CONT Hatley, Quebec, Canada
2 CONT ==Marriage==
2 CONT https://www.geni.com/people/John-Still/6000000226759137824 and Hannah married
2 CONT ==Children==
2 CONT # John Still (1801 - 1847) married Abigail Hovey 
2 CONT # Sophia Still married John Kent and Robert Brash
2 CONT # Amanda Still (1811 - after 1881) single  lived with Anna in 1881
2 CONT # https://www.geni.com/people/Hannah-Still/5705574279700028970 (1807 - 1899) married https://www.geni.com/people/Samuel-H-Mabee/5705571531740041478
2 CONT # Phebe Still (1815 - 1831) single
2 CONT # Anna Still (1816 - after 1881) married Samuel Miller and Richard Hoitt
2 CONT # Mary Still married Lucius Campbell Thomas
2 CONT ==Deeds==
2 CONT * No. 6147 - PROVINCE OF UPPER CANADA . GRANT to John Still of the Township of Whitby in the County of York the Home - - District Innkeeper . The East half of Lot number four in the Eighth concession of the Town ship of Brock cont
2 CONC aining 100 acres Under the Regulations of the 6th July 1884 G. O. No. 1061 Order in Council 5th May 1819 Survey or Generals Line of Location of the 5th February 1820 Under the Administration of Penge of 1 Maitland R .. B. Lieut Go
2 CONC odman Patent Fee and Survey paid W B Robinson Attorney General , The Settlement Duty performed . To the Surveyor General , & c. & c. Dated the 16th day of October 1823 R. G. ---
2 CONT
2 CONT John Still, an innkeeper from Whitby Township in York County, was granted the east half of Lot number four in the eighth concession of Brock Township, totaling 100 acres. The grant was administered under various regulations and or
2 CONC ders, notably those from May 1819 and February 1820, with the patent finalized on October 16, 1823, after settlement duties were fulfilled. Key officials involved included W. B. Robinson, Attorney General, and Lieut. Goodman, with
2 CONC  oversight from Penge and Maitland. The legal and administrative processes ensured all fees and requirements were appropriately settled.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Deed of Bargain - 9 October 1839
2 CONT John Still, dec'd
2 CONT Heirs
2 CONT Sophia Kent, widow of John Kent
2 CONT Anna Miller, wife of Samuel Miller
2 CONT Amanda Still, spinster
2 CONT Mary Still, spinster
2 CONT Hannah Mabee and Samuel Mabee
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-C3H6-59N8-F?view=fullText&keywords=Samuel%20Mabee%2CCanada&lang=en&groupId=
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Will==
2 CONT Gave only son John 100 acres of land. Ordered that his mother Phebe Wade Still 'shall have a good and comfortable support or maintenance out of my estate.' Left wife Hannah property and all personal belongings. Rest of estate divi
2 CONC ded between six daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-C3H6-RQ6J?view=fullText&keywords=John%20Still%2CCanada%2CYork&lang=en&groupId=
2 CONT
2 CONT Folder relating to Estate
2 CONT PARCEL 58 : 1839 
2 CONT 40 - 2024 
2 CONT Kent Sophia 8 - OTHERS 
2 CONT RG 40
2 CONT
2 CONT "Toronto, Ontario, Canada records," images, FamilySearch (https://www.familysearch.org/ark:/61903/3:1:3Q9M-CSVK-F31Q-Y?view=explore : Feb 11, 2026), image 852 of 1109; .
2 CONT Image Group Number: 008126198
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Obituary==
2 CONT An obituary in the Christian Guardian (York), Canada, written by William Marsh, September 19, 1832
2 CONT
2 CONT "Died in Whitby of Asiatic Cholera on the 30th ultimo., Deacon John Still, in the 50th [sic] year of his age. He had been troubled with a relax for several days but felt no alarm until 12 o'clock on the day of his disease when he 
2 CONC was stirred with spasms and was very soon in the last stage of Cholera. He sank very rapidly insomuch that at 3 o'clock p.m. he fell asleep never to awake until the last trumpet sounds, but we trust his immortal soul has wafted it
2 CONC s way to that happy place prepared by our exalted Savior for all his believing children.
2 CONT Brother Still became a member of the Baptist Church in Hatley, Lower Canada, about 30 years ago. About 23 years ago he removed from thence to Whitby, where he immediately connected himself with the church in the place, at that tim
2 CONC e consisting of but few members, and destitute of a pastor. In this destitute situation he always took an active part in maintaining public worship; and sparing no pains in obtaining occasional preaching from neighboring ministers
2 CONC . Soon after his settlement in Whitby he was appointed Deacon of the Church in which capacity he has given to those who knew him best a satisfactory evidence of his having in a good degree the necessary qualification for that resp
2 CONC onsible office of taking care of the temporal welfare of the Church, which he uniformly attended to in the proper season. He was a perservering man in every thing he put his hand to - few men in our country have been more generall
2 CONC y useful or more prosperous in their undertakings. Our much respected brother was in easy circumstances, and his house was a home for the weary pilgrim. Ministers of all denominations were made welcome at his hospitable board. He 
2 CONC was no bigot, but well established in the faith which he professed. His house was a house of prayer - the poor were not sent empty away from his door - he assisted the widow and fatherless in their distresses - he was always ready
2 CONC  to lend a helping hand to benevolent institutions. In short, he maintained the form, and we trust felt the power of true religion. The deceased has left a disconsolate widow, six children, the Church containing not far from one h
2 CONC undred members, and a large circle of friends to mourn their loss."
