2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/LTGC-F2R Thomas was a man of means, position and influence. From Hatten's Original List we learn that on April 29, 1635 Thomas age 50 and his wife Dorothy 45, and their 7 children w
2 CONC ere registered from the Port of London to New England on the ship Elizabeth Ann in 1636. Thomas and his family joined the party of Rev. Thomas Hooker and 100 others which departed from Newtown which later became Cambridge to form 
2 CONC a new settlement on the Connecticut River. Thomas and Robert Lord became an original proprietor and one of the first settlers of Hartford Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT From Find-A-Grave:
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early part of the seventeenth century, there were four "Lords" who settled in this country: Nathan Lord landed in Kittery, Me; Thomas Lord, who landed in Boston, Mass.; William Lord who landed in Salem, Mass.' and Robert Lo
2 CONC rd who landed at Ipswich, Mass. They were not brothers, but they were probably descended from the same "Lord" family back in history.
2 CONT
2 CONT The name, Lord, was derived with the Anglo-Saxon Ord or Ored, a governor, with the prefix L' or Le, denoting the person or place. The Gaelic ar. lord--high, lofty, the prime chief, superior.
2 CONT
2 CONT "The Descendants of Thomas For, an original proprietor an founder of Hartford, Conn. in 1636" ____________________________________ The son of Richard & Jeane Lord of County Towchester, Northampton, England, he married first Doroth
2 CONC y Buckley, daughter of Edward Buckley & sister of Rev. Peter Buckley. After her death, he married Dorothy Bird 23 Feb 1610/1611 in Townschester. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Lord aged 50, Dorothy Lord aged 46 (his second wife), son Thomas aged 16, Ann aged 14, William aged 12, John aged 10, Robert aged 9, Aymie aged 6, and Dorothy aged 4, came from England on The "Elizabeth Ann" to Cambridge Ma
2 CONC ss, 1635. [Hotten's "Original Lists"] The oldest son, Richard had come to America in advance to find a place to settle.
2 CONT
2 CONT They joined Rev. Thomas Hooker's party leaving Newtown and traveling though wilderness and rough terrain for nearly a fortnight purchasing land from the leader of the Suckiage Indians, Chief Sequassen where they founded Hartford, 
2 CONC Conn. in 1636. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas was an original proprietor of Hartford, Conn. His name is on the Founder's Monument in the Ancient Cemetery where he is buried. He and his family lived on the north side of the highway on the bank of the Little River (later
2 CONC  Wells Street) near Gov. Haynes, Rev. Hooker, Mr. Goodwin and Gov. Wyllys. His sons, Richard and Thomas had the lots next to him. Thomas & Dorothy (Bird) Lord were the parents of at least nine children all being born in England.
2 CONT
