2 CONC icial Calvinism. Instead of retracting, he returned to England in 1652 where he remained for the rest of his life. Pynchon built a trading post at Enfield Falls, which would become Springfield, Massachusetts from which he exported
2 CONC  between 4,000 to 6,000 beaver pelts a year between 1636 and 1652. The profits enabled him to retire to England as a wealthy man. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He is an ancestor of the novelist Thomas Pynchon and the actress Fay Wray.
2 CONT
2 CONT His daughter, Mary Pynchon, married Elizur Holyoke.
2 CONT
