1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Some years after William Pynchon lost his wife Ann Andrews to scurvy, 1630, he married the Widow Frances Sanford Smith. She was described by Eliot as the “grave matron of the church at Dorchester”. She is said to h
2 CONC ave been a fitting companion for an educated man having the attainments of Mr. Pynchon. Her son by her first marriage, Henry Smith, a “godly, wise young man,” later married William's daughter, Anne, and figured prominently in the 
2 CONC settlement that became Springfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Smith-11655
2 CONT
2 CONT Nothing is known about this man's birth date or place, his parents, or even his first name.
2 CONT
2 CONT We know only that he was married, as her 1st, to Frances (Unknown) Smith Sanford Pynchon. She afterwards married, as her 2nd, to Tobias Sanford, and as her 3rd, to William Pynchon.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT The only child of Unknown Smith and Frances:[2]
2 CONT #Henry Smith, b c. 1605; m Anne Pynchon, his step-sister, daughter of William Pynchon and his first wife, Ann (Andrew) Pynchon.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT One possibility is that the first husband was an Henry Smith, a cleric. Records in Dorchester contain the following two entries.
2 CONT
2 CONT * The Inrolement of Aprrentices within the Borough of Dorchester 30 Nov 1620 Henry SMITH son of Henry SMITH, Clerk deceased, master John COKE, of Dorchester merchant, apprenticed for 9 years[16]
2 CONT * Admission of Freemen to the Company of Freemen 30th July 1629, Henry Smythe, mercer, app to Mr John Coke, 2s (shillings)[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT A Henry Smith, was also a member of the Dorchester company. He has been suggested by Michael Russell to be the son of Frances Sanford. [18] This is possible but by no means certain.
2 CONT
