1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === William Hilliard
2 CONT * Birth:  about 1641, probably at Duxbury, Plymouth Colony
2 CONT * Baptized: 25 February 1655 at Hingham, Massachusetts Bay Colony
2 CONT * Death: 24 Jan 1713/4  at about age 72 in Little Compton, Newport, Rhode Island Colony
2 CONT * Son of William Hilliard and Hester / Esther 
2 CONT * Brother of Hester (Hillard) Bate, Mary (Hilliard) Farrow 
2 CONT * Husband of Deborah Warren  — married 12 Jun 1671 in Hingham, Massachusetts Bay Colony 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Hilliard (son of William Hilliard and Hester) married before 26 JAN 1671/2 to Deborah Warren (daughter of John Warren and Deborah Wilson).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT # [--?--] HILLIARD b: 26 JAN 1671/2 in Hingham, Plymouth Colony
2 CONT # William HILLIARD b: 21 JAN 1672/3 in Hingham, Plymouth Colony
2 CONT # John HILLIARD b: 16 FEB 1674/5 in Hingham, Plymouth Colony
2 CONT # Simon HILLIARD b: 14 MAR 1675/6 in Hingham, Plymouth Colony
2 CONT # David HILLIARD b: 25 FEB 1677/8 in Little Compton, Newport, RI
2 CONT # Deborah HILLIARD b: 7 JUN 1680 in Little Compton, Newport, RI
2 CONT # Isaac HILLIARD b: 19 APR 1683 in Little Compton, Newport, RI
2 CONT # Esther HILLIARD b: 8 MAR 1684/5 in Little Compton, Newport, RI
2 CONT # Mary HILLIARD b: 3 APR 1687 in Little Compton, Newport, RI
2 CONT # Abigail HILLIARD b: 12 JUL 1690 in Little Compton, Newport, RI
2 CONT # Sarah HILLIARD b: 28 JUN 1692 in Little Compton, Newport, RI
2 CONT # Jonathan HILLIARD b: 8 NOV 1696 in Little Compton, Newport, RI
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''William Hilliard''' was born about 1641 probably in Duxbury, Plymouth Co., Massachusetts and died 24 JAN 1713/14 in Little Compton, Bristol Co., Massachusetts. He was buried in Old Commons Cem., Little Compton, Newport Co., Rho
2 CONC de Island.
2 CONT
2 CONT William and Deborah resided at 'Liberty Plain' in Hingham, where William was a cooper, the industry for which Hingham was well noted. Some of their neighbors at Liberty Plain were Humphrey Johnson and son Benjamin; Robert Dunbar (
2 CONC a Scotchman); Capt. John Jacob; and James Whiton and son James.
2 CONT
2 CONT I could find no record of William participating in church or town affairs, nor could I find any evidence that he was a property owner.. They must have had some relationship with the church because a sister of Deborah's married a s
2 CONC on of Daniel Hobart, who was the twin brother of Rev. Peter Hobart, the minister of Hingham for many years.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was some indication that they may have had a fire sometime in NOV 1683 because the cause of death of their son Isaac on 25 NOV 1683 is recorded as due to "burned in the fire".
2 CONT
2 CONT During the King Philip's war, the home of a neighbor, James Whiton was burnt by the Indians. Also, the house of Nathaniel Chubbick, probably of nearby Accord Pond, was one of those destroyed on 20 APR 1676. No mention is made of W
2 CONC illiam’s home. The family moved to Little Compton, sometime between the birth in Hingham of Esther on 8 MAR 1684/5 and the birth in Little Compton of Mary on 3 APR 1687. The original settlement of Little Compton was about 1676 and
2 CONC  it appears that William may have owned land shortly after that date but he was not recorded as one of the original owners.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a flag denoting military service honoring his grave in Little Compton. No record could be found showing service in King Philip's war, but a record exists showing that a William Hillerd from Little Compton had service in t
2 CONC he Sir William Phips' Expedition to Canada in 1690. It shows that under Capt. Samuel Gallup, a body of about 60 men sailed from Plymouth in five Shallops. This battle was won at the expense of many lives. It is hard to believe it 
2 CONC is William because he would have been fifty years of age, more likely his son, William.
2 CONT
2 CONT William appeared to be more involved in the affairs of the town in Little Compton than in Hingham. At the 20 MAR 1703/4 town meeting it was voated that William Hillird is chosen "Clark of the Market" & William or Will Hillird (Hil
2 CONC liard, Hillard) was voated "Clark of the Market" at the town meeting every year thereafter until and including MAR 1708/9. He was voated as one of the "Surveyors of the Highways" on 18 MAR 1705/6 and 20 MAR 1707/7. On 18 MAR 1705/
2 CONC 6 it was also voated that Will be chosen as "a monitor to see to our highways". On MAR 1708/9 he was selected for the "Jury of Tryalss". On 5 MAR 1710/11 and 10 MAR 1711/12 he was chosen as the "Tithing Man".
2 CONT
2 CONT On 4 JUL 1691, William bought from Joseph Church, Sr., Lot 56 for 5 pounds. On 8 FEB 1692/3 he bought Lot 54 from Nathaniel Warren for 10 pounds. On 8 APR 1697 he bought from Joseph Church for eighteen shillings Lot 23, Lot 24, Lo
2 CONC t 33, 1/2 of Lot 25 and 1/4 of Lot 18 and for 24 shillings, Lot 11. On 6 JUN 1699 he bought from John Rouse for eighteen shillings, 3/4 of Lot 14. Additional purchases were made that day from Samuel Bradford and Benjamin Church. T
2 CONC he records indicate that a William Hillard sold land 29 APR 1714, to William Fobes. William had died three months earlier in JAN, so this sale may have been finalized after his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT The will of William, recorded in Taunton, book 3, part 1, page 186 was made DEC 1712 and proved 1 FEB 1713. ". . .to wife Deborah Hilliard use of real and personal for life. To son David Hilliard one half of my real estate after t
2 CONC he death of my wife. To son Jonathan one half of my real estate after the death of my wife. To daughter Deborah, wife of John Paddock 10 pounds. To daughter Esther, wife of Jeremy Geere ten pounds. To daughter Mary, wife of John P
2 CONC almer 10 pounds. To daughter Abigail Hilliard 15 pounds. To daughter Sarah Hilliard 10 pounds. Rest and residue to wife Deborah including Indian woman . . . ." William Hilliard
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Date and place of birth have also been erroneously reported to be June 26, 1766 at Hingham, Plymouth County, Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ''History of the town of Hingham, Massachusetts'', by Hingham (Mass.)  Page 332.  
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000189422762835&size=large}}
2 CONT ----
