2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/dc/31/93/1a/534448688662dc4d/attachment-1_original.jpg?hash=9cd12e8c47bc6179069835b812986d237cc3a312ea5ffbe0eecb3c2907284b61.1795420799
2 FORM jpg
2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE Notes for Nathan received from Nadine Ketterling
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathan Whiting age 41,Big strong man, 5' 11 1/2 " tall,could put a bag of flour on shoulder. Information from the archives in Tasmania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nate worked for Alexander Brown in Liverpool,NY. He lived in one of two yellow houses on the lot where Brown house stood opposite L. Corbin's barn. Whiting lived in the lower one (where Hasbrouck residence now stands), most of his
2 CONC  children were born there.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1838-1839 Winter, from a " Narrative of a Patriot Exile", before the spring time had blossomed into summer the fatal battle of the windmill had been fought. Nate was captured by the British on a raid into Canada. Patriot exile's, 
2 CONC 83 in all were tried for Treason by the British Govt. at Toronto and found guilty. After 10 months of imprisonment in Canada they were all transported to Fort Henry & then by barge down the Ridau canal, into the St. Lawrence to Qu
2 CONC ebec, taking 5 days and nights. They came alongside her Britanic Majesty's ship Buffalo anchored 1 mile offshore of Quebec (commanded by Captain Wood) and were stowed on the 2nd deck. On 28 Sep 1839 they weighed anchor and set sai
2 CONC l.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1840 Feb 12, Nathan W. aboard the Buffalo arrived at Lower Sandy Bay, Van Diemans Land (Tasmania).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1844 Dec 03, Nathan W. was granted a free pardon. No word from him all those years. Rhoda Toles his wife had given him up for dead & married Richard Goodman & had 2 children. Richard died before Nate returned from Tasmania.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1845 Jan 27, Nathan W. left Hobart Town for the Sandwich Islands (Hawaii) aboard the American Whaling vessel "Steiglitz". The Steiglitz, of Sag Harbor: Selah Young, Master, had put in, for repairs; and by the intercession of Mr. H
2 CONC athaway, American Consul, the 25 pardoned men were given passage. Info obtained from book "Notes of an Exile to Van Diemans Land".
2 CONT
2 CONT 1845 Apr 27, Nathan W. aboard the Whaling vessel "Steiglitz", arrived at the Sandwich Islands anchoring 3 qtrs. of a mile off shore of Honalula, Oahu.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1845 Oct 1, Nathan W. and 8 others to work their passage aboard the whale ship, "Samuel Robinson" commanded by Captain Warner, bound for New Bedford, Massachusetts by way of Cape Horn.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1846 Mar 13, the "Samuel Robinson" arrived at Bedford, Mass. in the morning and anchor was set and by 10 o'clock the same day, after seven years and four & a half months absence, had the pleasure of stepping upon there native soil
2 CONC . Three days later Nate was given free passage by train to Utica. From Utica to Watertown, Jefferson County by stage, and the rest is lost to history as no personal account was maintained by Nate that we know of.
2 CONT
2 CONT Further notes from the New Bedford Public Library-New Bedford,3-27-1846,pg. 2 col. 1
2 CONT
2 CONT Van Dieman's Land Prisoners-The Utica Gazette of Saturday mentions the arrival in that city of nine of the Van Dieman's Land exiles, via New Bedford, on their way to Watertown, most of them residing in Jefferson and Lewis counties
2 CONC . Their names are Orrin W. Smith, Leonard Delano, Nathan Whiting,Salina: Robert Marsh, Niagra Falls: Dewitt Fero, John Grant, Canada: L. Darby, Jefferson co.
2 CONT They worked their passage home in a whale ship on the 13th inst. and were immediately supplied by several of our citizens with the means which have enabled them to proceed thus far on their return to their friends. They are very d
2 CONC estitute, and completely broken down by their sufferings. They left 42 patriot prisoners still unpardoned on the island.
