3 CITY Harrisville
3 STAE Utah
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Farmer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} (son of Noah Shurtliff and Lydia Brown of Ravenna, Portage County, Ohio). Born March 13, 1807, Montgomery, Hampden county, Massachusetts. Came to Utah September 27, 1851, captain his own company.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Married:	Eunice Baggs Gaylord, Ravenna, Portage County, Ohio (daughter of John Gaylord and Joanna Baggs of Portage County, Ohio).
2 CONT
2 CONT     Their children:
2 CONT
2 CONT             Elcemina Imogene, married Haskell V. Shurtliff;
2 CONT             Mary Eliza, married Pleasant G. Taylor;
2 CONT             Lewis Warren, married Louisa C. Smith; married Emily M. Wainwright;
2 CONT             Lydia Ann;
2 CONT             Lydia Manda, married Vincent Shurtleff;
2 CONT             Jane Narcissus, married Pleasant G. Taylor;
2 CONT             Elizabeth Hatch, married Isaiah L. Steward.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Family home Harrisville, Utah.
2 CONT     Married:	Altamira Gaylord, who was born August 7, 1816, Westfield, Massachusetts, and died July 30, 1883.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Their children:
2 CONT
2 CONT             Noah Luman born November 25, 1846, died June 19, 1892;
2 CONT             Ellen Cordelia born March 14, 1849;
2 CONT             Francis Marion born January 23, 1851; married Alta Priscilla Hancock; married Betsy Jane Hancock;
2 CONT             Lucy Ann born September 16, 1853, died January 13, 1866;
2 CONT             Charles Vinson born January 26, 1856;
2 CONT             Samuel Darwin born March 9, 1858, died March 9, 1858.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Family resided Springville and Ogden, Utah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Home missionary in Weber county. Member state legislature. Settled in Weber county. Farmer; brickmason. Died at Harrisville, Utah. Sources:	
2 CONT
2 CONT     Pioneers and Prominent Men of Utah photographs, page 273
2 CONT     Pioneers and Prominent Men of Utah, page 1160
2 CONT
2 CONT From FindaGrave.com http://findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=13900717 - 
2 CONT
2 CONT Luman's Journal - http://www.boap.org/LDS/Early-Saints/LShurtliff.html
2 CONT
2 CONT He had five wives and thirty-three children. Oct. 20, 1827 Baptized into Baptist church. Winter, 1827 Joined Cambellites under Sidney Rigdon. Summer, 1829 Read of Joseph Smith finding the Golden Plates. Fall, 1831 First heard of "
2 CONC Mormonism". February,1832 First heard Joseph Smith speak. Aug. 21, 1836 Baptized by Sylvester Smith in Kirtland, Ohio. Confirmed by David Whitmer. Dec. 15, 1836 Ordained an Elder at Kirtland, Ohio. Dec. 6, 1837 Received a Patriarc
2 CONC hal Blessing from Joseph Smith, Sr., in the Kirtland Temple. Dec. 9, 1838 Ordained a Seventy in Far West, Missouri. Apr. 8, 1844 President of Seventh Quorum of Seventies in Nauvoo. Jan. 20, 1846 Called with wife, Altamira, to offi
2 CONC ciate in Nauvoo Temple. May 20, 1851 Left to cross the plains for Salt Lake City, Utah Territory. Sep. 23, 1851 Arrived in Great Salt Lake City, Utah Territory. May 17, 1873 Ordained a Patriarch by Orson Pratt, assisted by Brigham
2 CONC  Young, D.H. Wells, John Taylor, Wilford Woodruff, and F.D. Richards, in Salt Lake City. May 3, 1874 Joined the United Order in Salt Lake City.
2 CONT
2 CONT POLITICAL HISTORY
2 CONT
2 CONT Aug. 1, 1853 Elected to the Utah Territorial Legislature. Jun. 11, 1855 Appointed Prosecuting Attorney of Weber County Apr. 5, 1872 Delegate to Republican State Convention in Salt Lake City.
2 CONT
2 CONT MILITARY HISTORY
2 CONT
2 CONT 1824 First Sargeant in Ohio State Militia. 1838 Participated in the "Missouri War." June, 1844 Captain of a Rifle Company in the Nauvoo Legion. 1856 Commanded the Silver Grays. Apr. 22, 1857 Captain of Second Company of Infantry o
2 CONC f Nauvoo Legion. Sep. 29, 1857 Commander of Division 1 during Johnson's War. Luman Shurtliff built an adobe house. Later Luman's son, Noah L. Shurtliff, made red brick by hand. A brickyard was later established, and there has been
2 CONC  a brickyard in Harrisville ever since; local brick making has turned into a major business.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Son of Noah Shurtliff & Lydia (Brown) Shurtliff Luman's Journal http://www.boap.org/LDS/Early-Saints/LShurtliff.html He had five wives and thirty-three children. Oct. 20, 1827    Baptized into Baptist church. Winter,  1827    Join
2 CONC ed Cambellites under Sidney Rigdon. Summer,  1829    Read of Joseph Smith finding the Golden Plates. Fall,    1831    First heard of "Mormonism". February,1832    First heard Joseph Smith speak. Aug. 21, 1836    Baptized by Sylves
2 CONC ter Smith in Kirtland, Ohio.
2 CONT                  Confirmed by David Whitmer. Dec. 15, 1836    Ordained an Elder at Kirtland, Ohio. Dec.  6, 1837    Received a Patriarchal Blessing from Joseph Smith, Sr., in the
2 CONT                  Kirtland Temple. Dec.  9, 1838    Ordained a Seventy in Far West, Missouri. Apr.  8, 1844    President of Seventh Quorum of Seventies in Nauvoo. Jan. 20, 1846    Called with wife, Altamira, to officiate in Nauvoo 
2 CONC Temple. May  20, 1851    Left to cross the plains for Salt Lake City, Utah Territory. Sep. 23, 1851    Arrived in Great Salt Lake City, Utah Territory. May  17, 1873    Ordained a Patriarch by Orson Pratt, assisted by Brigham
2 CONT                  Young, D.H. Wells, John Taylor, Wilford Woodruff, and F.D.
2 CONT                  Richards, in Salt Lake City. May   3, 1874    Joined the United Order in Salt Lake City.
2 CONT
2 CONT POLITICAL HISTORY
2 CONT
2 CONT Aug.  1, 1853    Elected to the Utah Territorial Legislature. Jun. 11, 1855    Appointed Prosecuting Attorney of Weber County Apr.  5, 1872    Delegate to Republican State Convention in Salt Lake City.
2 CONT
2 CONT MILITARY HISTORY
2 CONT
2 CONT          1824    First Sargeant in Ohio State Militia.
2 CONT          1838    Participated in the "Missouri War." June,    1844    Captain of a Rifle Company in the Nauvoo Legion.
2 CONT          1856    Commanded the Silver Grays. Apr. 22, 1857    Captain of Second Company of Infantry of Nauvoo Legion. Sep. 29, 1857    Commander of Division 1 during Johnson's War. Luman Shurtliff built an adobe house. Later Luman
2 CONC 's son, Noah L. Shurtliff, made red brick by hand. A brickyard was later established, and there has been a brickyard in Harrisville ever since; local brick making has turned into a major business.
2 CONT
2 CONT Relationship to Helen Rowe 3rd Great Grandfather
2 CONT * Reference: [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=13900717 Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jan 2 2016, 8:00:34 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Son of Noah Shurtliff & Lydia (Brown) Shurtliff Luman's Journal http://www.boap.org/LDS/Early-Saints/LShurtliff.html He had five wives and thirty-three children. Oct. 20, 1827    Baptized into Baptist church. Winter,  1827    Join
2 CONC ed Cambellites under Sidney Rigdon. Summer,  1829    Read of Joseph Smith finding the Golden Plates. Fall,    1831    First heard of "Mormonism". February,1832    First heard Joseph Smith speak. Aug. 21, 1836    Baptized by Sylves
2 CONC ter Smith in Kirtland, Ohio.
2 CONT                  Confirmed by David Whitmer. Dec. 15, 1836    Ordained an Elder at Kirtland, Ohio. Dec.  6, 1837    Received a Patriarchal Blessing from Joseph Smith, Sr., in the Kirtland Temple. Dec.  9, 1838    Ordained a Sevent
2 CONC y in Far West, Missouri. Apr.  8, 1844    President of Seventh Quorum of Seventies in Nauvoo. Jan. 20, 1846    Called with wife, Altamira, to officiate in Nauvoo Temple. May  20, 1851    Left to cross the plains for Salt Lake City
2 CONC , Utah Territory. Sep. 23, 1851    Arrived in Great Salt Lake City, Utah Territory. May  17, 1873    Ordained a Patriarch by Orson Pratt, assisted by Brigham Young, D.H. Wells, John Taylor, Wilford Woodruff, and F.D  Richards, in 
2 CONC Salt Lake City. May   3, 1874    Joined the United Order in Salt Lake City.
2 CONT
2 CONT POLITICAL HISTORY
2 CONT
2 CONT Aug.  1, 1853    Elected to the Utah Territorial Legislature. Jun. 11, 1855    Appointed Prosecuting Attorney of Weber County Apr.  5, 1872    Delegate to Republican State Convention in Salt Lake City.
2 CONT
2 CONT MILITARY HISTORY
2 CONT
2 CONT          1824    First Sargeant in Ohio State Militia.
2 CONT          1838    Participated in the "Missouri War." June,    1844    Captain of a Rifle Company in the Nauvoo Legion.
2 CONT          1856    Commanded the Silver Grays. Apr. 22, 1857    Captain of Second Company of Infantry of Nauvoo Legion. Sep. 29, 1857    Commander of Division 1 during Johnson's War. Luman Shurtliff built an adobe house. Later Luman
2 CONC 's son, Noah L. Shurtliff, made red brick by hand. A brickyard was later established, and there has been a brickyard in Harrisville ever since; local brick making has turned into a major business.
2 CONT
