1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Newton Deming Strong'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/90081646/newton-deming-strong Find A Grave Memorial ID # 90081646]
2 CONT
2 CONT Strong was the second son of Rev. William Lightbourn Strong and Harriet (Deming) Strong, was born October 17, 1809, while his father was settled at Somers, Connecticut. He graduated from Yale College in 1831. During one year, 1834
2 CONC –35, he was a tutor in the college. He then settled in Alton, Illinois, in the practice of the law, and in partnership with his classmate, Junius Hall. After about ten years residence at Alton, during a part of which time he was a
2 CONC  member of the Illinois House of Representatives, he removed to Reading, Pennsylvania, being there in partnership with his brother, Hon. William Strong. He afterward practiced law in St. Louis, Missouri where he died on August 8, 
2 CONC 1866, aged 56 years.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Matilda B. Edwards of Alton, in Sept., 1846, who died February 7, 1851, leaving no children.
2 CONT ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT [http://thelincolnlog.org/Results.aspx?type=CalendarDay&day=1857-05-25&r=L0NhbGVuZGFyWWVhci5hc3B4P3llYXI9MTg1NyZyPUwwTmhiR1Z1WkdGeUxtRnpjSGc9 Monday, May 25, 1857]
2 CONT Springfield, Illinois
2 CONT
2 CONT President Lincoln writes to St. Louis attorneys Newton Deming Strong and George P. Strong regarding the status of the appeal case of Eads & Nelson v. Ohio & Mississippi RR. Lincoln and the Strongs represent the railroad which lost
2 CONC  an earlier suit in the U.S. District Court for the District of Missouri. That court ruled in favor of Eads & Nelson, a company that did salvage work for the railroad after sixty of the railroad's cars sank in the Ohio River durin
2 CONC g transport between Louisville, Kentucky, and St. Louis, Missouri. The railroad and the salvage company differ on the amount of compensation that Eads & Nelson should receive for salvaging fifty-two of the sixty cars. Although the
2 CONC y won the case in Missouri, Eads & Nelson are compelled to take the railroad to court in Illinois, the state in which the railroad had placed the cars. Eads & Nelson also win the suit in the U.S. District Court for the Southern Di
2 CONC strict of Illinois. Lincoln writes, "The admiralty case now stands on appeal to the circuit court and consequently, can only be tried by Judge [John] McLean; and I understand he will remain here only one week, commencing that firs
2 CONC t Monday of June." Lincoln advises the Strongs to meet with the plaintiffs' St. Louis attorney "and make an arrangement with him as to a day of taking up the case." Before the defendants appealed the case, Lincoln had argued that 
2 CONC the federal court in Illinois did not have jurisdiction because the federal court in Missouri had already tried the case and ruled for Eads & Nelson. Lincoln refers to the federal courts and jurisdiction, and takes the opportunity
2 CONC  to mock the U.S. Supreme Court's Dred Scott decision: "There is no longer any difficult question of jurisdiction in the Federal courts; they have jurisdiction in all possible cases except such as might redound to the benefit of a
2 CONC  'nigger' in some way. Seriously, I wish you to prepare, on the question jurisdiction as well as you can; for I fear the later decisions are against us." Abraham Lincoln to Newton Deming Strong and George P. Strong, 25 May 1857, I
2 CONC Hi, Springfield, IL; U.S. District Court transcript, 13 September 1855, Eads & Nelson v. Ohio & Mississippi RR, Record Group 21, case file 1; Judgment, 1 July 1856, Eads & Nelson v. Ohio & Mississippi RR, General Record, Vol. 1, 5
2 CONC 0, U.S. District Court Southern District of Illinois; Answer, 15 March 1856, Eads & Nelson v. Ohio & Mississippi RR, Record Group 21, case file 1, all in National Archives and Records Administration, Great Lakes Region, Chicago, I
2 CONC L.
2 CONT
2 CONT Newton Deming Strong, 1809 - 1866
2 CONT Newton Deming Strong was born on October 17, 1809, in Somers, Connecticut, to William Lightburn (Rev.) Strong and Harriet Strong (born Deming).
2 CONT William was born on October 18, 1782, in Salisbury, Connecticut.
2 CONT Harriet was born on November 6, 1789.
2 CONT Newton had 10 siblings: William (Judge) Strong, Harriet Williams Pratt (born Strong), Edward Strong, Mary Willson (born Strong), Sarah Elizabeth Cooley (born Strong), Theodore Strong, Samuel Worcester Strong, Abigail Bates Gaston 
2 CONC (born Strong), Julia Darling (born Strong) and Helen Maria Loveland (born Strong).
2 CONT Newton married Matilda Rachel Strong (born Edwards) on September 24, 1844, at age 34 in St. Louis, Missouri.
2 CONT Matilda was born in 1824.
2 CONT Newton passed away on August 8, 1866, at age 56 in St Louis, Missouri.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://sparedshared3.wordpress.com/letters/1832-newton-deming-strong-to-john-milton-clapp/ 1832: Newton Deming Strong to John Milton Clapp]
