1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sherman Day'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/166760693/sherman-day Find A Grave Memorial ID # 166760693]  
2 CONT * [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherman_Day Sherman Day Wikipedia Page] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sherman Day (1806–1884) was born in New Haven, Connecticut and died in Berkeley, California. He attended Phillips Academy in Andover, Massachusetts and graduated from Yale College in New Haven, A.B., 1826. He received his degree f
2 CONC rom his father, Jeremiah Day (1773–1867), who was then the president of Yale College 1817–1846. He was also the grandson of the American founding father Roger Sherman.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Biography''
2 CONT
2 CONT After his graduation he lived in New York and Philadelphia for a time, as a merchant. For several years he was in Ohio and Indiana as an engineer. In 1843, he published Historical Collections of the State of Pennsylvania. He came 
2 CONC to California in 1849, and engaged in civil and mining engineering at San Jose, New Almaden, Folsom and Oakland.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1855 he made for the state a survey of wagon-road routes across the Sierra Nevada; California State Senate, 1855–56; United States Surveyor General, California, 1868–71; original founding trustee, University of California, Prof
2 CONC essor, Mine Construction and Surveying.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sherman Day's ''Historical Collections of Pennsylvania'' is considered one of the most important documentations of early histories of Pennsylvania, mostly because it included individual histories for all the counties within the Ke
2 CONC ystone State. As well, it contained a series of wonderful images (produced by Sherman as well) of all parts of the Keystone State.
2 CONT
2 CONT "We are more familiar with the history of England, Rome or Greece' and with the career of Alexander, Caesar and Napoleon than with the events that have occurred in our own vicinity. Yes, even in the very fields that we ourselves a
2 CONC re tilling."
2 CONT ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT SHERMAN DAY, the only child of future Yale College President Jeremiah Day, by his first wife, Martha Prescott Sherman, daughter of founding father Roger Sherman, was born in New Haven, February 13, 1806, while his father was still
2 CONC  Professor of Mathematics.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1826-1835, he was engaged in business as a merchant in New York, Philadelphia and Marseilles, and while settled in New York was married, September 6, 1832, to Elizabeth Ann, daughter of Henry King of Westfield, Massachusetts;
2 CONC  a large part of the next two years was spent in Marseilles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1835 he abandoned mercantile pursuits, and from 1836-1841 resided in Ohio and Indiana, engaging in civil engineering. He then returned to the East and while living in New Haven and Philadelphia, compiled a volume entitled ''"Hi
2 CONC storical Collections of Pennsylvania,"'' which was published in 1843 (708 pp. 870). He then resumed a mercantile life, doing business in New York and St. Louis.
2 CONT
2 CONT Severe losses, together with failing health, induced him to embark for California in June, 1849. Thenceforth, he devoted himself to surveying and engineering, civil and mining and both in his profession and outside it impressed hi
2 CONC mself on the community as a man of unswerving Christian principle and integrity. In 1854, his family joined him, and for 1855-1856, he was a member of the State Senate from Santa Clara County. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1855 he surveyed a route for a wagon road across the Sierra Nevada Mountains. At this time his residence was at Oakland, California, and there most of his later years were spent. He was one of the original trustees of the Colle
2 CONC ge of California, and for a short time held the professorship of Mine Construction and Surveying. From September, 1868 to February, 1871, he was United States Surveyor General for California. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After a year or two of feebleness, he died from a disease of the heart at Berkeley, Alameda County, California on December 14, 1884; in his 79th year. His wife died in 1873. Two sons and two daughters are still living; one daughte
2 CONC r is the wife of Charles Theodore Hart Palmer (Yale College 1847).
2 CONT
2 CONT Yale Obituary Record, 1886-87
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.google.com/books/edition/Historical_Collections_of_the_State_of_P/AEIVAAAAYAAJ?hl=en Historical Collections of the State of Pennsylvania by Sherman Day, 1843]
2 CONT * [https://pcad.lib.washington.edu/person/8656/ Sherman Day, Pacific Coast Architecture Database]
