1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Florence Mary “Flora” Bagley Sherman'''
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/224635975/florence-mary-sherman Find A Grave Memorial ID # 224635975]
2 CONT
2 CONT Four articles in The New York Times from 1883-85 regarding Forence Sherman (Bagley)'s messy divorce from Roger Minott Sherman:
2 CONT
2 CONT  * * *
2 CONT The New York Times, October 13, 1883
2 CONT
2 CONT THE SURETIES IN A DIVORCE SUIT.; A CASE IN WHICH EX-GOV. BAGLEY'S DAUGHTER IS INTERESTED.
2 CONT
2 CONT At Detroit, Michigan, on Nov. 13, 1876, Miss Florence Bagley, daughter of ex-Gov. Bagley, of that State, was married to Roger M. Sherman, of this City [New York], who was then an Assistant United States District Attorney. About a 
2 CONC year and a half ago Mr. and Mrs. Sherman separated, and the latter went back to Michigan. Soon after their separation Mr. Sherman began a suit in Connecticut for a divorce and for a decree awarding him the custody of his two child
2 CONC ren. Renewed attention is drawn in this City to this litigation by some statement made yesterday by Charles W. Brooke, counsel for Mr. Sherman, before Judge O'Gorman, in Superior Court, Chambers. Some time ago Robert Robertson beg
2 CONC an a suit to obtain $320 from Mr. Sherman, asserting that he was entitled to that sum as compensation for having taken lucrative business to Mr. Sherman's office. In that suit an attachment was granted against Mr. Sherman's proper
2 CONC ty, and was sustained on the ground that he was not a resident of this State. The sureties given on the bond by Robertson at the time he procured the attachment were directed to appear before Judge O'Gorman yesterday to justify. T
2 CONC he parties having appeared in court, and Judge O'Gorman being too busy to proceed personally with the matter, Mr. Brooke asked for an adjournment. He said he wished the sureties to be examined in open court, for the reason that he
2 CONC  believed the suit against Mr. Sherman to be without merits, and to have been begun with the mere intent to procure, if possible, an adjudication to the effect that Mr. Sherman was not a resident of Connecticut, where he is suing 
2 CONC his wife. Such an adjudication, he said, would be used to influence the course of the divorce suit to the disadvantage of Mr. Sherman. As to the bondsmen presented by the plaintiff, Robert Robertson, one of them, John Robertson, w
2 CONC as a person who had left Scotland as an insolvent debtor, and who was now a clerk at a small salary. He had admitted that Mrs. Florence Bagley Sherman had indemnified him against any loss as a surety by giving him her personal bon
2 CONC d for $30,000. Judge O'Gorman adjourned the examination of the sureties until next Thursday.
2 CONT
2 CONT * * *
2 CONT
2 CONT The New York Times, March 29, 1884
2 CONT
2 CONT MR. SHERMAN'S SUIT FOR DIVORCE; HIS VIEW OF THE CHARACTER OF MRS. GOV. BAGLEY.
2 CONT
2 CONT For about two years Roger M. Sherman, a well-known young lawyer of this city, has been trying to procure a decree separating him from his wife, Florence B. Sherman, the eldest daughter of the late Gov. John J. Bagley, of Michigan,
2 CONC  and awarding him the custody of their two children, a boy and a girl. The couple were married in 1876 in Detroit, Mich., with great pomp, and they came to this city to live, where Sherman was an Assistant United States District A
2 CONC ttorney. In 1881 Mrs. Sherman went to Detroit to her mother's home with her children, and has since remained there. The next year her husband brought a suit in Connecticut for an absolute divorce on the ground that she was a habit
2 CONC ual drunkard, but the complaint was dismissed for lack of jurisdiction.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Sherman waited until January last and then began a suit in the Supreme Court here for a limited divorce on the ground that his wife had abandoned him. Last week Mr. Sherman obtained from Judge Lawrence an order directing his w
2 CONC ife to show cause why she should not be ordered to bring her children within the physical jurisdiction of the court and obey any order it might make respecting their disposition pending the litigation. The order to show cause was 
2 CONC based on a voluminous affidavit, made by Mr. Sherman, and on sworn declarations of other persons, to the effect that Mrs. Sherman was addicted to the use of liquor. Mr. Sherman averred that his wife was not only a drunkard but tha
2 CONC t she had told him that she hated him, and that she loved another man whom she had known in her girlhood. She had discussed Zola's novel "Nana" with male visitors at her home in this city while he was absent, and had spoken in an 
2 CONC indelicate manner of marital relations. He had been deceived and lured into his marriage with her, notwithstanding that he loved her. He considered that her mother was the main actor in the fraud practiced upon him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Bagley, Mrs. Sherman thought, was responsible for her daughter's misconduct, and his own domestic sorrows. By her own statements to him Mrs. Bagley had given her children liquor in their childhood, and thus aroused the appeti
2 CONC te of which Mrs. Sherman is possessed. Mrs. Bagley wished to manage his household, to have her name conferred on his daughter, and to nourish his children, as she had nourished her own, with liquor, but he would not allow her to.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Sherman formed a dislike for Mrs. Bagley on account of her peculiar advanced notions of religion and morality, her frivolity, and the deception which he knew she had practiced on her husband. He therefore was unwilling to have
2 CONC  her exert any influence over his children. His dislike became apparent to her, and she therefore chose to make him unhappy through the extraordinary control she had over his wife. Mr. Sherman avers that his wife and mother-in-law
2 CONC  are unfit to have the custody of his children at all, and he asks to have them taken out of their hands until this suit is determined.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the motion was called for hearing by Judge Lawrence, in Supreme Court, Chambers, yesterday morning, Mr. Charles W. Brooke, counsel for Mr. Sherman, said that neither side was ready, and he asked for an adjournment until April
2 CONC  9. Mr. D. B. Ogden, who appeared for Mrs. Sherman, said that he wished the matter adjourned until April 11, and also wished to give notice of a motion then to be made to strike out parts of Mr. Sherman's affidavit as scandalous. 
2 CONC Mr. Brooke objected to a delay beyond April 9. He said that since Mr. Sherman began his suit Mrs. Sherman had begun one against him in Detroit, the process in which was returnable on April 10. Mr. Sherman had not appeared in the M
2 CONC ichigan action, and it might happen that if the hearing should be adjourned until April 11 the court here would then be confronted with some sort of order, taken by default in Michigan, apparently depriving it of jurisdiction and 
2 CONC raising questions that would complicate the case.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judge Lawrence remarked that if a delay of six weeks had been asked it would not have been so suspicious as was, under the circumstances, the request for adjournment until the day after the Michigan process. He adjourned until Apr
2 CONC il 9 the hearing of the motion to strike out parts of Mr. Sherman's affidavit, as well as that for the production of the children in court.
2 CONT
2 CONT * * *
2 CONT
2 CONT The New York Times, May 15, 1884
2 CONT
2 CONT MRS. SHERMAN GETS A DIVORCE.
2 CONT
2 CONT DETROIT, Mich., May 14.--The matrimonial troubles of Mr. and Mrs. Roger Sherman have long been a source of regret to their Detroit friends. When Mr. Sherman, then Assistant District Attorney for New York, came here eight years ago
2 CONC  and wedded the late Gov. Bagley's eldest daughter, Florence, the alliance promised to be an exceptionally happy one. But grave differences sprang up and the divorce court was sought. Mr. Sherman sued for divorce in the East alleg
2 CONC ing drunkenness and other offenses on the part of his wife. Reports of the trial were telegraphed all over the country, and the case excited considerable comment, because of the social prominence of the parties. A few weeks ago an
2 CONC  interview with Mr. Sherman was published in a New-York paper in which he made a bitter attack upon Mrs. Bagley. Pending the issue of the Eastern case Mrs. Bagley quietly instituted a divorce suit in the Wayne County Circuit Court
2 CONC , in this city. The bill was filed in December last and was hidden away from public gaze, while the papers in ordinary cases are open to anybody's inspection. The allegations were adultery and desertion. Employees of the Ebbit Hou
2 CONC se in Washington, testified to Mr. Sherman's visit there under the name of Shepherd with a woman whom he registered as his wife. It was also alleged in the wife's bill that Mr. Sherman had been guilty of like offenses with other w
2 CONC omen in and about New-York and upon his yacht at Newport. Mrs. Sherman yesterday testified that the defendant had not contributed to her support since the separation. Judge Chambers granted a divorce and awarded Mrs. Sherman custo
2 CONC dy of her two children.
2 CONT
2 CONT * * *
2 CONT
2 CONT The New York Times, May 26, 1885
2 CONT
2 CONT COMPLICATING A DIVORCE SUIT.; A QUESTION OF ALLEGED PERJURY IN THE SHERMAN LITIGATION.
2 CONT
2 CONT In February last the suit of Kate M. Sherman for an absolute divorce from Walter R. Sherman was tried before Judge Beach in the Supreme Court. Judge Beach gave her a decree of divorce and awarded her the custody of her son, Walter
2 CONC  M. Sherman, 9 years of age. One of the main witnesses against Sherman was Robert Robertson, formerly a partner of his brother, Roger M. Sherman. Soon after the trial Robertson was arrested on a charge of perjury, and was committe
2 CONC d by a Police Justice for trial, Roger M. Sherman and Charles W. Brooke having appeared against him as the representatives of the District Attorney. Charles W. Brooke is counsel for Roger M. Sherman in his divorce litigation with 
2 CONC Florence M. Sherman, a daughter of ex-Gov. John J. Bagley, of Michigan. Robertson having been stigmatized as a perjurer, Messrs. Sherman and Brooke, as counsels for Walter R. Sherman, moved to have the decree of divorce against hi
2 CONC m vacated and for a new trial.
2 CONT
2 CONT The motion was heard by Judge Van Vorst yesterday. In their affidavits the Shermans declared that one James Annette swore falsely on the trial as well as Robertson. Walter Sherman swore that the charges of infidelity against him w
2 CONC ere untrue, and he was supported by his brother in the averment that his wife had treated him badly. One of the instances of ill-treatment was that she struck him in the face with a glass vase, which cut him deeply when it shivere
2 CONC d to bits.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mrs. Sherman made a long affidavit in response to the moving papers. She averred that her husband had inserted matters of malicious slander in his affidavits respecting her, and that his brother had joined him in slandering her un
2 CONC der cover of his privilege speaking in court, as well as in an affidavit. She asserted that Robertson and Annette had told the truth, and that the proceedings in the Police Court against Robertson were a mockery. Next she told how
2 CONC  she had eloped from Ithaca, when she was only 15 years old, with Walter R. Sherman, who had been a student at Cornell. They were married here, and very soon after their marriage he began to ill-treat her. She was compelled to lea
2 CONC ve him, but she returned to him at his earnest solicitation. Finally, in 1882, he left her, she said, because the proof of his infidelity was at hand. He took her child from her, and afterward put him in boarding schools, but fail
2 CONC ed to pay for his tuition, and she had to pay for it out of her small store. In February last she got possession of the boy after the burning of a school he was in at Chappaqua, and she has since retained him under the decree of t
2 CONC he court. Affidavits made by Robertson and Annette, repeating the testimony given by them on the trial, and insisting that Walter R. Sherman was utterly immoral, were also read by ex-Judge Richard Busteed, who appeared for Mrs. Sh
2 CONC erman. At the conclusion of the reading of the affidavits of Robertson and Annette Mr. Brooke moved that they be stricken out as scandalous, because they contained matter reflecting on Mr. Roger M. Sherman. Judge Van Vorst reserve
2 CONC d his decision on that question and will hear more on the motion for a new trial on Thursday.
2 CONT
2 CONT = = < = > = = < = > = = < = > = - - - - - - - - - - - - - - = < = > = = < = > = = < = > = = 
2 CONT
2 CONT /|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\
2 CONT
2 CONT Anna Eleanor Roosevelt, future wife of Franklin Delano Roosevelt, was born in 1884, to Anna Hall and Elliott Roosevelt, Theodore's younger brother. "Terribly overshadowed by his older brother, Elliott was or became an unstable and
2 CONC  in many ways weak character. He was an alcoholic incapable of steady work or consistent behavior. He oscillated unpredictably between irrational jubilation and blank depression. His marriage to Ana had started breaking down almos
2 CONC t as soon as it began..." He often engaged in "reckless and dangerous sporting endeavors such as night fox-hunting. He broke a succession of bones, ... was kicked in the head by his horse, and became dependent again on alcohol as 
2 CONC an antidote to pain and depressive thoughts. His mood was sometimes explicitly suicidal."
2 CONT
2 CONT "In the summer of 1890 the whole family was propelled by Anna to Europe in the hope of finding help for Elliott in the spas of Germany and Switzerland." But he continued to drink, and so he went to a sanitarium in Austria. "After 
2 CONC three months the family moved to the west Paris suburb of Neuilly, where Eleanor [age seven] was sent to a convent."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Paris proved an irresistible trap for Elliott, who was terribly depressed when sober. He gave way to debauchery on a prodigious scale, and took up with an expatriate American woman, Florence Bagley Sherman, of Detroit. Mrs. Sherm
2 CONC an infuriated the Roosevelts by blaming all Elliott's problems on Anna's supposedly miserable and domineering personality. In the midst of this domestic turmoil, Elliott was accused in absentia in New York by the Roosevelt's maid,
2 CONC  Katy Mann, of siring her illegitimate child. He could not promise Anna that the claim was not true."
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Franklin Delano Roosevelt: Champion of Freedom
2 CONT
2 CONT  By Conrad Black, pp 34-5
2 CONT
2 CONT copyright 2002, downloaded 2010 from books.google at http://books.google.com/books?id=lYVCi70HaigC&pg=PA35&lpg=PA35&dq=Florence+Bagley+Sherman&source=bl&ots=qzj3aW5KUM&sig=W21bg11EOkTs7FtT7if24qZw0pk&hl=en&ei=1FdwTJDMH5TUtQO2l5iWC
2 CONC w&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=3&ved=0CBgQ6AEwAg#v=onepage&q=Florence%20Bagley%20Sherman&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT /|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\|/|\
2 CONT
2 CONT ================================================================
2 CONT
2 CONT Letter of Florence Bagley Sherman to Swami Vivekananda
2 CONT
2 CONT February 24 1894
2 CONT
2 CONT c/o  Mrs. Hale 541 Dearborn Avenue, Chicago:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dear Mr. Vive Kananda:
2 CONT
2 CONT My mother wishes me to write for her, expressing her regret that the draft for the money received here was not sent today. It will go early Monday morning, and I trust will be received on time to save you any annoyance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Freer, the gentleman at whose house we dined, sent a check last evening, for two hundred dollars — to Mrs. Bagley — to be forwarded to you, so that is included in the draft.
2 CONT
2 CONT Do believe me — my dear Mr. Kananda — when I say that when you have been able to enlist the interest and practical help of men like my friend Mr. Freer, in your work, you have no right to go back to India without one supreme effor
2 CONC t for your normal school: your personal appeal — with your magnetism — your powerful — masterful presence, can alone make that appeal effective. If in any way we can aid you — I am sure you will ask us, feeling sure of our sympath
2 CONC y and respect.
2 CONT
2 CONT Yours very sincerely
2 CONT
2 CONT Florence Bagley Sherman 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Downloaded 2010 from http://www.vivekananda.net/LettersToVivekananda.html
2 CONT
