1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DAR#: A089298
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pipes Jr., son of John Pipes Sr. and Susanna Hathaway, was born between the years of 1736 and 1739 in Morristown New Jersey. He firstly married Jemima Harriman in 1760, and they had at least three sons, though nothing is enti
2 CONC rely certain. John Pipes remarried to Mary Morris in 1777 and with her had twelve children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After his death, his widow, Mary Morris, stated that John Pipes had served with the Cummens’ and Dayton’s Regiment in 1776. According to her, he commanded a company and was also present at the battle of Ticonderoga. There, he was 
2 CONC created 1st Lieutenant of Captain Conway’s Company and his second-in-command. Later in 1776, John Pipes was discharged, though he rejoined the army under General Washington, and served with him for two years as a regiment. Also un
2 CONC der Washington, John Pipes was promoted to Captain. Towards the end of the war, John Pipes served as a minuteman under Horatio Gates. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Revolutionary War, John Pipes and his family moved to Kentucky. They settled in Mercer County, where they purchased 38 acres of land. In Kentucky, he and his family where farmers, and they produced tobacco and other sust
2 CONC enance crops, along with raising pigs, cows, and horses. 
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pipes died on the 6th of August, 1821 in Mercer County. His wife Mary would later file for a war pension:
2 CONT
2 CONT                       The Pension Application of Mary (Morris) Pipes On this third day of November in the year 1837 personally appeared before me Charles F. Burton, a Justice of the Peace for the state and county aforesaid, and on
2 CONC e of the Judges of the Mercer County Court. Mary Pipes, widow and relic of John Pipes who was a Lieutenant and Captain during the war of the Revolution who being first duly sworn according to law doth on her oath make the followin
2 CONC g declaration in order to obtain the benefit of the provisions made by the Act of Congress passed July 4, 1836. That she is the widow of the said John Pipes and seventy seven years old, that she was married to the said John Pipes 
2 CONC in Morristown, State of New Jersey, in the month of August, in the year 1777 or 1778. She was then in her seventeenth year, he the said Pipes was on furlow from Washington's Army, then encamped at Elizabethtown. Several officers o
2 CONC f the Regiment to which he belonged were at the wedding, among them were Captain Wade,Lieutenant Horn, and my uncle Joseph Morris, who was wounded in the Battle of Brandywine and died of his wound, and was buried with honours of w
2 CONC ar. He held the commission of Major. Mr. Pipes was then acting under the commission of lieutenant, during service in New Jersey, he was sometimes attached to Cummen's and Dayton's Regiment--before his marriage. Mr. Pipes commanded
2 CONC  a company and was in the battle of Ticonderoga. He served one year and received his discharge. He then joined the army of Genl. Washington, and was in actual service with him for two years as a regular. His regiment were nearly a
2 CONC ll killed up and they had more officers than they wanted, and he was offered to return home at half pay, but he refused to do so. This offer was made to him by General Washington in person. Sometime in the year 1779 or 1780 he rec
2 CONC eived an honorable discharge and they removed to North Carolina where his father then lived and joined the army under Gates and Green, not as a Regular but subject to regular draft as a minute man. Her husband John Pipes was in si
2 CONC xteen battles during the war of the Revolution. She will further attest that she has had by her said husband John Pipes, eleven children, four of them were born before husband's services closed in the war of the Revolution. Then i
2 CONC s second Evidence of his service in New Jersey she knows it to be the fact that he served (2) two years as Lieutenant and (1) one year as Captain. She farther attests that her said husband departed this life on the 6th day of Augu
2 CONC st 1821, and that she has remained a widow ever since that period as will more fully appear by reference to the proof hereto annexed. Sworn to and subscribed the day and year aforesaid in the presence of witnesses. D. Bolling (sig
2 CONC natures) Mary X Pipes N. B. his commission and discharge have long since been lost. She forwards a piece of old money her husband received for his services. Mary X Pipes
2 CONT
2 CONT The affidavit of Peter Harman and Dred Boling taken at the same time and place the affidavit being of lawful age and first duly sworn according to Law state that they have been intimately acquainted with Old Mrs. Pipes widow of Jo
2 CONC hn Pipes did for the space of forty years past, and that they were intimately acquainted with old Capt. John Pipes in his life time for a number of years, that they know the fact that he was reputed and believed by all who knew hi
2 CONC m to have been a soldier and an officer in the war of the Revolution--and they farther state that the said Mary Pipes is the widow and relic of the said John Pipes and a highly credible person sworn to and subscribed. This day and
2 CONC  year aforesaid. Peter Harman D. Bolling
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.pipesfamily.com/captjohn.htm]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT The Pipes, the Hathaway's and other families leaving New Jersey and heading to southwest Pennsylvania. Were attacked by Indians along a river and had to turn back. It has been speculated that this isthe time when Joseph Pipes, son
2 CONC  of John Jr. and Jemima (Harriman) was kidnapped and that perhaps Jemima was killed by the Indians. The next year some of the same group made another attempt and this time they were successful. He signed a mortgage with father in 
2 CONC law John Harriman on 20 October 1766 in Pequannack, Morris Co., New Jersey. John Pipes and John Harriman from Morris Co. New Jersey. Mortgage book A pp. 52-53, 1766, 52-53, He was listed in sixteen battles during the war of the Re
2 CONC volution
