0 @I6000000003221403267@ INDI
1 NAME Jacob John /Piatt/ I
2 GIVN Jacob John
2 SURN Piatt
2 NSFX I
2 _MARNM Piatt
2 NICK Jacob Pyatt
2 NICK Jacob Pyeatt
2 NICK Piat
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 31 OCT 1678
2 ADDR
3 CITY Piscataway Township
3 STAE New Jersey
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 DEAT
2 DATE AFT 1750
2 ADDR
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 OCCU Indian trader
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Sources:''
2 CONT [https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Piatt-28 WikiTree]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jacob Piatt, Sr.''', son of Rene Piatt and wife Elizabeth Sheffield, was born October 31, 1678 in Piscataway, Middlesex County, New Jersey and died after 1750, probably in the Cumberland Valley of Pennsylvania. He was an Indian
2 CONC  trader. Jacob's name is spelled Piatt, Pyatt and Pyeatt. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob married Mary Hull in June 1703. Mary, daughter of Samuel Hull and Mary Manning, was born February 2, 1680/81 in Piscataway, Middlesex, New Jersey and died c.1750. They had nine children.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Jacob Piatt and wife Mary Hull:
2 CONT * Jacob Piatt, b. 14 January 1705/06, Middletown, Piscataway, New Jersey, d. Aft. 1755, Woodbridge, Middlesex County, New Jersey.
2 CONT * Dinah Piatt, b. 03 February 1703/04
2 CONT * Peter Piatt, b. 19 January 1707/08, 
2 CONT * Rachel Pyeatt, b. 25 September 1709
2 CONT * Thomas Piatt, b. Abt. 1710
2 CONT * John Piatt, b. Abt. 1712, 
2 CONT * Mary Piatt, b. Abt. 1714, 
2 CONT * William Piatt, b. Abt. 1716, 
2 CONT * Samuel Piatt, b. Abt. 1718
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacob Piatt and his son Jacob were involved in a number of interesting encounters with those trying to keep intact the delicate balance of peace with the Indians. Father and son were Indian traders, and lived near them, even when 
2 CONC there was a serious threat of trouble.
2 CONT
2 CONT In "HUGUENOTS IN AMERICA; MEMORIALS OF THE HUGUENOTS, WITH SPECIAL REFERENCE TO THEIR EMIGRATION TO PENNSYLVANIA," by Rev. A. Stapleton, AM, MS, 1969 (p. 129) there is a statement, "Jacob Piatt, evidently a second son of the immig
2 CONC rant, appears in Lancaster County, Pennsylvania, as an Indian trader at an early day. Jacob Piatt, Jr, was a pioneer on the frontiers, and was dispossessed of his lands in Path Valley as an intruder in 1750."
2 CONT
2 CONT Again, in "HISTORY OF CUMBERLAND COUNTY AND ADAMS COUNTY, PENNSYLVANIA", 1886 (p. 18) there is an account of the 1750 ejection from Path Valley of Jacob Pyeatt, Jacob Pyeatt, Jr., James Blair, and others. There are similar account
2 CONC s in the minutes of the Provisional Council, found in the Colonial Records of Pennsylvania, Vols. 4 & 5:
2 CONT
2 CONT "In the early development of Pennsylvania when there was much land available for settlement and much danger to be apprehended from trespassing upon the lands of the Red man which had not yet been bargained for, laws were enacted p
2 CONC rohibiting the people from going into this country beyond the Kittochtinny Mountains for the purpose of settlement. These laws were evoked from the fear that such actions would precipitate serious trouble, if not war, with the Ind
2 CONC ians; or as the prohibition said, 'A breach might ensue between the Six Nations of Indians and this Province.' And yet, as more and more people came into the eastern parts of the Province, the stories circulated of the wondrous we
2 CONC alth of fine lands there were just over the mountains, we cannot wonder that the adventurous spirit of the times incited some of the more adventurous settlers to flout the law — even as they do in this enlightened age — where they
2 CONC  saw a chance of adventure and profit. 
2 CONT
2 CONT One Richard Peters, having been directed by Governor Hamilton to give information to the magistrates against all who had presumed to violate the law, wrote the governor by letter dated July 2, 1750, as follows:
2 CONT
2 CONT 'On Wednesday, the 30th of May, the magistrates and company, being detained two days by rain, proceeded over the Kittochtinny Mountains and entered in the Tuscarora Path, or Path Valley through which the road to Alleghany lies. Ma
2 CONC ny settlements were formed in this valley and all the people were sent for, and the following persons appeared, viz: Abraham Slack, James Blair, Moses Moore, Arthur Dunlap, Alexander McCartie, Felix Doyle, Andrew Dunlap, Robert Wi
2 CONC lson, JACOB PYATT, JACOB PYATT, JR., William Ramage, Reynold Alexander, Samuel Patterson, Robert Baker, John Armstrong and John Potts who were all convicted by their own confession to the magistrates, of the like trespasses with t
2 CONC hose at Sherman’s Creek and were bound in the like recognizances to appear at court, and bonds to the proprietors, to remove with all their families, servants, cattle, and effects and all having voluntarily given possession of the
2 CONC ir houses to me, some ordinary log houses, to the number of eleven, were burnt to the ground; the trespassers, most of them cheerfully, and a very few of them with reluctance, carrying out all their goods. Some had deserted before
2 CONC , and lay waste.'
