1 NAME James Clark /Dunham/
2 GIVN James Clark
2 _MARNM Dunham
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Andrew Cemetery, Andrew, Jackson County, Iowa, USA
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1812
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 OCT 1889
1 OCCU Tanner, shoemaker
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} James trained in adolescence as a tanner & shoemaker, and was a cousin of Justus DUNHAM (FindAGrave memorial #104227111), who's 1st wife's sister James married in 1833.  The newlyweds lived c.1834-48 in Hickory Tow
2 CONC nship (today’s Town of Hermitage), Mercer Co., PA., before moving to nearby Sharon, seat of Mercer County (c.1848-49).  
2 CONT
2 CONT In about 1850 – not long after his cousin Justus and wife's sister Eliza & family had moved 1,100 miles west to northeastern Iowa – James & Sarah joined them, making the tedious, 3-week journey by wagon team.  In Jackson County, I
2 CONC owa (bordering the Mississippi River to the east), James bought 200 acres of “wild land” in Fairfield Township, that he “improved” (turned into a farm) near today’s hamlet of Spragueville.  An 1879 history of Jackson County notes 
2 CONC that, for 17 years after he came to the area, James “ran the ferry over the Maquoketa River, known all over the county as Dunham’s Ferry. He served his township for many years in different capacities” including – starting in 1859 
2 CONC – 26 years as Justice of the Peace, and was elected several times as Township Trustee, School Director and Road Supervisor.  Honored by locals with the title “Squire”, he was respected as “a man of sterling worth” and known as a “
2 CONC staunch old Jacksonian Democrat” in politics, and a member of the local Baptist church.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Three of his & Sarah’s sons would serve in the Civil War (with one dying at Vicksburg), while their only daughter Cordelia graduated from Normal School (teacher-training academy) at age 15, then taught school for 7 years, before s
2 CONC he was married in Aug. 1860 in Jackson Township to the future-Captain John DOWNING.  After the Civil War, James and Sarah moved to the town of Andrew, Jackson Co., Iowa, where James died "of general debility” at the home of daught
2 CONC er & son-in-law Cordelia & John DOWNING.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/15733308/james-clark-dunham Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 17 2022, 23:43:21 UTC'
