1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Henry George Mechling was born in Butler County, PA on February 8, 1845. Henry was the eighth child of Christian Mechling, a farmer, and Susan Isabella Robinson. Henry wrote that he "worked long and hard to learn a
2 CONC  calling by which he could earn a livelihood" as a carpenter. On August 15, 1862 when he was 17 (military records show 19), he enlisted in the 137th PA Volunteer Regiment of Infantry, Company F (initially Co. I) in Prospect, Butle
2 CONC r County. The regiment was organized at Camp Curtin at Harrisburg and initially trained near Washington, D.C. During his nine-month enlistment in the Army of the Potomac as a Private, Henry's regiment was in service at Sugar Loaf 
2 CONC Mountain, Crampton's Gap at South Mountain, Antietam (Sharpsburg), Washington, D.C., Aquia Creek, the "Mud March," Belle Plains, Pollock's Mill Creek, and Chancellorsville. During this period, military records show that Henry was 
2 CONC present for every roll call. He was mustered out with his Company on June 1, 1863 at Harrisburg. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In June, when General Lee was threatening to invade Pennsylvania, Henry enlisted at Pittsburgh in the 56th Regiment of the Emergency Militia as a First Sargent in Company F. His term of service ended in December, and on January 5,
2 CONC  1864 he enlisted at Pittsburgh in the 14th PA Volunteer Regiment of Cavalry, Company L for a period of three years. On June 19, while covering the Union retreat from Lynchburg after General David Hunter's Raid, Henry was wounded 
2 CONC by gunshot at Liberty (now Bedford) three times "in the same day, but not at the same time." He then had to "travel 14 days and nights without any care except just cold water to pour over" his wounds to reduce inflammation. Two of
2 CONC  Henry's wounds were in the right thigh and left shoulder, but his most severe wound was to his upper left arm which required several inches of bone to be removed. Henry wrote that he had "been reduced from a robust, muscular youn
2 CONC g man, to total inability to do manual labor." <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT For four months, Henry was in the U.S. Army General Hospital at Gallipolis, OH. On October 20, 1864 Henry was given a 30-day furlough and was ordered to report subsequently to the U.S. Army General Hospital at Pittsburgh. Based on
2 CONC  his 1/2 disability, Henry was discharged from the U.S. Army on February 10, 1865 and returned to Butler. An operation was required two years after he was discharged so his wounds would heal properly. Henry petitioned and appealed
2 CONC  to the Federal government for years to improve his pension situation which he believed was inadequate and unfair. He was finally awarded an increase to $30 per month starting on March 4, 1911 by an Act of the U.S. Congress. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 27, 1866 Henry and Maria Moody of Westmoreland County were married in Brady's Bend by Methodist Episcopal Pastor George W. Moore. While living in Pennsylvania, Henry and Maria had three daughters: Lewella, Ida (died y
2 CONC oung), and Cora Etta. Henry was a member of Encampment Lodge, I.O.O.F of East Brady. In 1873 Henry moved to Fairmont, NE where he was involved initially in the farm implement business. There were few towns then between Fairmont an
2 CONC d Denver. Later, he expanded into the sale of sewing machines and wind-mills. In the 1876 Fairmont City Directory, Henry was listed as a carpenter. While in Fairmont, Henry and Maria had two more daughters: Iva May and Anna Martha
2 CONC . Here he was a member of the W. A. Webb Post No. 18 of the Grand Army of the Republic (G.A.R.). <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In the winter of 1878-79, Fairmont was nearly destroyed by a big fire which burned several buildings. In the fall of 1880 Henry moved to York and continued the same business there until September 1881 when he moved on to Wymore a 
2 CONC few months after the town was platted and recorded. At Wymore he worked as a real estate dealer and a Justice of the Peace, and was also engaged in contracting and building, and the selling of second-hand furniture. One of Henry’s
2 CONC  rulings as the Justice of the Peace was appealed all the way to the Nebraska Supreme Court in 1891 as Lininger et al. v. Glenn et al. In Wymore, Henry was a member of Local 2107 of The Knights of Labor, the Mutual Aid Union, and 
2 CONC the Coleman Post No. 115 of the G.A.R. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In February and March of 1885, Henry worked as a clerk for the Nebraska Commission on the Penitentiary. In the 1885 Official State Atlas of Nebraska, there were the names of leading business and professional men, and leading farme
2 CONC rs and land owners, in Gage County who aided in the publication of the atlas, including an entry for Messrs. Mechling, Allison, and Craig who were in the business of real estate and loans. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was listed in the 1885 Nebraska State Census as a farmer, but disabled. He and Maria operated a farm consisting of 40 acres of tilled land, 60 acres of permanent pasture, 75 acres of woodland, and 20 acres of unimproved land
2 CONC . They had six horses, five milk cows, eight other cattle, seven swine, and 175 of various poultry. Forty acres were planted in Indian corn, and one-half acre in Irish potatoes. The Mechlings had hired farm labor for 28 weeks duri
2 CONC ng the previous year. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry and Maria separated sometime in 1890 or perhaps earlier. In 1890 Henry was working in real estate in Denver. The 1892 St. Louis City Directory showed the peripatetic Henry G. Mechling working as a lawyer in St. Louis, Missou
2 CONC ri with Frederick W. Fout. Mr. Fout headed one of the most extensive and successful pension and claim businesses in the West, and was involved also in real estate. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry was in Oklahoma Territory in 1894. He married Kate E. May of Perry in March 19, 1894. Henry then moved to the small town of Parkdale in Fremont County, Colorado (in 1890 there were only 144 people in Parkdale). Perhaps this 
2 CONC was a result of the effects of the Panic of 1893 on the economy which did not end until 1897. The repeal of the Sherman Silver Act in 1893 sent most of the state into a depression, but because the Cripple Creek gold mines kept bus
2 CONC y, Fremont County remained relatively prosperous. Or did he move to Parkdale to be close to the mining operations there? Nearby Canon City served as a trade center and a gateway to the gold and silver mining camps to the west. Ano
2 CONC ther possibility was that this move was an outgrowth of his previous real estate work in Denver. There were good farms on the north bank of the Arkansas River near Parkdale where the soil was not too gravelly for cultivation.  <br
2 CONC />
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 20, 1895 Henry and Helen H. Fowler of Canon City were married by Pastor James F. Harris. Helen's maiden name was Helen H. Lloyd. During a major blizzard in November, 1895, exposure to the cold and the lack of circulation a
2 CONC nd sensation in his left arm from his Civil War wounds resulted in severe frostbite in his hand. He entered the Colorado State Soldiers' and Sailors' Home hospital in Monte Vista in May, 1896 for treatment for this condition which
2 CONC  required the partial amputation of three fingers. On his Application for Admission Henry stated that his occupation was "farmer." Henry was discharged in July, but stayed in the Monte Vista area until January, 1897. Henry may hav
2 CONC e been looking for non-farming work or some other business opportunities in the area.  <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1898 City Directory for Denver shows Henry living on Larimer St. Henry visited relatives in Pennsylvania in June, 1898 and said that he had been engaged in the gold mining business. When Henry Mechling was admitted to the Cent
2 CONC ral Branch of the National Home for Disabled Volunteer Soldiers in Dayton, OH on October 15, 1904, he had been working as a clerk. He was discharged at his request on August 19, 1909. Henry apparently made good use of the extensiv
2 CONC e library at the Soldier's Home because in the 1910 U.S. Census and the 1910 and 1911 City Directories for Dayton, Henry was listed as a pension attorney. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT The 1913 City Directory for Washington, D.C. showed that Henry was renting at 326 Indiana Avenue NW, next door to the previous home of Admiral Robley D. Evans and up the street from Blagden Row. Although he lived very close to the
2 CONC  old District Courthouse and the Pension Building in Judiciary Square, Henry may have been retired then, since there was no occupation listed for him. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry suffered from heart disease for many years. On February 10, 1920 Henry died of bronchial pneumonia and valvular heart disease, exactly fifty-five years after he was discharged from the U.S. Army. Henry's attending doctor was
2 CONC  John A. Stoutenburgh, M.D. The undertaker was A. J. Schippert. Henry's housekeeper, Martha E. Wheelock, paid Henry's final medical bill and funeral expenses. <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry G. Mechling was buried in Arlington National Cemetery in Virginia on February 12, 1920 in location 19652.
2 CONT
