1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Lutellus, son of Lutellus and Sarah Mecham Burdick was born in Provo, Utah County, Utah November 11, 1859. While still a boy, he took up the making of boots. He moved with his father to Spring City, Sanpete, Utah, 
2 CONC where he continued making shoes and boots.
2 CONT
2 CONT On November 6, 1884 he married Esther Caroline Zabriskie, daughter of Lewis Curtis and Sarah Ann Park Zabriskie. She was born in Spring City on July 19, 1867. In 1885 the family moved to Ferron, Utah where they engaged in farming 
2 CONC and raising cattle. Wherever Lutellus moved he continued to make and repair shoes and boots.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Sunnyside mine opened and started coke ovens, the Burdick family moved to Sunnyside. The men all found employment in the mines. Esther and her girls started a boarding house, furnishing meals and lunches for the miners. T
2 CONC hey accumulated quite a herd of cattle at Sunnyside. They grazed on the open range both summer and winter. The rustlers started stealing their cattle so they decided to file on homestead in the Indian reservation. Lutellus and his
2 CONC  married children made filings on land in Bluebell and Mountain Home, Utah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lutellus was a bee man. The bees were kept out at the ranch. In fact, he was the first state bee inspector in Duchesne County, a position he held for many years. It was a great pleasure to just sit and listen while he talked about
2 CONC  bees and their culture. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Justice of the Peace for a number of years. He was a very strict man and believed the law was made to be respected.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early days the young people were active in the social events. As the people had to entertain themselves, they had community plays. The family participated in these events. The Burdicks loved to sing and Beatrice often sang 
2 CONC duets with Porter Merrell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa Burdick finished his last pair of boots on March 4, 1936. They had been ordered by one of the Bird boys from over in the "Gulch". On March 8, 1936 he died at age 77 in his home in Bluebell.
