2 CONT Pioneering orthopedic surgeon who changed the way children with cerebral palsy were treated by his insistence that their intelligence was not necessarily limited by their physical disabilities, and that they could have productive 
2 CONC lives. He coined the term "cerebral palsy" to differentiate them from those born with mental retardation.
2 CONT
2 CONT He earned a B.S. from Princeton University in 1916 and an M.D. from the Johns Hopkins University School of Medicine in 1920. He became interested in children who suffered physical manifestations of brain damage while he was teachi
2 CONC ng at the Yale School of Medicine.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1932, Phelps published his first article on brain-damaged infants and spastic children. Four years later, he moved to Baltimore to establish a private practice in the treatment and rehabilitation of children with cerebral palsy
2 CONC . In 1937, he founded the Children's Rehabilitation Institute in Cockeysville, Maryland, and later moved the facility to Reisterstown. The institute was a training facility for physicians and therapists as well as a treatment cent
2 CONC er for children afflicted with cerebral palsy. Phelps also established cerebral palsy clinics in Baltimore and other major U.S. cities. He was a founder and first president of the American Academy for Cerebral Palsy, which was est
2 CONC ablished in 1947.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among his patients was Karen Killilea, the subject of the best-selling book "Karen" by her mother Marie. Marie wrote warmly of Dr. Phelps (under the pseudonym "Dr. B") as having been the first doctor who gave her hope that her dau
2 CONC ghter could live a normal life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of the Rev. Arthur Santforth Phelps and Gertrude Lindell Tappan. Married Naomi Lee Forsythe, 19 Sep 1925. One daughter.
