2 CONT He He resided in New Town, Connecticut Autumn 1635. Sixty disaffected members of the Puritan congregation in New Town, Massachusetts Bay colony moved to what is now known as Connecticut, establishing another New Town there. In 163
2 CONC 6, under the leadership of Thomas Hooker and Samuel Stone nearly all of the remaining parishioners moved from New Town, Massachusetts to New Town, Connecticut and Thomas Stanton may have come with these if he did not come with the
2 CONC  first group. At this time Connecticut was not a colony, only a group of settlements until 1667. The following year, 1637, they renamed their new settlement 'Hartford,' after Stone's English home, Hertford. In 1650 he established 
2 CONC a trading house in Stonington, Conn., on the Pawcatuck river. His family lived in New London for a few years until finally their permanent residence came to be on the Pawcatuck.
2 CONT
