2 CONT Stonington, Richard Anson Wheeler, Genealogical Pub Co, 1977.            
1 NOTE Born 30 Jul 1616, Warwick Co. England. He embarked at London EN, 02 Jan 1635 in the merchantman "Bonaventura". He went first to Virginia and then to Boston. He and Roger Williams, founder of the state of Rhode Island were commissi
2 CONC oned by the King to negotiate a treaty with the Uncas and the Narragansett Indians. He helped draft and was a signer of the Constitution of the Colony of Conn. In June, 1636, Rev. Thomas Hooker and 100 other persons came from Newt
2 CONC own (Cambridge), Massachusetts and founded Hartford, Connecticut. In February, 1639, there were 127 property holders in Hartford, and among them were Dr. Thomas Lord and his son-in-law, Thomas Stanton. Thomas is believed to have b
2 CONC een in the original 100 settlers of Hartford where he and his family lived until moving to Stonington near his trading-house. The services of Stanton, as interpreter during the Pequot War, were invaluable. He was moreover a man of
2 CONC  trust and intelligence, and his knowledge of the country and of the natives made him a useful pioneer and counselor in all land questions, as well as difficulties with the Indians. He served through this war. Special mention is m
2 CONC ade of his bravery in the battle of Farifield Swamp, where he came close to losing his life. He was commissioned to set up New London County, and in 1650 he established a trading house in Stonington CT on the Pawcatuck river. His 
2 CONC family lived in New London for a few years until finally their permanent residence came to be on the Pawcatuck. Thomas married Ann LORD in 1637, daughter of Dr. Thomas and Dorothy (BIRD) LORD. She was born 18 Sep 1614 in Towcester
2 CONC  Northam EN. Thomas died 02 Dec 1676 and Ann in 1688, both in Stonington CT.
1 NOTE His Life
