2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Stanton was the fifth child of Anna Lord & Thomas Stanton. He was born in 1646/7 at Hartford, Connecticut, where his birth family resided at that time. He was baptized there 21 Mar 1646/7.[1][2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Stanton removed with his parents to Stonington and settled on a large tract of land which his father had bought of a Narragansett Indian chief for a half bushel of wampum. The sachem's child was a captive and was redeemed b
2 CONC y the aid of Thomas Stanton, so the Indian sold the land as part payment of the price. In 1669 he was appointed assistant magistrate to hold court in New London. In 1685 he leased land in Charlestown, Rhode Island, formerly a part
2 CONC  of Westerly "where I do now live", showing that he had moved thither.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT An excerpt from James Savage First Settlers of New England reads: Joseph Stanton, "of Stonington, younger brother of the preceding [Robert Stanton] was yet old enough to be propound for freeman 1669, may be, tho. not prob. that so
2 CONC n-in-law of William Mead of Roxbury, in whose will of 1683 he is named "tho. undutiful," for we can hardly doubt, that our Stonington man had some years. bef. married Hannah Lord, or even third wife a Prentice. But no children is 
2 CONC given him."[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT King Philip's War
2 CONT
2 CONT Both he and his son, Joseph Stanton Jr., volunteered for the Narragansett War.[5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriages
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Stanton married at least three times:
2 CONT
2 CONT He married first Hannah (Mead) Stanton on 19 Jun 1673 in Roxbury, Massachusetts.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT They had 2 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Stanton Jr.[1]
2 CONT Hannah (Stanton) York[1]
2 CONT He married, second, 13 Aug 1677 Hannah (Lord) Stanton, his cousin.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT They had 2 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Stanton[1]
2 CONT Rebecca (Stanton) Babcock[1]
2 CONT He married, third or fourth, Unknown (Prentice) Stanton.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT They had 3 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Stanton[1]
2 CONT Daniel Stanton[1]
2 CONT Samuel Stanton[1]
2 CONT The Sheffield House
2 CONT
2 CONT It is probable that Joseph Stanton constructed The Sheffield House, in what was then the Town of Westerly, RI, sometime after 1685. The one-and-a-half-story gambrel-roofed farmhouse was added to the National Register of Historic P
2 CONC laces in 1976.[6]
2 CONT
2 CONT Death
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph Stanton died in 1714.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources
2 CONT
