1 NOTE {geni:about_me} No Evidence she was connected to [http://www.geni.com/people/William-Nickerson-II/6000000003490631675 William Nickerson] & [http://www.geni.com/people/Anne-Nickerson/6000000003490631688 Anne Busby]!! UnRecorded at 
2 CONC the Nickerson Family Association!! Surely  was not born in 1621 in America or CT. 
2 CONT
2 CONT support for that may be found (among other places) in Burton N. Derick, "The Fictional Hattie (Nickerson) Lord," ''Connecticut Nutmegger'' 32 (Sept. 1999): 189.  He asserts, among other things that this
2 CONT
2 CONT '''was an error made in the ''Boston Transcript'' of Nov 5 1823, and perpetuated ever since.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Unknown_%2826927%29
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * From http://ncpermar.blogspot.com/2007/07/william-lord-and-hattie-nickersonlydia.html WILLIAM LORD AND HATTIE NICKERSON/LYDIA BUCKLAND] comments:
2 CONT **1. Hattie Nickerson, as the first wife of William-2 Lord, is a phantom with good arguments to back up this statement. The inclusion of "Hattie" into online pedigrees keeps proliferating. When I first started looking into the Lor
2 CONC d genealogy, Hattie was assigned a birth date of 1620 in Connecticut, some said Saybrook. Lately, changing information has been cropping up - the dates have been pushed back as far as 1612 and forward as much as 1627 and the birth
2 CONC  location has been shifting eastward to Massachusetts and even England, in order, perhaps, to make the myth conform more closely to known facts.
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1620+/- is an extremely early date for a European child to have been born anywhere in Connecticut. This was the first red light for me that something was suspiciously wrong with "Hattie".
2 CONT
2 CONT * In 1636, Gov Winthrop constructed a fort at Saybrook Point, at the mouth of the Connecticut River, to turn away the Dutch from taking possession of their own lands. Afterwards, the Village of Saybrook developed around the fort. 
2 CONC So, Saybrook just wasn't available in 1620 to be Hattie's birthplace - it was unsettled, undeveloped Dutch wilderness.
2 CONT
2 CONT * William Nickerson and Anne Busby, however, DID exist, but didn't marry in England until about 1627/8.
2 CONT
2 CONT * The Busby-Nickerson family didn't emigrate from England to the Massachusetts Bay Colony until 1637. They brought 4 children with them: Nicholas, Robert, Elizabeth, and Anne (another child named Thomas died as an infant 4 years b
2 CONC efore they departed). Once in North America, the couple had 5 more children: Samuel, John, Sarah, William, and Joseph. There is no record of a child named Hattie / Harriet / Mehitable.
2 CONT
2 CONT * They arrived in Salem, Massachusetts Bay Colony, on 20 Jun 1637 (well after the Lord family had left Boston to help found Hartford, Connecticut). The Busby-Nickerson family is known never to have set foot in Connecticut, but mov
2 CONC ed from the Boston area onto Cape Cod, about 1640. Then the Busby-Nickerson family moved on to Monomoit on Cape Cod in 1656 after purchasing the land from the Natives. The area is now called Chatham.
2 CONT
2 CONT * No mention of a Hattie Nickerson existed at all before an article was published in the "Boston Evening Transcript" of Nov 5 1823 giving this name as William Lord's first wife. This article was published nearly 160 years after he
2 CONC r supposed death. Lord's actual first wife still remains unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT * According to the book "Genealogy of the Descendants of Thomas Lord. . .", William was baptized 27 Dec 1618 in Towcester, England. He came to New England with his family in 1635 as a teenager and moved with his family in 1636 to 
2 CONC Hartford. (William Lord never returned to Massachusetts after the CT migration.) He married 1st (about 1642), quote: "______ ______"; the author only parenthetically notes: ("The Boston Transcript" of Nov 5 1823 names the wife as 
2 CONC Hattie NICKERSON). Farther on, in spite of that previous note he included, the author continues to present William's first wife as, quote: "_______ _______". This does not illustrate a strong embrace of the "Hattie Theory" by the 
2 CONC author. March 23, 2014 at 6:27 PM
2 CONT  Anonymous said...2. Sources:
2 CONT ** "The Fictional Hattie (Nickerson) Lord", "Connecticut Nutmegger", v.32 no.2 Sept.1999 Burton N. Derick, VP, Genealogy, Nickerson Family Association Roberta G. Bratti, CSG#3011 Page 189-192. A transcript of the article can be se
2 CONC en here: [http://]remleml[dot]com/family/source.php?view=preview&sid=S293& Accessed 26 May 2012.
2 CONT ** "Chapter V.: William Nickerson and the Settlement of Monomoit." From "A history of Chatham Massachusetts: Formerly the Constablewick or Village of Monomoit with Maps and Illustrations and Numerous Genealogical Notes", William C
2 CONC  Smith F. B. & F. P. Goss, Publishers Hyannis, Mass.; 1909 Pages 55-58 [http]archive[dot]org/details/historyofchatham00smit Accessed 26 May 2012.
2 CONT ** "Genealogy of the Descendants of Thomas Lord: an Original Proprietor and Founder of Hartford, Conn., in 1637", compiled by Kenneth Lord, New York: 1946 Pages 71, 264. (No free online source for this book is known to me.)
2 CONT ** Photograph of the Busby-Nickerson memorial plaque at Chatham, MA, "William Nickerson and the History of Early Chatham", naming the couple's children. There is no mention of a Hattie/Harriet/Mehitable. The Nickerson Family Assoc
2 CONC iation, [http://]nickersonassoc[dot]org/willNickerson.shtml Accessed 26 May 2012.
2 CONT ** Telephone conversation with several members of The Nickerson Family Association located at Chatham, Cape Cod, Massachusetts --- I was assured that no known Hattie / Harriet / Mehitable Nickerson existed in North America in the 
2 CONC 17th Century. They pointed out the marriage and emigration dates of the Busby-Nickerson couple which render the question moot and referred me to the "The Fictional Hattie (Nickerson) Lord" article in the Connecticut Nutmegger. The
2 CONC y told me that they often had to field calls about "Hattie Nickerson". However, they said that a number of bona fide Hattie / Harriet Nickersons crop up in New England in the 19th Century, though none had direct associations with 
2 CONC the Lord family. August 2008.
2 CONT ** "Living in the Past" (Lord family history and research), Access pathway: "The Lords of Towcester" ---> "...an end unto my fewe dayes in this life" ---> "William Lord: Age 13 on arrival in Hartford" [www][dot]living-in-the-past[
2 CONC dot]com/index.html#home . Acessed 26 May 2012.
2 CONT ** I'm sorry, but perpetuating the Hattie Nickerson myth does a big disservice to the real woman who married William Lord and bore those children. If you are able to find real sources for her existence, please let me know. I love 
2 CONC Cape Cod and would love to be a descendent of one it's early families.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Apparently there is no evidence Harriet Lord (wife of William Lord) was connected to William Nickerson and Anne Busby. This connection is probably an error made in the Boston Transcript of Nov. 5, 1823, and perpetuated ever since.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT She is not Hattie Nickerson the person is a fiction from an article in 1863. It was likely misquoted as the name, but the Nickerson/Busby that Hattie is linked to does exist. They NEVER had a child named Harriet, Hattie, etc.</lin
2 CONC e><line /><line>2/2/21
