1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Rebecca Phillips===
2 CONT * Christening 13 May 1632	St. Peter's, Great Berkhamsted, Hertfordshire, England
2 CONT * Died in England after say 1670
2 CONT * Daughter of William Phillips and Mary Bishop
2 CONT * Wife of Robert Lord, married 1650, son of Thomas Lord and Dorothy Bird
2 CONT
2 CONT Returned to England 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===family===
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of Robert and Rebecca Lord were:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Robert, b. Apr. 6, 1661
2 CONT * Thomas, b. May 18, 1663, (died young)
2 CONT * Mary, b. July 13, 1668,
2 CONT * Sarah, most likely born in England, her parents evidently having gone there to end their days.
2 CONT
2 CONT The said Robert Lord and his family, while in Boston, lived in a house there that came from his father-inlaw."[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Mentioned in Susanna Phillips 1655 will: 
2 CONT
2 CONT * to daughter Rebecca Lord  five pounds; 
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===disputed origins===
2 CONT
2 CONT However her christening as daughter of William Phillips, published in 2013, would seem to settle the dispute.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anderson, Robert Charles; George F. Sanborn; and Melinde Lutz Sanborn. The Great Migration: Immigrants to New England, 1634-1635. (NEHGS, 1999-2011), 4:333.
2 CONT
2 CONT > 'ROBERT LORD, bp. Towcester, Northamptonshire, 12 May 1620 [Flagg 274] ... m. by 1651 Rebecca _______ ... daughter of CHRISTOPHER STANLEY {1635, Boston} or WILLIAM PHILLIPS (1639, Charlestown} [GDMNH 548; Pope 430; Savage 3:416,
2 CONC  4:164].'
2 CONT
2 CONT Savage, James. A Genealogical Dictionary of the First Settlers of New England: Showing Three Generations of Those Who Came Before May, 1692, on the Basis of Farmer's Register. (Boston: Little, Brown, and Co, 1860-1862).
2 CONT
2 CONT > "CHRISTOPHER [Stanley], Boston, came in the Elizabeth and Ann, from London 1635, aged 21, with w[ife]. Susanna, 31, join[ed]. our ch[urch]. 16 May 1641, and was adm[itted]. freem[an]. 2 June foll[owing]. He is call[ed]. taylor, 
2 CONC wh[ich]. in my opin[ion]. means a mem[ber]. of the gr[eat]. comp[any]. of Merch[ant]. Taylors of London; was a capt[ain]. and d[ied]. early in 1646, leav[ing]. good est[ate]. to his wid[ow]. beside sev[eral]. legacies and devises,
2 CONC  but names no ch[ildren]. Of his will. 27 Mar. in that y[ea]r. abstr[act]. is in Geneal. Reg. IV. 52, and therein it appears not to have been bro[ugh]t. to pro[bate]. until 29 Jan. 1650. His wid[ow]. m[arried]. William Phillips, a
2 CONC nd 10 Sept. 1650, with assent of her new h[usband]. made her wll of the est[ate]. that came from Stanley pro[bated]. 2 Aug. 1655. See Phillips, or a larger abstr[act]. in Geneal. Reg. V. 447.
2 CONT
2 CONT From A Genealogy of the Warne Family in America: Principally the Descendants of ... By George Warne Labaw [https://books.google.com/books?id=drRYAAAAMAAJ&lpg=PA56&ots=ka7elpdk7k&dq=christopher%20stanley%201646%20susannah&pg=PA56#v
2 CONC =onepage&q&f=false page 56] 
2 CONT
2 CONT >Mary Lord, b. July 13, 1668, at Cambridge, Mass., m. Thomas Carhart in November, 1691, and lived on Staten Island until the spring of 1695, when they removed to Woodbridge, N. J. Three sons were born to this union, John, Robert, 
2 CONC and William Carhart. Thomas Carhart died in Woodbridge. "Some time between Mch. 16, 1695, the date of his will, and April 6, 1696, the date that the will was probated." Of Mary Lord's ancestry we will say that she was the daughter
2 CONC  of Robert Lord, of Cambridge, Mass., b. in England about 1627, and Rebecca, formerly .of Boston, Mass., who (according to Carhart Genealogy, p. 25), was the daughter of Major William Phillips, of Saco, Me., and Boston, Mass. This
2 CONC , however, is an error, as Rebecca Lord's maiden name was Stanley, not Phillips.   William Phillips was her stepfather, not her father. Her father's name, who was her mother's previous husband, was Christopher Stanley, a sea capta
2 CONC in, [SIC] and a man of good estate, who with his wife came over in the same ship with Robert Lord's father and family, and settled in Boston, Mass., so that she also must have been born in England.
2 CONT
2 CONT >"The Pioneers of Massachusetts," by Charles H. Pope, 221 Columbus Ave., Boston, Mass., gives us: "Capt. Christopher Stanley, aged 32, with wife Susannah, aged 31, came in the Elizabeth and Ann, 1635. Settled in Boston; freeman, J
2 CONC une 2, 1641. Wife Susannah admitted to church 23 June 1639. '''Children: Rebecca, (m. Robert Lord); Martha, (m. Richard Thurston).''' He (Christopher Stanley) died 27 Mch. 1646; will dated 27 Mch. 1646; probated 19 Mch. 1649." Als
2 CONC o from same we have, p. 291: "Rebecca Lord's mother gave her part of a house in which she was already living (in Boston) adjoining her sister Thurston's, Aug. 18, 1652." Again, from same we have: "Capt. Richard Thurston and wife M
2 CONC artha, and Robert Lord and Rebecca, his wife, living in England, sold their right in a certain house in Boston in connection with Lieut. Phillips, Jan. 8, 1656." 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources===
2 CONT * https://www.werelate.org/wiki/Person:Rebecca_Phillips_(29)_
2 CONT * William1 Phillips, in Bradley, Hal. The English Origin of William1 Phillips of Charlestown and Boston, Massachusetts, and Saco Maine. American Genealogist (D.L. Jacobus). (Jul, Oct 2013), 86:300. "Rebecca Phillips, bp. (St. Pete
2 CONC r's) 13 May 1632 "
2 CONT * Labaw, George Warne. A Genealogy of the Warne Family in America; Principally the Descendants of Thomas Warne, Born 1652, Died 1722, One of the Twenty-four Proprietors of East New Jersey. New York: Frank Allaben Genealogical, 191
2 CONC 1. 57-59. Print. GoogleBooks [https://books.google.com/books?id=drRYAAAAMAAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false Link]
2 CONT * [https://books.google.com/books?id=arxBAQAAMAAJ&lpg=RA1-PP7&ots=YB8Xtfe3up&dq=richard%20thurston%20martha%20phillips&pg=RA1-PP7#v=onepage&q=richard%20thurston%20martha%20phillips&f=false page 7] of Suffolk Deeds, Volume 2 By Suf
2 CONC folk County (Mass.)
