2 CONC jgU#v=onepage&q=John%20Ingersoll%201615&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT On 28 November 1654, he was fined 10 sh. by the Connecticut Particular Court for breach of the law against lying.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1679 he wrote a "relation" or confession, considered "extraordinary" by Greene. Its contents suggest he suffered from clinical depression.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT This information (down to Abigail Ingersoll, m Guy Woodworth) is from _The Ingersoll Family in America 1629-1925_, compiled by Lillian Drake Avery. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT John was the second of seven children born to Thomas and Margery (Easton) Ingersoll. John was born in England and immigrated to America (Salem, Mass.), at the age of 14, with his older brother, Richard. He later moved to Hartford,
2 CONC  Conn. He was married to: (1) Dorothy (Lord) in 1651 and they had three daughters: Hannah, Dorothy and Margery 'Mary'. He next married (2) Abigail (Bascom) in 1657 and they had five children: Abigail, Sarah, Abijah, Hester and Tho
2 CONC mas. Later he married (3) Mary (Hunt) in 1668 and they had seven children: John, Abel, Ebenezer, Joseph, Mary, Benjamin and Jonathan.
2 CONT
