2 CONT He and his brother, Sergeant Isaac Graves, were killed by Indians in an attack on Hatfield, 19 Sept. 1677. Hatfield was the most exposed, as it was thefrontier town. In consequence of the numerous attacks by the Indians, the peopl
2 CONC e, to better protect themselves, built a stockade, probably in the autumn of 1675, composed of posts of timber set in the ground, and about ten feet high. This stockade was built on both sides of Main Street, some twelve or fiftee
2 CONC n rodsfrom the east and west lines of the street, extending north from the Northampton road, not far from 100 rods. this stockade enclosed the bulk of the village. the houses of Isaac and John graves were within the stockade. Unfo
2 CONC rtunately for them, on September 19, 1677, they were both employed in building a house forJohn Graves, Jr., about half a mile above the northerly end of the stockade, on a lot adjoining that of Sergeant Benjamin Waite. Without any
2 CONC  warning or thought of danger, they were attacked by the Indians, and Isaac and John were shot down while engaged, as one tradition has it, 'in laying shingles on the roof of the house,' and with them were likewise two other men w
2 CONC ho were working witht hem, John Atchinson and John Cooper. Eight others were killed, and we presume scalped, as the account speaks of them as being disfigured, and seventeen were made prisoners. All but one, Obadiah Dickinson, wer
2 CONC e women and children.
2 CONT
2 CONT John's first five children were born in Wethersfield, CT and his last fivechildren were born in Hatfield, MA.
2 CONT
