2 CONT == Biography == 
2 CONT John Graves (aka Greaves) was the son of Thomas Graves (____ - 1662) and Sarah Whiting Graves (c.1590 - 1666). He was baptized December 26,1626 at Thundridge, Hertfordshire, England.<ref name=FAG>[[#FAG|Find a Grave]]</ref> (Alter
2 CONC nate birth: about 1622 in England.) It appears that he came to New England with his parents. He was made a freeman on 18 May 1654 in Hartford, Hartford County, Connecticut.  He first owned land in Hartford and Wethersfield, Connec
2 CONC ticut. He then moved to Hatfield, Massachusetts, in 1661.<ref name=FAG/> John and his brother, Sergeant Isaac Graves, were killed by Indians inan attack on Hatfield, September 19, 1677.<ref name=FAG/> "3. John, s. of Thomas, (1) H
2 CONC at., slain by Indians Sept. 19, 1677. He m. (1) Mary, dau. of Lt. Samuel Smith. She prob. died Dec. 16, 1688. (2) Mary, widow of John Wyatt of Haddam, CT. Children -- John, Mary m. (1) Jan. 15, 1667 Samuel Ball of Springfield, (2)
2 CONC  April 2, 1679 EdwardStebbins; Isaac; Samuel; Sarah, m. Edward Stebbins; Elizabeth, b. Dec. 9, 1662, m. Thomas Jones; Daniel, b. Dec. 7, 1664; Ebenezer, b. Dec.20, 1666; Bethiah, b. June 28, 1668, d. Jan 21, 1669; Nathaniel, b. Ju
2 CONC ne 10, 1671."<ref>[[#Judd|Judd]]</ref> Children with first wife, Mary Smith (____ - 1668):<ref>[[#AA01]]</ref> : [[Graves-210|John Graves]] : [[Graves-211|Mary Graves Ball Stebbins Warriner]] : [[Graves-204|Samuel Graves]] (1657 -
2 CONC  1731) : [[Graves-198|Sarah Graves Stebbins]] : [[Graves-3256|Elizabeth Graves Jones]] : [[Graves-3158|Daniel Graves]] : [[Graves-3254|Ebenezer Graves]] : [[Graves-3253|Bethiah Graves]] : Isaac Graves (father of Isaac Graves Jr) B
2 CONC enjamin?
2 CONT
2 CONT Child with second wife, Mary Bronson Wyatt (1627 - 1688): : Nathaniel Graves (1671 - ____) Burial: Center Cemetery, Whately, Franklin County, Massachusetts<ref name=FAG/> John Graves, 1st, of Wethersfield, Conn., and Hatfield, Mas
2 CONC s. Married,1st, Mary, daughter of Lieut. Samuel Smith ; 2d, Mary, widow of John Wyatt, of Haddam, Conn. He had ten children mostly by his first wife. He was a civil engineer as well as farmer, a man "of probity and education." He 
2 CONC and his brother Isaac, were killed by Indians, Sept. 19, 1677, in King Philip's War.<ref>[[#Graves|Graves]]</ref> John Graves was made a free man by the General Court of Election 5/18/1654, from Wethersfild along with Philip Smith
2 CONC , Thomas & James Wright.Another John Graves was made free before the Court 5/21/1657.<ref>[[#T01]]</ref> Either John Graves moved back to Hatfield, MA before 1677 or was visiting there when him and his brother, Isaac Graves, were 
2 CONC killed by Indians while working on a house for John's son, John, who was soon to be married. Killed by the Indians September 19, 1677 while working on a house for his son John Graves, Jr. in Hatfield. Freeman in Wethersfield, Conn
2 CONC ecticut He was a man of property and education, and was frequently employed torun the lines of towns. A carpenter and farmer.
