1 OCCU Sergeant in King Philip's War, Carpenter, Solider
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Isaac Graves (2) was born probably as early as 1620 in England, and died 19 Sept. 1677, killed in an Indian attack on the Hatfield settlement.  He married Mary Church, daughter of Richard and Anna Church.  She came
2 CONC  from England in 1637, and died 9 June 1695.
2 CONT
2 CONT He came to New England with his father and settled in Hartford, Conn. before 1645.  He was made freeman at General Court, Boston, Mass. 16 May 1669, was Sergeant in the Colonial Militia, and Clerk of the Writs for Hatfield, to whi
2 CONC ch he moved in 1661.  He was a prominent man in his time, and one of the representatives of that portion of Hadley (later Hatfield) who appeared before the General Court at Boston in favor of separate church and town rights for Ha
2 CONC tfield.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isaac was a carpenter, and was a resident of Wethersfield, Conn. before 1645.  He "keyed down" Goffe's Bridge in Wethersfield in 1648.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the continuance of what is usually called "King Philip's War", the inhabitants of these isolated frontier towns were naturally filled with forebodings of danger to their homes and families.  For their own protection and saf
2 CONC ety, the inhabitants of Northampton, Hadley and Hatfield practically united for mutual defense and assistance.  These three town were occupied by troops from the eastern portion of the Colony of Massachusetts Bay.  At Northampton 
2 CONC 26 soldiers were located, at Hadley 30, and at Hatfield 36.  This was the most exposed, as it was the frontier town.  Then they had a committee, called a council of war, chosen from the several towns.  Among the members was Sergea
2 CONC nt Isaac Graves.  The object of this council of war was to provide better security to the inhabitants of the several towns.  They counselled with the commander, Major Appleton, relative to the ways and means best to be used for th
2 CONC e protection of life and property.
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 25, 1675, a scouting party of ten was sent out and fell into an ambuscade, and nine were killed. Then on the 17th of September, Captain Lathrop and his company and several teamsters from Deerfield were attacked and massa
2 CONC cred.  Only a few escaped.  Sixty-four were buried in one grave as the result of the "Bloody Brook" fight.  Seventeen of the sixty-four were Deerfield men.  In consequence of the numerous attacks by the Indians, the people, to bet
2 CONC ter protect themselves, built a stockade, probably in the atumn of 1675, composed of posts of timber set in the ground, and about ten feet high.  This stockade was built on both sides of Main Street, some twelve or fifteen rods fr
2 CONC om the east and west lines of the street, extending north from the Northampton road, not far from 100 rods.  This stockade enclosed the bulk of the village.  The houses of Isaac and John Graves were within the stockade.  Unfortuna
2 CONC tely for them, on September 19, 1677, they were both employed in building a house for John Graves, Jr., about half a mile above the northerly end of the stockade, on a lot adjoining that of Sergeant Benjamin Waite.  Without any wa
2 CONC rning or thought of danger, they were attacked by the Indians, and Isaac and John were shot down while engaged, as one tradition has it, "in laying shingles on the roof of the house," and with them were likewise two other men who 
2 CONC were working with them, John Atchinson and John Cooper.  Eight others were killed, and we presume scalped, as the account speaks of them as being disfigured, and seventeen were made prisoners.  All but one, Obadiah Dickinson, were
2 CONC  women and children.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the early vital records of Hartford, Conn., the first 4 children of Isaac are given as being born there - Mary on 5 July 1647, Isaac on 21 Aug. 1650, Rebecca on 3 July 1652, and Samuel on 5 Oct. 1655.  (R‑200)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children - Graves
2 CONT
2 CONT   8.  Mary Graves, b. 5 July 1647, m. Eleazer Frary, 28 Jan. 1665.  It was previously believed that this Mary Graves m(2) Joseph Haskell.  However, it is much more likely that the Mary Graves who married Joseph Haskell was a daugh
2 CONC ter of Mark Graves and Amy ‑‑‑‑‑‑ and a granddaughter of immigrant Samuel Graves of Lynn, MA (genealogy 83).
2 CONT
2 CONT   9.  Isaac Graves, b. 22 (or 21) Aug. 1650, never married, d. before 1677.
2 CONT
2 CONT   10.  Rebecca Graves, b. 3 July 1652 (or 1653), never married, d. before 1677.
2 CONT
2 CONT +11.  Samuel Graves, b. 1 (or 5) Oct. 1655, m(1) Sarah Colton, 31 Oct. 1678, m(2) Deliverance ‑‑‑‑‑‑, 1 Jan. 1690, d. 8 Feb. 1692.
2 CONT
2 CONT   12.  Sarah Graves, m. Benjamin Barrett (of Sunderland, Mass.), 27 April 1677, d. Sunderland.
2 CONT
2 CONT   13.  Elizabeth Graves, b. 16 March 1661, m. Benjamin Hastings, 1683, d. before 8 Feb. 1697.  He was b. 9 Aug. 1659, m(2) Mary Parsons (of Northampton, Mass.), d. 8 Feb. 1697 (Hatfield).
2 CONT
2 CONT +14.  John Graves, b. 1664, m. Sarah Banks, 26 Oct. 1686, probably d. 1746.
2 CONT
2 CONT +15.  Hannah Graves, b. 24 Jan. 1666, m. William Sackett, 27 Nov. 1689.
2 CONT
2 CONT +16.  Jonathan Graves, b. 24 Jan. 1666, m(1) Sarah Parsons, m(2) Mary King, 19 July 1721, d. 12 Oct. 1737.
2 CONT
2 CONT   17.  Mehitable Graves, b. 1 Oct. 1671, m(1) Richard Morton (of Hatfield), 29 Jan. 1690, m(2) William Worthington, d. 22 March 1742.  She moved to Colchester, Conn. with William.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.gravesfa.org/gen168.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Of Hat. Slain by Indians.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: <http://www.kinnexions.com/kinnexions/hadley/fg02/fg02_134.htm>
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ISAAC2 GRAVES (THOMAS1) was born Abt. 1620 in England, and died 19 Sep 1677 in Hatfield.  He married MARY CHURCH 1646 in Hartford, Hartford, Connecticut, daughter of RICHARD CHURCH and ANNE MARSH.  She was born 02 Nov 1632 in Engl
2 CONC and, and died 09 Jun 1695 in Connecticut.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for ISAAC GRAVES: --of Hartford, Connecticut;--it appears the same Isaac married either Hannah or Mary Church;--it was Mary Church;--killed in the Indian attack on Hatfield (King Philip's War);--
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for MARY CHURCH: --came from England in 1637;- 	
2 CONT Children of ISAAC GRAVES and MARY CHURCH are: 	i.	MARY3 GRAVES, b. 05 Jul 1647; m. ELEAZER FRARY, 28 Jan 1665. 	ii.	ISAAC GRAVES, b. 22 Aug 1650; d. Abt. 1677.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://familytreemaker.genealogy.com/users/p/o/p/Sandra-Popiel/FILE/2113text.txt
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sgt. Isaac Graves was killed by indians
2 CONT --------------------
