1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==William Andrews==
2 CONT * BIRTH 1595 Essex, England
2 CONT * DEATH 3 Aug 1659 (aged 63–64) Hartford, Hartford County, Connecticut, USA
2 CONT * BURIAL Ancient Burying Ground Hartford, Hartford County, Connecticut, USA
2 CONT * MEMORIAL ID 17096164 Photos added by Ronni Croft & Sue
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biographical Summary:==
2 CONT '''William Andrews''', freeman, Massachusetts, March 4, 1634-5; constable, Newtown, October, 1636; an original proprietor of Hartford, received 30 acres in the division of 1639—40, and his home-lot was south of the Little River, o
2 CONC n land now included in the West Park. He was the first schoolmaster, teaching from 1643 until 1656; town clerk, 1651-1658. He died in 1659; will dated April 1; inventory August 8, 1659, £211. 14. 11. He married  Abigail, mentioned
2 CONC  in his will, and he also names "bro. George Grave." His widow married Nathaniel Bearding. [2]  
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==  
2 CONT William Andrews. William and Mary Andrews were the emigrant ancestors of the Andrews line. But little is known as to William Andrews' early life and nothing whatever is known with reference to his wife Mary. There were several Wil
2 CONC liam Andrews in the Colonies during the early days of the Puritans and it is often difficult to distinguish between them. About all that is known of his early life is contained in the following statements from Pope's Pioneers of M
2 CONC assachusetts, and it is certain that this does not all apply to our William Andrews. This book states that "Godly parents prought him up until be was 17 years of age." That he was apprenticed at Ipswich, England, and came first to
2 CONC  Charlestown and that in his absence his wife moved to Cambridge, "which pleased him." He was made Freeman March 4, 1634-5, and that he lived in Cambridge and sold all his house and lands and rights n Cambridge Sept. 25, 1637. Tha
2 CONC t his wife died in 1640. The book contains other statements but they evidently apply to another William Andrews who came to Cambridge a few years after our William Andrews went to Hartford and the writer of the book confused the t
2 CONC wo William Andrews. This second William Andrews was a mariner. The History of Massachusetts 1630 to 1877 also states that ancestor William Andrews was a mariner, but also confuses him with his follower at Cambridge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestor Wm. Andrews first appeared at Cambridge, so far as reliable records go, Dec. 1, 1634. But he was probably there some time before then. He soon took a prominent plan in the affairs of the town. His ability to do this was o
2 CONC wing perhaps to his superior education. He was afterwards a school teacher and was no doubt well educated for those times. He wrote an excellent hand, as is indicated by the old Hartford Records, written by him when town clerk and
2 CONC  county recorder. His home lot in Cambridge, then called Newtown, was on the northeast corner of Dunster and Winthrop streets and occupied about one-fourth of the block, surrounded by Dunster, Winthrop, Mt. Auburn and Holyoke stre
2 CONC ets. Dec. 1, 1634, William Andrews was granted five acres in the West Field. This Field was about one mile northwest of the settlement. Aug. 20, 1635, all the meadow land, undivided, belonging to Cambridge, was ordered measured an
2 CONC d divided to every man, according to his several proportions. In this division, William received two and one-half acres. On Feb. 8, 1635-36, William owned two houses in the town. It may be that one of these was for Francis Andrews
2 CONC , who seemed to be a younger brother of William, but Francis was then not married.
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 4, 1635, William was made Freeman in Boston, the county seat, together with Thomas Scott, Thomas and Timothy Stanley, Samuel Greenhill and William Pantry, all men intimately associated with William Andrews.
2 CONT
2 CONT William also was chosen one of the nine townsmen or selectmen for the town for one year on Nov. 23, 1635, at a general meeting of the town. He was also, at the same meeting, chosen constable for the year following. The constables 
2 CONC during those years, at least until 1666, were always townsmen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Oct. 27, 1636, according to the books of record of Mass. Bay Colony, Newtown presented a book of their records under the hand of William Andrews, constable, John Benjamin and William Spencer.
2 CONT
2 CONT He attended a meeting of selectmen on the 7th of Dec., 1635. At this meeting it was ordered that a bridge be built on the town side of the river, down to a low water mark, and a ladder on the other side. Apparently this was a swam
2 CONC py place, called a "ship marsh," on the town side of the Charles river, which they bridged so as to reach the ferry, over the river; while on the other side the banks were high and a ladder was needed. There were but five of the n
2 CONC ine selectmen present at this meeting, so they ordered that whosoever appears not within half an hour after the ringing of the bell shall pay for the first offense six pence, and for the second day twelve pence, and double it ever
2 CONC y day, unless he has a just excuse such as may give satisfaction to the rest of the company. The following meetings were much better attended. On Jan. 4, 1635-36, William attended the meeting and seven other selectmen were present
2 CONC . They granted some lots in the pine swamp field to several persons, two acres being granted to James Hosmer, "provided he buy a house in the town or else to return again to the town." On the 8th of February, 1635-36, all the sele
2 CONC ctmen were present. Various orders were entered in reference to land. It was ordered that whosoever hath or hereafter shall have any ground lying in the bounds of the town and shall desire to let or sell the same, he shall not let
2 CONC  or sell the same to anyone who is an inhabitant of the town, or will be within twelve months thereafter upon the forfeiture of all such land unto the town. (Apparently they were seeking to get new comers.) That there shall be one
2 CONC  acre railed in with double rail, for dry cattle to lie in at night. A list was made showing 38 persons having 78 houses in the town, many persons having more than one. William Andrews had two, with a population of about 600 perso
2 CONC ns, including servants and slaves.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the meeting of March, 1635-36, the selectmen agreed with Richard Rice to keep 100 cows for three months, and to have ten pounds after the ships come in, or in June, also to have three men to help him the first 14 days, and one 
2 CONC man the next seven days. He to fetch the cows into the town every morning out of the common, half an hour after the sun is up, at the furthermost and to bring them into the barn half an hour before the sun goeth down; and to pay t
2 CONC hree pence a cow every night he leaveth out any.
2 CONT
2 CONT The meeting of June 6, 1636, was probably the last attended by the selectmen William Andrews, Thomas Hosmer, Andrew Warner and Clement Chaplin, as it is supposed that they all went to Hartford about that time, and none of these we
2 CONC re reelected the following November. This exodus to Hartford seemed to disorganize the government, as there were no more meetings of the council until Oct. 3, 1636. At this meeting on June 6, 1636, it was agreed with Mr. Andrews, 
2 CONC that his man keep the calves for 13 shillings a week, "so long as we think good, only we are to provide him a man for the present, if he should require it of us." From this it would seem that Mr. Andrews was contemplating leaving 
2 CONC for Hartford, but expected to retain at least a part of his land and a hired man.
2 CONT
2 CONT Just when William Andrews went from Cambridge to Hartford is not known but probably it was in the summer of 1636, and it may be that he went with the Hooker company in early June.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is said that his wife Mary died at Braintree Jan. 29, 1640 (new style). The records show the death there of Mary, the wife of Mr. William Andrews 19 (11) 1639, that is the 19th day of the 11th month, 1639, which would be Feb. 2
2 CONC 9, 1640, new style. There was another William Andrews whose wife died about that time apparently and in that neighborhood, and who went to Cambridge about 1640, and it is very likely she was the one who died about then, as ancesto
2 CONC r William's wife was alive in May, 1638, and married his second wife Abigail Graves, apparently before 1641. There is also a record of William's wife dying in Cambridge Feb. 29, 1639. This was probably our ancestor. She may have r
2 CONC eturned to her father's home.
2 CONT
2 CONT His second wife was Abigail Graves, a sister of George Graves, who lived within a block of William at Hartford, probably lived with her brother before her marriage. They did not come from Cambridge but probably came direct from He
2 CONC rtford, England.
2 CONT
2 CONT William Andrews' home lot in Hartford, presumably granted to him when he first came to Hartford, was the northeast corner lot on what was then called the road from the mill to the south meadow and the highway on the bank of the ri
2 CONC ver at the crossing of the road from the mill to the country. These roads are now called respectively Elm street and Trinity street. This lot, however, was in what is now the West Park and was on the south bank of the little river
2 CONC , just at the west end of the island. The house was located in the southwest corner of the lot, just at the street corner.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was first town clerk in 1639. In the land division in the same year he drew 30 acres of land. In 1640 he was awarded 10 pounds sterling, for teaching school. At a particular court held March 4, 1651, he was confirmed recorder f
2 CONC or the Town of Hartford.
2 CONT
2 CONT William first appeared in Hartford, according to the records, as one of the proprietors in the division of land Jan. 13, 1640 (new style), when he was awarded 30 acres. Jan. 24, 1640, his name appears among those specified by orde
2 CONC r of the townsmen as "the names of such inhabitants as have rights in undivided lands," and a year later, March 13, 1641, he received 33 acres in the distribution of the east side of the river in Hocazi.m (now East Hartford). This
2 CONC  land was all bought finally from the Indian tribe of Suchlago.
2 CONT
2 CONT William was the schoolmaster of Hartford, also sometimes called the letter writer. Dec. 16, 1642, it was ordered that 30 pounds a year shall be settled upon the school by the town forever, and at a meeting in April, 1643, it was o
2 CONC rdered that Mr. Andrews should teach the children in the school one year next ensuing from the 25th of March, 1643 (0. S.), and that he shall have for his pains 16 pounds, and therefore the townsmen shall go and enquire who will e
2 CONC ngage himself to send their children and all that do, shall pay for one quarter at the least and for more if they do send them after. And Mr. Andrews shall keep account between the children's schooling and himself and send notes a
2 CONC t the time of payment and demand it. And if his wages doth not so come in then the townsmen must collect and pay it. (A pound was worth at that time about $3.00.)
2 CONT
2 CONT This order contains the germ of the free public schools, which bore fruit April 23, 1674, when a free public school was established. Apparently Mr. Andrews continued to teach until shortly before his death in 1659. In March 1649, 
2 CONC he was granted by the town 10 pounds for the year which began Sept., 1648, which paid him until the 25th of December, 1648.
2 CONT
2 CONT Nov. 29, 1650, it was ordered that Mr. Andrews shall attend to keep the school for this present year and to have 10 pounds paid him by the town, beside that which he is to have from the parents of the children. His year began on t
2 CONC he 29th of September, last. Jan. 17, 1656, the town granted him 10 pounds for schooling for the year 1655, and 10 pounds for the year 1656, when it is out. As Wm. Pitkin was made schoolmaster after William Andrews' death, it is pr
2 CONC obable that the latter died "in the harness," as it were.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is probable that the school was kept in the house of Mr. Andrews or in the church or some other building, as on Feb. 11, 1649, the townsmen agreed that 40 pounds shall be paid to the townsmen to the end that a suitable building
2 CONC  be built for such purpose, not to be devoted to any other use or employment. There seems to have been some trouble in regard to it as there was no house built during the lifetime of Mr. Andrews.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Andrews was also town recorder, being appointed at a meeting of the town Jan. 22, 1652, and he acted as such until 1658. He is said to have been an accomplished clerk and scholar, as the records which are yet preserved, in his
2 CONC  exact and beautiful penmanship sufficiently indicate.
2 CONT
2 CONT He died in 1659. His will dated April 1, 1659. Inventory taken Aug. 3, 1659. Amount 211£, 14s, 11d. His second wife was Abigail Graves, aunt of George Graves of Hartford. After his death she married Nathaniel Barding, who lived on
2 CONC  Lord's Hill, and who died about 1674.
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT He may have 1st married Mary Savage born 1600 in England then dyeing in Braintree, Mass., 18th November, 1639  before 2nd marring Abigail Graves.  They had six children: John, Abigail, Elizabeth Grannis, Thomas, Esther Spencer, & 
2 CONC Samuel.  William referred to George Graves as brother. So either, Abigail was George's sister, or William could have been the brother of George's unknown wife. After William's death Abigail married Nathaniel Barding / Berding / Be
2 CONC arding (-1674).[3] She died 26 March 1682/3, the date of her estate's inventory, or perhaps earlier.[4][3] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Immigrant, American patriarch, William Andrews born 8/3/1659, Kingnorth, Kent, England came with Hooker's Company in 1634 during the Puritan Great Migration (1621-1640) [4] and was a founder of the town. Although his place of buri
2 CONC al is not known his name is on the Founder's Stone in the Ancient Cemetery in Hartford, along with Francis Andrews, assumed to be a brother, and Thomas Spencer and William Spencer.  His home was south of Little or Park River in la
2 CONC nd now part of Bushnell Park and he was an early member of First Church in Hartford, CT 
2 CONT
2 CONT "It is certain that William was in Hartford before 1640 when he became schoolmaster, and in fact he had a child born there in 1638. Although many of the Hartford settlers came from Cambridge, I think he was a different man from th
2 CONC e William Andrews of Cambridge whose wife Mary died there 19 Jan. 1639/40. One reason for this conclusion is that William's wife Abigail is the proven mother of William's three sons - proven by William's will which called the chil
2 CONC dren "our" children, as well as by the fact that Abigail's second husband gave them legacies, and finally by a deed of Abigail Barding which calls John, Thomas and Samuel Andrews her sons..." William Andrews "certainly married Abi
2 CONC gail, most probably a sister of George Grave (later called Graves), Sr., of Hartford, but possibly sister of Grave's unknown first wife." [8]
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Last Will & Testement== dated April 1, 1659 in Hartford, CT: -- p.13 "
2 CONT I, William Andrews, being sick and weak, but of perfect memory do in this my last will and Testament, give and bequeath to Abigail, my wife, my house, barn, and home lot, as it is enjoyed between my son, Edward Branniss, and me; a
2 CONC lso I do give and bequeath unto the said Abigail, my wife, all my meadow and swamp lying in the south meadow, and that lyeth in the place called Hockanum, and all my uplands also, during the time of her natural life; also I give t
2 CONC o the said Abigail, my wife, two cows and one yearling calf; also I give unto her 4 yds. of kersey, and 3 doz. buttons, and silk, and all of her movable goods, during the time she liveth unmarried. 
2 CONT
2 CONT If the said Abigail, my wife, shall see cause to alter her condition by marrying another man, then my will s, tht such of the movables as she can conveniently spare, shall be disposed of among the children as she shall advise with
2 CONC  the overseers of this my Will. Also my will is, to give unto my son John one working steer. Also my Will is that Abigail, my wife, shall dispose of my land, Meadow, Swamp, Housing and home lt, among the children, to every one of 
2 CONC them some, as she shall think fit, with the advise and consent of the overseers of this my Will. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I do hereby make Abigail, my wife, sole Executrix of this my last Will and Testament, and do also instruct my loving friend, Edward Stebbins, and my brother, George Grave, to assist my wife, and to see this my Wll executed and per
2 CONC formed. Dated 1st April, 1659, in presence of Edward Stebbins, George Grave, Wit. Signed William Andrews. The inventory was taken 3d August, 1659 by John Barnard, Edward Stebbins, George Grave. Amt., £211 12s 11d At a Particular C
2 CONC ourt held 4th March, 1651, Mr. Andrews was confirmed Recorder of the town of Hartford. 
2 CONT
2 CONT His 1st wife had died at Braintree, Mass., when 2nd he married this Abigail, to whom he gave in his Will as above. After his death (the precise date being unknown) she distributed the estate to the children then surviving, and she
2 CONC  married Nathaniel Bearding, who lived on Lord's Hill, and who died about 1674. The sons were John Andrews, Thomas Andrews, Samuel Andrews, born 20th October, 1645; son-in-law, Thomas Spencer, Jr., husband of their daughter Esther
2 CONC . ... [Note: some genealogists contend that William did not first marry Mary Savage who died in MA -- more research is needed to confirm.]
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Margaret Harrison of Battersea, England, March 1, 1641, was witnessed by Richard Andrews and Thomas Andrews was executor.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Chronology==  
2 CONT * 1595-1600: Born in England[2] 1607 or earlier, based on marriage date.:[4] Jacobus points out that Winthrop calls him "senex" in 1658. This would imply that he was 65, at least, and having children in his 50's, so may have been 
2 CONC born about 1593.[3] An unsourced birthplace of Rotley, Warwickshire, England, has been suggested by Ancestry, Millenium Files and Yates Publishing U.S. and International Marriage Records, 1560-1900. Another suggested but unsourced
2 CONC  birth place is Kingsnorth, Kent, England, but at this time his place of birth & parents are unknown
2 CONT * 1634 Migration first to Cambridge, Massachusetts, then Hartford, Connecticut.[4]
2 CONT * 3/4/1634/5 Freeman[4][5]
2 CONT * 1634, 1635 Granted land in Cambridge.[4]
2 CONT * 11/23/1635 Selectman and Constable in Cambridge, Massachusetts.[4]
2 CONT * 1636 Constable in Newton, MA  
2 CONT * 7/14/1636 - It has been suggested he arrived in Hartford after a two week journey, with Rev. Dr. Thomas Hooker, from Newtown. William is on the founders monument.[2]
2 CONT * 9/25/1637 - Samuel Shepard bought Andrews' house and land.[4]
2 CONT * 1/3/1639/40 - William owned land in Hartford beginning with this first grant in 1639. Eventually he owned eleven parcels: "one acre and one rood 'on which his dwelling house now standeth with other outhouses, yards & gardens'; t
2 CONC wo acres of upland; eighteen acres and two roods of upland (annotated 'four acres of this sold to J: Ensigne"): two roods and twenty-­five perches in the Little Meadow; eight acres in the South Meadow, four acres, three roods and 
2 CONC twenty perches of 'meadow lying in Hockanum"; six acres of swamp in the Great Swamp; six acres and three roods of 'swamp lying in Hockanum'; one acre and one rood of 'Indian Ground lying in the South Meadow which he had of John Cr
2 CONC ow' (annotated 'March 7th 1657,' presumably indicating the date of purchase); '[o]ne parcel of upland (which he had of Mr. Thomas Wells, magistrate) containing 40 acres ... and is in exchange for all his upland on the east side of
2 CONC  the Great River ; and 'one parcel of upland which he received of the town containing thirteen acres' "[4]
2 CONT * 1640 -1656 First School Master in Hartford,[2][3] 
2 CONT * 1651-1658 Town Clerk of Hartford, CT  
2 CONT * 3/5/1644/5 Juryman in Connecticut [CCCR 1:122][4]
2 CONT * 4/8/1645 Earmark of "Wil[ia]m Andrewes' was recorded at Hartford[7][6]
2 CONT * 12 Jan 1651/2 Chosen town recorder[4][6]
2 CONT * 1659 Died in Hartford, Hartford County, Connecticut, USA
2 CONT * 4/1/1659 Will dated. [8]
2 CONT * 8/3/1659 Inventory taken  £211. 14s. 11d.[2][4][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Spouses'''
2 CONT Mary Abigail Savage Andrews 1602–1639 in Braintree, MA m. 1619
2 CONT Abigail Andrews Barding 1593–1682 m. 1632
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Children''' likely all 7x b in Hartford, CT
2 CONT John Andrews  ca 1632 - 6/6/1690 m. 1st Lilly, 2nd Mary NN in Hartford, CT
2 CONT Abigail Andrews ca 1634 - 5/1653 in Fairfield, CT
2 CONT Elizabeth Andrews Grannis 1632/6–1656 m Edward Grannis of Hartford, CT 5/3/1654/5
2 CONT Thomas Andrews 5/4/1638–1690/1 m Hannah Kirby, dau. of John
2 CONT Esther Andrews Spencer 9/1641–3/6/1698 in Suffield, Connecticut. m Thomas Spencer of Suffield by 1655.
2 CONT Samuel Andrews 10/20/1645–1/1712 m Elizabeth Spencer daughter of Sergeant Thomas Spencer;
2 CONT William Andrews, probably died young with no descendants [11 ,23] 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Descendant Details==
2 CONT Elizabeth Andrews was married to Edward Grannis, May 3, 1654. She was probably born in 1636 or 1637, and perhaps some years earlier. She was no doubt a daughter of William Andrews by his first wife Mary. When she married Grannis s
2 CONC he was living at Farmington, apparently permanently, as she was called Elizabeth Andrews of Farmington, daughter of William. There was no other Andrews at Farmington then but the members of John's family, and so far as I know, Eli
2 CONC zabeth had no other relatives or intimate friends there. After her marriage she went to Hartford, and she and her husband lived with the family of William Andrews. At that time John of Farmington had five children, the oldest but 
2 CONC 8 years old, and they were coming every two years. John's wife must have been badly in need of some assistance, especially as they were apparently attending church in Hartford, and they couldn't take all the babies there. Therefor
2 CONC e I believe it to be fair to assume that Elizabeth was living there with her brother John's family. Also three years before Elizabeth was married John's son Joseph was born, and he was the baby of the family until about a month be
2 CONC fore Elizabeth was married, when Rachel was Elizabeth was married May 3, 1654, and on March 31, 1656, she had a son born and she named him Joseph.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Notes==
2 CONT Christian Andrews who married John Birchard, son of Thomas Birchard, was not a daughter of this William (as claimed in Families of Early Hartford by Barbour), but was daughter of widow Catherine Andrews per Biographical Timeline
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughter Elizabeth Andrews, b. say 1636; married 3 May 1655, Hartford, Edward Grannis.[9][3][4]. Her marriage record calls her of Farmington, daughter of William of Hartford. Edward Grannis later moved to New Haven and married 166
2 CONC 2 Hannah Wakefield. He died 10 Dec 1719.[3]  Child: Joseph Grannis b. Hartford 31 Mar 1656, must have died young. 
2 CONT
2 CONT D.L. Jacobus in an American Genealogist Article entitled "Andrews Families of Western Connecticut," explains that Savage's Genealogical Dictionary has combined two William Andrews of Cambridge. One of them who died in Cambridge ha
2 CONC d a wife named Mary who died in 1639 and a second wife Reana, the other William had a wife Abigail and removed to Hartford, Connecticut where he died. The William of Hartford died 1659.  William who stayed in Cambridge died about 
2 CONC 1652. William who died in Cambridge was a mariner and shop owner while William who died in Hartford was a school master and Clerk.  D.L. Jacobus points out in his 1959 article on "William Andrews of Hartford" that this is instead 
2 CONC two distinct men, the William Andrews who married Mary, who died at Cambridge, Jun. 19, 1639-40, is not the same as the Hartford Original Proprietor.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the court records of Hartford county there is no record with reference to any of William Andrews' sons, except the statement that John of Farmington was made Freeman May 18, 1658. Apparently the records are not complete in refe
2 CONC rence to Freemen. Thomas and Samuel Andrews were both made Freemen but the court records do not show it. However, the list of all Freemen in 1669 are preserved, and they are among the number.
2 CONT
2 CONT Per Jacobus the William Andrews who married Abigail was probably an original settler of Hartford, Connecticut. His early lands were probably in the 1639/40 era since those of Francis Andrews were of this date, and William's lands 
2 CONC were entered on the preceeding page.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Andrews of Hartford and Fairfield and John Andrews of Farmington may have been younger half-brothers of William Andrews [TAG 35:57, 59] being the possible husband of Mary Savage ['''SIC: unproven'''] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Williams wife, Abigail Graves 2nd  married Nathaniel Barding, & died 26 Mar 1682/3 (unless this is the date the inventory was taken, in which case she died somewhat earlier).
2 CONT
2 CONT Savage and Frank Andrews[2] both claim that he had a first wife Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT When William died his wife was Abigail. In his will he refers to "our sons" John, Thomas and Samuel. Abigail's second husband also gave them legacies, and Abigail in a deed calls them her sons. Thomas, son of William, by Hartford 
2 CONC town record, was born 4 May 1638, but Mary the wife of William of Cambridge didn't die until 19 Jan 1640 in Cambridge.[3] 
2 CONT
