2 CONT
2 CONT Often mistaken relationships
2 CONT
2 CONT   Elizabeth Andrews, b. say 1636; married 3 May 1655, Hartford, Edward Grannis.[9][3][4]. Her marriage record calls her of Farmington, daughter of William of Hartford. Edward Grannis later moved to New Haven and married 1662 Hanna
2 CONC h Wakefield. He died 10 Dec 1719.[3]
2 CONT         Child: Joseph Grannis b. Hartford 31 Mar 1656, must have died young.[3] 
2 CONT     Thomas, born 4 May 1638, in Hartford[9]; settled in Middletown; died 1690/91; married Hannah Kirby.[3][4]
2 CONT     Esther Andrews, born Sept 1641 in Hartford[9]; married Thomas Spencer Jr. by 1655; died 6 March 1697/8, in Suffield, Connecticut.[3][4]
2 CONT     Samuel, was born 20 Oct 1645, at Hartford[9]; died between 1 Jan 1711/2 and 29 Jan 1711/2; married 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Andrews of England had sons John, Samuel, Nathaniel, Elinor and Alice in New England.
2 CONT According to Dr. Jack T. Spencer (R-1), "Abigail Graves had a most interesting family history in Hartford, CT. She was first married to a William Andrews who died at Hartford in 1659."
2 CONT
2 CONT "After the death of her first husband, Abigail Graves Andrews married Nathaniel Barding (Bearding, Berden) of Hartford. A daughter, Sarah Barding (Berden), was born to this second marriage. Sarah married Thomas1 Spencer, one of th
2 CONC e founding fathers of Hartford. A son of Thomas1 Spencer, Thomas2 Spencer, married Esther Andrews. In short, we have a case where Thomas2 Spencer married the daughter of his grandmother from an earlier marriage (to William Andrews
2 CONC )."
2 CONT
2 CONT The inventory of the estate of William Andrews was taken by Edward Stebbing, George Grave, and John Barnard. The will, dated 1 April 1659, was witnessed by Edward Stebbing and George Grave, and stated, "I doe make Abigail, my wife
2 CONC , Executrix, and I doe Intreat my friend Edward Stebbinge and my Brother George Grave to assist and to see this my Will performed."
2 CONT
2 CONT The inventory of the estate of Nathaniel Berding was taken by Paul Peck, George Grave, and Caleb Stanly. The will, dated 7 Jan. 1673, was witnessed by George Grave and Thomas Olcott.
2 CONT
2 CONT There is obviously a close relationship between George Grave(s) and Abigail Graves, almost certainly Abigail is either his sister or his daughter. Since George was born about 1600 and Abigail may not have been born until as late a
2 CONC s 1620 (based on the date of her first marriage), she could be his daughter. However, based on the fact that Abigail is not mentioned in the will or estate settlement of George Graves and that the will of William Andrews calls him
2 CONC  "brother" (probably meaning brother-in-law), it is almost certain that Abigail and George were sister and brother.
