1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Not the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Mary-Haynes/6000000002339904576?through=6000000003275183064
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=hvd.32044058114075 page 479
2 CONT
2 CONT "She was, we are informed, an elegant and accomplished woman, and eminently fitted, both by birth and education, to fill with dignity and grace the exalted station which she attained by marriage." - Glover Memorials and Genealogie
2 CONC s
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT From Glover, Anna. ''Glover memorials and genealogies : an account of John Glover of Dorchester, and his descendants, with a brief sketch of some of the Glovers who first settled in New Jersey, Virginia, and other places''.  (1867
2 CONC ). Page 479.  < [https://archive.org/details/glovermemorialsg00glov/page/n498/mode/1up?q=Haynes+ Archive.Org] > 
2 CONT
2 CONT (5) MARY GLOVER, the second and youngest daughter of Rev. Pelatiah and Hannah (Cullick) Glover, was born in Springfield, April 17, 1672, and died in Hartford, Conn., Aug. 19, 1727, in her 55th year. She and her elder brother Pelat
2 CONC iah were the only children who survived the venerable Springfield pastor. She was provided for in her father's will. (See pp. 465, 466.)
2 CONT
2 CONT She was married, Nov. 7, 1693, to John Haynes, Esq., of Hartford, Ct., and went there to reside. She was, we are informed, an elegant and accomplished woman, and eminently fitted, both by birth and education, to fill with dignity 
2 CONC and grace the exalted station which she attained by marriage. Although she had four children, she has left few descendants, and those of other names.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of John, Esq., and Mary (Glover) Haynes, born in Hartford, Conn. :
2 CONT *14. Joseph, b. Sept. 14, 1695 ; graduated at Yale College in the class of 1714, and intended for the law; died unmarried, in Hartford, Sept. 14, 1716, aged 21 years.
2 CONT *15. Sarah, b. in 1697 ; d. Nov. 9, 1724, in her 27th year.
2 CONT *16. Jared, b. in 1699 ; died young. +17. 
2 CONT * 17. Mary Glover, b. in 1703;  m. 1st,  Elisha Lord, of Hartford; 2nd, Capt. Rosewell Saltonstall; 3d, Thomas Clap, D.D., of New Haven.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Haynes, Esq.,* the husband of Mrs. f Mary Glover, was born in Hartford, Conn., in 1669, and died there, Nov, 27, 1713, aged 44 years, leaving a widow (and, it is said, a son and a daughter, viz., Joseph and Mary). He was the 
2 CONC eldest son of Rev. Joseph and Sarah (Lord) Hayncs. His father was the third pastor of the first Church in Hartford, and a graduate of Harvard College in 1658; died May 24,1679, in Hartford. His mother was Sarah Lord, daughter of R
2 CONC ichard t and Sarah Lord, and granddaughter of Thomas and Dorothy Lord, who were among the early settlers of the Connecticut Colony. John Haynes, their son, was sent to Cambridge to be educated, and graduated there in the class of 
2 CONC 1689. He was intended for the ministry, and studied divinity with his father at Hartford. After the decease of the Rev. Pelatiah Glover at Springfield, he was called to labor with that Church, and subsequently to fill the vacancy 
2 CONC caused by the death of their former pastor. He preached there a short time with eminent success, but declined to settle among them as their minister. He returned to Hartford, and commenced the study of law. He is said to have main
2 CONC tained an honorable position in the colony, as a Lawyer, Judge Advocate, Judge of the Superior Court, and was an assistant and council for the Governor from 1708 until he died, a period of five years. His line of ancestry was high
2 CONC ly distinguished — being the son of a minister of Wethersfield and Hartford, and grandson of the first Governor of the Colony of Connecticut, who in 1634 was Governor of Massachusetts.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * Gov. Winthrop states, in Ms journal, that Mr. John Haynes, a gentleman of great estate, came to New England in the Griffin, a ship of 200 tons burthen, commanded by Capt. Gallop.
2 CONT * " May 14, 163 i. The Court chose a New Governor, viz. Thomas Dudley, Esq., Mr. Ludlow Deputy, and Mr. John Haynes Assistant. The 3 mo. 6 day, 1634, a General Court was held at Newtown (Cambridge), when Mr. John Hayncs was chosen
2 CONC  Governor ; R. Bellingham, Deputy Governor ; Mr. Hough and Mr. Dummer, Assistants." His residence was at Cambridge.
2 CONT * " 3'' : 2 : 1635. Mr. Haynes, one of our Magistrates, removed with his family to Connecticut." The first year after his removal there, it is stated, he became father of the new Colony of Connecticut.
2 CONT * t She was so styled before marriage, on all records, in that day of titles and ranks.
2 CONT * + Richard Lord was born in England, about 1611, and probably married there about 1635.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT Pelatiah Glover was a prominent New England preacher based in Springfield, Massachusetts. A 15 page biographical sketch is provided by an 1865 Glover family genealogical history.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Pelatiah Glover married Hannah (Cullick) Glover (abt.1640-abt.1689) in 1660 in Boston, MA.[7][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT Hannah and Pelatiah had at least 5 children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # Samuell, b. 28 Nov 1661[9]
2 CONT # John, b. 1 Jul 1663[10]; d. 14 Jan 1664[11]
2 CONT # Pelatiah, b. 27 Jan 1665[12]
2 CONT # Anna, b. 21 Aug 1668[13] and d. 6 Jun 1690.[14]
2 CONT # '''Mary''', b. April 1671[15] or 20 Aug 1671[16] or 17 Apr 1672[17]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
