1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==Wikipedia Biographical Summary:==
2 CONT
2 CONT '''James Pierpont''' (born January 4, 1659, Roxbury, Massachusetts; died November 22, 1714, New Haven, Connecticut) was a Congregationalist minister who is credited with the founding of Yale University in the United States. In 170
2 CONC 1, Pierpont, a graduate of The Roxbury Latin School and Harvard University, secured the charter for The Collegiate School of Connecticut, which soon thereafter took the surname of its benefactor Elihu Yale.
2 CONT
2 CONT James Pierpont married in 1698 as his third wife Mary Hooker, daughter of Rev. Samuel Hooker and granddaughter of Rev. Thomas Hooker, chief founder of the Colony of Connecticut. Their daughter, Sarah Pierpont, married noted coloni
2 CONC al minister Jonathan Edwards. James Pierpont's descendants also include U.S. Vice President Aaron Burr and financier John Pierpont (J.P.) Morgan.
2 CONT
2 CONT >''SOURCE'': http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Pierpont_%28Yale_founder%29
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT was for 30 years pastor of the First Church in New Haven Ct and one of the founders of yale college
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.ancestry.com/family-tree/person/tree/28702968/person/26011681165 Ancestry Genealogy] - [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Apr 29 2017, 9:38:44 UTC''
2 CONT **Immigrant abt 1640, see Browning p. 613-615 for spelling in same family of Pierpont & Pierrepont; Harvard Univ grad, pastor of First Church of Christ Congrational in New Haven, CT., taught moral philosophy at Yale.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
