2 CONT ===Note===See [http://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Space:New_England_Locations_and_Dates ''New England Colonial Names and Dates of Towns and Counties'']for more about places in New England and what they were called before 1776. In the ma
2 CONC rriage data for Elizur and Mary Pynchon, the location is Springfield, New England Colony because Springfield had withdrawn from Connecticut Colony and not yet joined Massachusetts Bay Colony; allthe colonies were under a loose fed
2 CONC eration since September 6, 1628, known as New England Colony.
2 CONT
2 CONT == Biography ==
2 CONT {{Roll of Honor | image = Roll of Honor Template-15.jpg  | category = Killed in Action, King Philip's War | description = KIA | war = King Philip's War }} Elizur Holyoke is the son of [[Holyoke-6|Edward Holyoke]] and [[Stockton-50
2 CONC |Prudence Stockton]]. <ref name="Hale">''Hale, House and Related Families: Mainly of the Connecticut River Valley'', database, [https://books.familysearch.org/primo_library/libweb/action/search.do?vid=FHD_PUBLIC FamilySearch.org]:
2 CONC  (accessed 13 Nov 2013), entry for Edward Holyoke, extracted from Donald Lines Jacobus and Edgar Francis Waterman, book of the same name (Baltimore, Massachusetts: Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc., 1978), page 641.</ref> He was b
2 CONC aptized May 4, 1617, at St. Lawrence Jewry,<ref name=Richardson>Vital Records from The NEHGS Register. Online database. AmericanAncestors.org. New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2014. (Compiled from articles originally pub
2 CONC lished in The New England Historical and Genealogical Register.)''"The English Ancestry of Edward Holyoke and of His Nephew, Thomas Morris of NewHaven"'' Douglas Richardson. Vol. 147, p. 21.</ref>an ancient church on Gresham Stree
2 CONC t in London. (That church burned in the Great London Fire in 1666.) <ref>St. Lawrence Jewry, database, [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/St_Lawrence_Jewry Wikipedia.org],  Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike License. </ref> Elizur
2 CONC 's parents, [[Stockton-151|Prudence Stockton]] and Edward Holyoke, were both christened in Alcester, Warwickshire, England. Edward andPrudence emigrated to America with their family about 1637/38, when Elizur was about twenty. Ban
2 CONC ks, in his ''Topographical dictionary of 2885 English emigrants'', says Edward was from Tamworth in Staffordshire<ref>''Topographical dictionary of 2885 English emigrants to New England, 1620-1650'', database, [http://babel.hathit
2 CONC rust.org/cgi/pt?id=mdp.39015008455175;view=1up;seq=196 HathiTrust.org] (accessed 13 Nov 2013) Entry for Holyoke, Edward; extracted from Charles Edward Banks, bookof the same name (Philadelphia, Pennsylvania: The Bertram Press, 193
2 CONC 7), page 148.</ref> (it is about forty miles north of Alcester). The parents settled on a farm in Chelsea, Massachusetts, near Boston. Elizur married Mary Pynchon September 20, 1640 at her hometown of Springfield, Massachusetts.<r
2 CONC ef name="Hale"/> It was about one hundred miles from Chelsea to Springfield by land, but Elizur may have gone by ship, around Cape Cod and up the Connecticut River, since rivers were a preferred way to travel in those times. The P
2 CONC ynchons and the Holyokes were close friends, dating back to England. Elizur's father-in-law, William Pynchon, was the greatest land-owner in Springfield, and that iswhere Elizur settled with his bride. Elizur and Mary had eight ch
2 CONC ildren, six of whom lived to become adults. Mary died in 1657, when their youngest was only a baby. Some think that because there is no record of the date Elizur married widow EdithaStebbins Day Maynard, that he waited years to re
2 CONC marry. The writers who believe they married in late 1657 or in 1658 are on the side of practical considerations. (Jacobus is in the latter group, although his book says Elizur married Editha in 1685, Elizur died in 1675 and it is 
2 CONC clearly a typo, meaning 1658.) Editha's family were grown, Elizur had ababy and young children in need of a mother - what would they have been waiting for? <ref name="Hale"/> Elizur administered his father's will June 25, 1660. Ed
2 CONC ward left the bulk of his estate to Elizur. Besides the farm at Lynn, with 500 acres,valued at 400 pounds, and the farm near Reading valued at 150 pounds,Elizur inherited his father's books and manuscripts, which included aBible i
2 CONC n Latin. <ref name="Hale"/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1675,  Elizur Holyoke died at Springfield, Hampshire County, Massachusetts, on February 6, 1676,<ref name=Richardson/> age about fifty-nine years. He had crossed the Atlantic Ocean, helped found the town of Spring
2 CONC field, fathered eight children, and performed a lifetime of faithful service to his community as Deputy to the Court, Selectman, Town Clerk and Magistrate. <ref>''The First Century of the History of Springfield: The Official Recor
2 CONC ds from 1636 to 1736'', database, [https://books.familysearch.org/primo_library/libweb/action/search.do?vid=FHD_PUBLIC FamilySearch.org], (accessed 13 Nov 2013), extracted from Henry M. Burt, book of the same name, (Springfield, M
2 CONC assachusetts, self-published, 1902), Vol 2, page 590 - 592. </ref>
2 CONT
2 CONT :Spouse: Mary Pynchon Holyoke (1623 - 1657) :Children:<ref name="Hale"/> :: John, b 27 Aug 1641; d 8 Oct 1641 :: John b 5 Aug 1642; d 6 Feb 1711/12 grad. Harvard 1662 :: Hannah b 9 Jun 1644; 2 Feb 1677/8; m 7 Nov 1661 Capt Samuel 
2 CONC Talcott :: a child b and d 21 May 1646:: Samuel Holyoke b 9 Jun 1647; d 31 Oct 1676; a hero of the "Falls Fight" :: Edward b 8 Aug 1649; d 16 Jun 1708:: Elizur b 13 Oct 1651; d 11 Aug 1711; m 2 Jan 1677/8 Mary Eliot (grand-niece o
2 CONC f Rev. John Eliot of Roxbury) :: Mary b 14 Oct 1656; Note: Hampshire County was formed in 1662, and the default is to list the location as it was named at the event. Springfield is currently inHampden County, (formed 1812) as of t
2 CONC his posting, 5 Dec 2013. ''No more info is currently available for Elizur Holyoke. Can you add to his biography?''Narrative by [[Dellinger-332|April Dauenhauer]] (rough draft) - 5 Dec 2013
2 CONT
