1 NOTE {geni:about_me} == William Davis of Boston the **Apothecary** ==
2 CONT * Arrived Boston 1643 and joined the church 1644
2 CONT * Born ***1617*** based on depositions and age at death
2 CONT * Was 26 when he joined the artillery Company in 1643
2 CONT * Married Margaret Pynchon 6 Dec 1644 Boston ("married in the Bay 10 mon 6 day 1644). She died 3 d 5 mo 1653 Boston. 
2 CONT * Married 2nd Huldah Symmes before 1654 who died shortly after 1674
2 CONT * Married 3rd Sarah Farmer before he wrote his will on 17 May 1676. She was his widow and married to Edward Palmes of New London. 
2 CONT * Died 24 May 1676
2 CONT * Will dated 17 May 1676
2 CONT * His will named his ****mother Elizabeth Davis of London*** but his father is not known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT William Davis of Boston the **Apothecary** was the third William Davis to be recorded in Boston and the last to die. He was also called Captain Davis and Lt Davis. On the Internet he is often conflated with William Davis **Locksmi
2 CONC th** of Boston and his son William the Gunsmith but genealogists sorted these three men out in 1878. All three are discussed in:
2 CONT
2 CONT Samuel Forbes Rockwell, Davis Families of Early Roxbury and Boston (Andover 1932) 177-240 
2 CONT
2 CONT See specifically page 203 https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=uva.x000485671&view=2up&seq=234&skin=mobile
2 CONT
2 CONT Even Robert Charles Anderson states that Rockwell did a very good job sorting out these three men. Savage made a mess of them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT A list of his children is given on page 245: https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=uva.x000485671&view=image&seq=245&skin=mobile
2 CONT
2 CONT See also the discussion on page 318 of William Davis, in Anderson, Robert Charles; George F. Sanborn; and Melinde Lutz Sanborn. The Great Migration: Immigrants to New England, 1634-1635. (NEHGS, 1999-2011), v2, p 316-8. 2:2:316-20
2 CONC  https://www.ancestry.com/interactive/2496/42521_b158313-00422?pid=20228#?imageId=42521_b158313-00418
2 CONT
2 CONT Please do not conflate with William Davis Locksmith of Boston and his son William the Gunsmith: https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/G9PX-FPP
2 CONT
2 CONT William Davis, apothecary, wrote his will on May 17, 1676, and it was proved on May 26, 1676. He left bequests to the following 
2 CONT
2 CONT * wife Sarah
2 CONT * sons Thomas, Benjamin, William and John Davis
2 CONT * daughters Elizabeth, Maria, Rebecca, Huldah, Ruth, Margarita and Hannah Davis
2 CONT * any child of wife Sarah not yet born
2 CONT * mother Sarah Symmes for her counsel in the care of my children
2 CONT * my mother Elizabeth Davis of London
2 CONT * brother Brock & sister (sister-in-law Sarah Symmes married Henry Brock) 
2 CONT * brother Savage & his wife  (sister-in-law Mary Symmes married Thomas Savage) 
2 CONT * brother Willis & his wife  (sister-in-law Ruth Symmes married Edward Willis) 
2 CONT * brother Prowtt & his wife  (sister-in-law Deborah Symmes married Timothy Prout )
2 CONT * brother Usher & his wife  (sister-in-law Elizabeth Symmes married Hezekiah Usher)
2 CONT * brother William Symmes  (brother-in-law)
2 CONT * mother Farmer & sister Wymon 
2 CONT * the poor of Boston
2 CONT * South Church 'where I am a member'
2 CONT * Pastor Reverand Thatcher and Edward Rainford
2 CONT * son Benjamin to be sole executor
2 CONT * overseers - my brother Major Jno. Pynchon and friend Captain Thomas Lake
2 CONT * He further states that the portion of the estate left by 'my father Zachariah Symmes' should be equally divided among the children of my wife Huldah, deceased, after the death of 'my mother Sarah Symmes'. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * After his death, his widow Sarah (Farmer) Davis married Edward Palmes as his second wife, in September, 1677. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * On June 11, 1708, the following heirs of Captain William Davis, submitted a letter to the court regarding administration of their brother Major Benjamin Davis', apothecary of Boston, estate, contesting that the administrators ha
2 CONC ve found many debts of their father in England and have therefore stated that there the heirs have been paid and there is no further money from their father's estate. 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Elizabeth Whetcombe
2 CONT * Mary Frost
2 CONT * Rebecca Lawrence
2 CONT * Huldah Rainsford
2 CONT * Ruth Royse
2 CONT * Hannah Davis
2 CONT * Margaret Davis 
2 CONT
2 CONT == Children by Margaret ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Thomas bp 3 SEP 1645 
2 CONT * 2 Anna bp 5 SEP 1647
2 CONT * 3 Benjamin bp 19 AUG 1649; died on November 26, 1704 ; in December, 1704, his widow Mary refused administration of his estate. ; married first Sarah Richards by 1680; married second Mary Tippet on January 15, 1696/7. 
2 CONT * 4 Ephraim bp . 8 MAY 165; son of William and Margaret, died at Boston on August 2, 1652 [2: 6: 1652]. 
2 CONT * 4 William born at Boston on June 25. 1653. 
2 CONT
2 CONT == Children by Huldah ==
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1 Sarah baptized on September 3, 1654. 
2 CONT * 2 Elizabeth baptized on [26: 6: 1655] August 26, 1655.; died at Boston on August 18, 1712 , aged 57   ; married James Whitcomb about 1680 
2 CONT * 3 Mary born on December 3, 1656  ; married Captain John Frost about 1680, as his second wife. Her will was proved in 1732. 
2 CONT * 4 Rebecca born on August 3, 1658; married Joseph Lawrence on March 13, 1670/1. 
2 CONT * 5 Huldah born on December 21, 1659 ; buried at Boston on August 2, 1721; married Edward Raynsford. 
2 CONT * 6 Ruth bp  17 FEB 1660 died young
2 CONT * 7 Ruth born on February 12, 1662  ; married (?Daniel) Roys/Royce. (note: Daniel Royse and Huldah Rainsford were among the witnesses to the will of James Whitcomb, Elizabeth (Davis) Whitcomb's husband. 
2 CONT * 8 John born on June 10, 1663. 
2 CONT * 9 Deborah born on April 13, 1665 ; likely died young, not mentioned in her father's will.
2 CONT * 10 Joseph born on September 3, 1669 ; likely died young, not mentioned in his father's will. 
2 CONT * 11 Margaret baptized on [24:12: 1666] February 24, 1667  ; unmarried in 1708.
2 CONT * 12 Hannah born on August 19, 1674 ; unmarried in 1708. 
2 CONT * 13 Jane born on August 19, 1674 ; likely died young, not mentioned in her father's will. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT * Selectman in Boston 1654
2 CONT * A Founder of Old South Church 1669
2 CONT * Captain in Artillery Company 1674
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biographical notes===
2 CONT
2 CONT From [https://books.google.com/books?id=k_4KAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA124&lpg=PA124&dq=margaret+pynchon+william+davis&source=bl&ots=aaiMGGcWDh&sig=ZFKBg0pqvzOdm1DgSzKuFD-7qCI&hl=en&sa=X&ved=0ahUKEwilubGntbPSAhVqslQKHXCVC4AQ6AEIPDAH#v=onepage&
2 CONC q&f=false page 124] of Publications of the Colonial Society of Massachusetts, Volume 6.  "NOTE ON BENJAMIN DAVIS, THE LOYALIST."
2 CONT >''Captain William Davis, of Boston'', apothecary, was of the Ancient and Honorable Artillery Company, 1643; was admitted to the First Church, 28 July, 1644; and, in 1669, was one of the principal Founders of the Old South Church,
2 CONC  his name standing on the Records at the head of the List. He was a Representative for Springfield, 1652, 1666, 1671 and 1672, and for Haverhill, 1668.l He was a wealthy and enterprising citizen, a man of discretion, many years on
2 CONC e of the Selectmen of Boston at different times between 1647 and 1674, and joint Commissioner (1653) with Governor Leverett to the Dutch at New York. Thrice married, his first wife was Margaret, daughter of William Pynchon of Spri
2 CONC ngfield, his second, Huldah, daughter of the Reverend Zechariah Symmes, and his last, Sarah, daughter of John Farmer.
2 CONT >Captain William Davis lived in State Street, on the north-easterly corner of Exchange Street (Boston Record Commissioners' Reports, ii. (Third edition) Part 2, 22; and see ante, v. 289) until 1645, when he sold his estate (Suffol
2 CONC k Deeds, i. 63) and bought of Valentine Hill the lot in Washington Street at the southerly corner of Court Avenue (Ibid. i. 60). This estate had a frontage of twenty feet on the street (this portion of it being now the site of Tho
2 CONC mpson's Spa) and, including subsequent additions, extended back, on irregular lines, to Court Square, about 350 feet. In 1736 William Price bought it, and in 1770 bequeathed it to King's Chapel. The most valuable part of the estat
2 CONC e now constitutes The Price Fund (Suffolk Deeds, xxvi, 169; and Foote's Annals of King's Chapel, ii. 421 and notes).
2 CONT
2 CONT >He died 24 May, 1676 (Sewall's Diary, i. 13). His will, executed a week before his death, mentions " my mother Mrs. Elizabeth Davis in London " and contains valuable particulars (Suffolk Probate Files, No. 780).
2 CONT
2 CONT -----
