1 TITL Pvt
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} DAR Ancestor #: A115332
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Tower BIRTH	12 Jan 1728 Hingham, Plymouth County, Massachusetts, USA DEATH	4 Aug 1814 (aged 86) Cummington, Hampshire County, Massachusetts, USA BURIAL	 Bryant Cemetery Cummington, Hampshire County, Massachusetts, USA Add to
2 CONC  Map MEMORIAL ID	55579323 · View Source SHARE  SAVE TOSUGGEST EDITS
2 CONT
2 CONT Pictures added by CathyJ1952 & Michael Stottle
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Tower performed military service during the Revolution despite being more than 45 years old. He was in Capt. Pyam Cushing's company, guarding the shores while the British ships were lying at the outer harbor of Boston subseq
2 CONC uent to its evacuation in March 1776. He served in 2 other companies doing guard duty. Near the end of the Revolution, Peter and several other family members moved from Hingham to Cummington. (Excerpt from Tower Genealogy by Charl
2 CONC emagne Tower).
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouses Deborah Stowell Tower, 1726–1780 (m. 1746)	 Mary Shaw Tower, 1737–1808
2 CONT
2 CONT Children	 Leah Tower Tower, 1747–1847	 Stephen Tower, 1755–1826	 Asa Tower, 1762–1843	 Lucy Bailey, 1764–1831	 Deborah Tower Pratt, 1767–1853 Patience Tower, 1770–1806
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/55579323/peter-tower
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Tower, though more that forty-five years old when the War of Revolution began, performed military service.   He was in Capt. Pyam Cushing's company, employed in guarding the shores while the British ships were lying at the o
2 CONC uter harbor of Boston subsequent to its evacuation, March 1776, and before they sailed for Halifax. Again he served with Capt. Cushing's company from Dec 18 1776 to Apr 1 1777, when he is credited with three and one-half months' s
2 CONC ervice. In 1778 he was in Capt. Elias Whiton's company doing guard duty in Boaton.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Tower removed from Hingham to Cummington, Mass near the close of the war.  At this time, or soon after his several children went there also, and shared in the work belonging to the early settlement of that town.  His oldest 
2 CONC child and daughter, Leah, who had married Nathaniel Tower, was among the number.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Although he was 45 years old when the Revolutionary War broke out Peter Tower, the son of Peter Tower Sr. and Patience Garnet, served in the Hingham militia in 1776, 1777, and 1778. He was employed as a "guardian of the shore" in 
2 CONC 1776 while the British fleet lingered in Boston Harbor prior to sailing for Halifax. Before the War ended Peter and his wife Deborah Stowell moved their family to Cummington, MA. Land was scarce in Hingham and, as Peter's daughter
2 CONC  Leah put it, "We were very poor and (Nathaniel's) wages could not support our family; and we were advised to remove to the western part of the state. " The proprietor of Cummington, Col. John Cummings lived in Concord and never s
2 CONC et foot in the town. Typically he sold land to the settlers in 100 acre parcels. Many families from the south shore of Massachusetts, including Hingham, took advantage of this opportunity to start anew.
