1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Henry Austin Palmer'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Banker, Founder of Claremont, California; principal of the Pacific Land Improvement Company and a founder & the first president of the Pomona College Board of Trustees ''
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/142113969/henry-austin-palmer Find A Grave Memorial ID # 142113969]
2 CONT * [https://claremont-courier.com/opinion/t15810-34894/ It Happened on College Avenue - Claremont Courier 06/12/2015]
2 CONT
2 CONT Palmer was born on December 23, 1842, a native of Stonington, New London County, Connecticut; worked there as an express agent, then a clerk in the U.S. custom house; came to California in 1862 and was employed in a banking and as
2 CONC say office in Folsom until 1864, and then spent a year in assaying in Michigan Bluff; worked as cashier in U.S. Mint at San Francisco, 1865-67; cashier at banking house of Banks & Company until 1869 when he organized and was appoi
2 CONC nted cashier of the Union Savings Bank of Oakland; became the first cashier of the Union National bank, established in 1877, serving as president from 1882-86, while also serving as vice president and treasurer of the Union Saving
2 CONC s Bank; in 1867, he married Jane O. Day; their children were Theodore S., Elizabeth Day, and Harold King Palmer; in 1882, along with C.T. Mills and M.L. Wicks, Palmer incorporated the Pomona Land and Water Company; in 1884 served 
2 CONC as vice president, and then president until 1887; also became the owner and incorporator of the Pomona Bank in 1883.
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://oac.cdlib.org/findaid/ark:/13030/kt7f59p0pz/ Palmer Family Papers] 
2 CONT * [https://libguides.library.cpp.edu/c.php?g=771946&p=7979534 CalPolyPomona Pomona Valley Historical Special Collection] 
2 CONT * [https://claremont.as.atlas-sys.com/repositories/6/resources/1092 Palmer Canyon Deeds Collection]
2 CONT
2 CONT HISTORY OF THE TOWN OF STONINGTON, County of New London, Connecticut, from its first settlement in 1649 to 1900, by Richard Anson Wheeler, New London, CT, 1900, p. 523
2 CONT ----
