1 NAME Alexander Samuel /Salley/
2 GIVN Alexander Samuel
2 SURN Salley
2 _MARNM Salley
2 NICK Alex s Salley
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 16 JUN 1871
2 PLAC Orangeburg, Orangeburg, South Carolina, United States
2 ADDR
1 BURI
2 DATE AFT 19 FEB 1961
2 PLAC Sunnyside Cemetery, Orangeburg, Orangeburg, South Carolina, United States
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 19 FEB 1961
2 PLAC Columbia, Richland, South Carolina, United States
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/81720605/alexander-samuel-salley
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.scencyclopedia.org/sce/entries/salley-alexander-samuel/
2 CONT
2 CONT Historian. Alexander Samuel Salley, son of Alexander McQueen Salley and Sallie McMichael, was born on June 16, 1871, on his father’s plantation in Orangeburg County. He attended primary schools in Orangeburg and, from 1881 to 1887
2 CONC , Sheridan’s Classical School in the same city. He entered the Citadel in Charleston on October 1, 1887, and graduated from that institution on July 8, 1892. Late in 1892 Salley undertook the study of law, gaining admission to the
2 CONC  South Carolina Bar in 1899. While studying law, he worked for the editorial department of the Charleston News and Courier. Fascinated by history, he also investigated local records and other primary sources, preparing his first m
2 CONC onograph based on research in 1897. From that time forward he wrote continuously on South Carolina historical topics, producing countless articles and over one hundred monographs.
2 CONT
2 CONT On October 1, 1899, Salley assumed the position of secretary, treasurer, and librarian at the South Carolina Historical Society in Charleston, a private organization concerned with the preservation of the state’s past. Simultaneou
2 CONC sly, the discovery in the State House of Revolutionary War records previously believed to be lost spurred Salley to advocate their preservation. His Sunday News and Courier articles included all sorts of historical matters, as wel
2 CONC l as plans for the custody and care of the newly found State House records. A South Carolina Historical Society–sponsored bill six years later passed the General Assembly, creating the funding and a full-time secretary for the pre
2 CONC viously nonfunded, functionless State Historical Commission and legislatively making the agency responsible for all state public records. Salley began as that secretary on April 1, 1905. For the next forty-five years Salley organi
2 CONC zed, filed, and wrote about South Carolina’s documentary heritage, the beginning of what is now the South Carolina Department of Archives and History. A pioneer in the nationwide archives movement, he was renowned for being a sour
2 CONC ce of information that assisted scholars and citizens. Salley’s lasting contribution to South Carolina is the state’s rich historical traditions preserved in her priceless documents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Salley died on February 19, 1961, and was buried at Sunnyside Cemetery in Orangeburg. He was survived by his wife, Harriet Gresham Milledge, whom he had married on July 11, 1918.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/81720605/alexander-samuel-salley
2 CONT
2 CONT Historian, Author. Born in Orangeburg, South Carolina; grandson of Dr. Alexander Samuel Salley, prominent Orangeburg Physician and Civil War Surgeon, and a descendant of Captain John Salley, of the American Revolution. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He attended Sheridan's classical school in Orangeburg, 1881 to 1887; was graduated from the South Carolina Military Academy (now The Citadel) in 1892; served on the engineer corps of the World's Columbian exposition, August to Sep
2 CONC tember, 1892; was private secretary to William Elliott, state representative, 1896; employed on the News and Courier newspaper, Charleston, S.C., 1896 to 1899; admitted to the SC bar in 1899, and elected secretary, treasurer and l
2 CONC ibrarian of the South Carolina Historical society that same year. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He served as the secretary of the Historical Commission of the State of South Carolina from 1905 to 1949, and was the first South Carolina State Historian. Responsible for first organizing South Carolina's official records archive
2 CONC s, he published an extensive series of pamphlets and indexes of those records. His other publications include: The History of Orangeburg County, S.C. (1898); Bibliography of William Gillmore Simms (1897), and Henry Timrod (1899) f
2 CONC or the Southern History Association, along with many other bibliographies, reports and articles. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was editor of The South Carolina Historical and Genealogical Magazine 1900 to 1908, and continued to contribute to the magazine throughout his life. Before his death he arranged a bequest to the Orangeburg County Historical Soc
2 CONC iety (OCHS) to establish a permanent historical archives building in that county which was completed in 1967 and stands as a monument to his work preserving the history of South Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT
