1 NAME Ernest Cantelo /White/
2 GIVN Ernest Cantelo
2 _MARNM White
1 NAME Ernest Cantelo White
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE APR 1880
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} E. Cantelo White is cited in several articles in "Electrical Merchandising," a periodical devoted to the lighting manufacturing industry.
2 CONT
2 CONT See for instance, 1921, February, Volume 25, number 2. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the article, "'Removable Fixture' Idea Favored by All Branches of the Industry: Proposal to 'Hang a Fixture Like a Picture' discussed by illuminating engineers, representatives of trade, manufacturers, inventors and inspectors,
2 CONC  before New York section of Illuminating Engineering Society on Jan. 13." The article describes a proposal that lighting fixtures be fitted with a plug device that allows fixtures to be moved from one location to another by the us
2 CONC er, without further need of an electrician; that this new plan will mean more volume of wiring for the contractor and dealer, and will enable him to earn his money more easily, at the same time giving the public a better job.
2 CONT
2 CONT      "Bennett Tousley, representing the Electric Outlet Company, New York City, described and exhibited the 'Elexit' form of receptacle and plug devised by E. Cantelo White, who is the manager of the lighting fixture department of
2 CONC  the Western Electric Company. (p. 72)"
2 CONT
2 CONT This periodical describes several devices besides the Elexit ones for which E. Cantelo White either applied for patents or received them. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Electrical Merchandising, 1921, September, Volumes 25-26:
2 CONT
2 CONT "E. Cantelo White, President Electric Outlet Company, 8 West Fortieth Street, New York City" was the author of a letter [accompanied by three illustrations] to the editor of 'Electrical Merchandising' magazine:
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. White writes rejoinder to 'Electrical Merchandising's objections that new Elexit Plugs do not employ "the Standard Plug with Parallel Blades."
2 CONT
2 CONT To the Editor:
2 CONT
2 CONT    In 'Electrical Merchandising' for August (page 90) there is an editorial entitled "A Step Backward From Plug Standardization,"  in which you criticize the new Elexit devices because the Elexit wall plug is not made with paralle
2 CONC l blades.
2 CONT
2 CONT      I am sure that everyone appreciates the good work which 'Electrical Merchandising' has done in helping to promote the adoption of a standard attachment plug for all electrical appliances. Such appliances, of course, are used 
2 CONC at a distance of a few feet from the outlet. Their only connection with the outlet is an electrical connection. The standard pug with parallel blades is used to make that connection and the whole value of that standardization rest
2 CONC s in the fact that anyone who purchases an appliance may find it ready for use.
2 CONT
2 CONT      'Electrical Merchandising' has also done pioneer work in helping to promote the adoption of standard receptacles and plugs for lighting fixtures, which standardization has now been accomplished in Elexits so that any lighting
2 CONC  fixture properly fitted with an Elexit plug becomes an elexilier, — i.e. ready to plug into any Elexit or to be readily moved from one Elexit to another. 
2 CONT
2 CONT      Elexit plugs are not designed for use as a mere electrical attachment plug for appliances. They provide for both electrical contact and mechanical support. They are intended for the sole purpose of changing lighting fixtures 
2 CONC into elexiliers.
2 CONT
2 CONT      As you indicate in your editorial, Elexit wall receptacles do take the standard plug with parallel blades, Every possible public convenience has been served in this respect. The standard plug with parallel blades may be plugg
2 CONC ed into an Elexit.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Elexit plugs are made for a new purpose and do not conflict with anything in public use for that purpose. The details of their construction were decided upon by a considerable number of the most expert engineers engaged in th
2 CONC e manufacture of wiring devices, and the reasons controlling the various details were engineering reasons.
2 CONT
2 CONT      It is true that Elexit devices have ample patent protection, and it is in this fact you may find the greatest assurance that Elexit standardization will be more than a theory. Things will fit. There will be a satisfactory rea
2 CONC lization of the results that we all want in the use of electric lighting. There is certainly no set-back to the use of the standard plug with parallel blades for connecting other electrical things. 
2 CONT
2 CONT           E. Cantelo White
2 CONT
2 CONT      President Electric Outlet Company.
2 CONT
2 CONT      [To produce the Elexit devices, this company had made licensing agreements with several lighting manufacturers: Arrow Electric Company, Benjamin electric Manufacturing Company, Bryant Electric Company, Cutler-Hammer Manufactu
2 CONC ring Company, Economy Fuse and Manufacturing Company, General Electric Company, Hart & Hegeman Manufacturing Company, Harvey Hubbell Inc., H. T. Paiste Company, Pass & Seymour, Inc., and Weber Electric Company. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT Downloaded 2010 from Google Books 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://books.google.com/books?id=11jlAAAAMAAJ&pg=RA1-PA120&lpg=RA1-PA120&dq=E.+Cantelo+White&source=bl&ots=U2w0iKtWp5&sig=wWyE4qA8_Hxts0TWuf5hNpqQJdk&hl=en&ei=jWdzTNCPEIGKlweZls2DAQ&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=1&ved=0CBIQ
2 CONC 6AEwAA#v=onepage&q=E.%20Cantelo%20White&f=false
2 CONT
2 CONT 1921 - Business & Economics
