1 NAME Johann "George" Jerig /Raudenbusch/
2 GIVN Johann "George" Jerig
2 SURN Raudenbusch
2 _MARNM Raudenbush
2 NICK Johan
2 NICK John
2 NICK George
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC New Goshenhoppen UCC Cemetery,  East Greenville, Montgomery County, Pennsylvania, USA
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 27 MAY 1697
2 PLAC Steinsfurt, Baden-Wurttemberg, Germany
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 17 AUG 1783
2 PLAC Upper Hanover, Philadelphia Co.
2 ADDR
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Johann George Raudenbusch, generally referred to simply as "George," arrived at Philadelphia aboard the ship William and Mary out of Rotterdam on September 21, 1727. On the immigration list his name is shown as "Ha
2 CONC ns Jerig Roadebas," and it states he was "sick on board, never came to be qualified," that is, to take the Oath of Allegience to the British Crown. There were two persons in his party, the other presumably being his wife, and thei
2 CONC r destination was said to be Skippack. Many of the passengers with them settled in the Skippack Creek Valley in what is now Skippack Township of Montgomery County, Penn.
2 CONT
2 CONT As was stated above, a fellow passenger with George was George Michael Weiss, the first pastor of the New Goshenhoppen Reformed Church, located in present day East Greenville, Penn. George and his wife were life long members of th
2 CONC is church.
2 CONT
2 CONT George quickly became a landowner. He is shown as "George Roudenbush," one of the German and Dutch inhabitants of Philadelphia County who paid quit rents prior to 1734. At this time he had a 150 acre farm in hanover Township. As "
2 CONC George Rooreback," he was warranted this land on October 2, 1740. Hanover Township now lies in Montgomery County, and George and his wife aparently lived out the remainder of their days on this farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Naturalization was not the simple thing it is now. The conditions were exceptionally severe, and comparatively few German immigrants to Pennsylvania qualified themselves to vote, but George was naturalized in Philadelphia on Septe
2 CONC mber 22, 1764.
2 CONT
2 CONT George died August 17, 1783 at 84 years, 2 months and 20 days (born May 27, 1699). His wife, Margaretha (maiden name unknown), was born September 28, 1702 and died November 24, 1776 at 74 years and eight weeks. Both are buried in 
2 CONC New Goshenhoppen Cemetery. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial: New Goshenhoppen UCC Cemetery  East Greenville Montgomery County Pennsylvania, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Created by: RabbitTrack Record added: Mar 11, 2011  Find A Grave Memorial# 66779158
