2 TITL Mary Ann  Roudabush  (born Miller)
2 _PRIM Y
1 NOTE Benjamin Roudabush was born near Landstreet, Somerset County on November 30, 1846 and was the son of Samuel and Elizabeth Weible Roudabush. He died at the age of 72 years at his store. He was the senior member of the grocery firm 
2 CONC of B. Roudabush & Sons on Bedford St. He was one of a large family, surviving brothers being Jacob Roudabush, Holsopple, and David Roudabush, of Paint Creek, Somerset Co., Pa. When the Civil War broke out in April of 1861 Samuel R
2 CONC oudabush enlisted in one of the Somerset County companies and died of camp fever at Little Washington, S.C. Soon after his death Benjamin Roudabush enlisted to take his place and was assigned to Company K., 206 Regiment, P.V.I und
2 CONC er Joseph C.Coulter. This was the same company in which the late Samuel Menoher, father of Gen.Charles Menoher, of the Rainbow Division, served as a private. Bejmain Roudabush's military record, a copy of which is proudly possesse
2 CONC d by his sons and daughters, makes mention of Benjamin Roudabush " having achieved a most gallant record for meritorious service and soldierly conduct at all times." Folowing the war Mr. Roudabush was engaged in business as a mill
2 CONC er and was locaed at Summerhill, this county, from 1874 to 1877 when he located at Elton, where he was in charge of a mill until locating here about 30 years ago. Soon after the Johnstown Flood of 1889 he purchased the grocery sto
2 CONC re of the late Nathan Chilcoat, then located on the site of the homestead of the late Jacob Hamilton and has since grown to be one of the leading grocery establishments of Johnstown. About 12 years ago Mr. Roudabush formed a partn
2 CONC ership under the firm name of B. Roudabush & Sons, the latter being Galvin B. Roudabush, Cloyd S. Roudabush, and Samuel W. Roudabush. The Roudabush apartments were erected soon after the sons became associated with their father. T
2 CONC he deceased and Miss Mary Ann Miller, daughter of the late Daniel Miller, were married at Paint creek on October 5, 1869 by Justice Seese. Besides his widow and brothers, Mr. Roudabush is survived by three sons and a daughter, as 
2 CONC follows: Galvin B., who resides in the Roudabush apartments, Bedford St.; Cloyd s. who also resides in the apartments; Samuel W. Roudabush, at home ,and a daughter Rosie May wife of Merchant Edward F. Wright, Horner St., 7th. Ward
2 CONC  at 722 Sherman St. South Side. Five grandchildren also survive. They are Edgard S. who is in military training at Albright College, Myerstown; and Miss Margaret Roudabush, a son and daughter of Mr. & Mrs. G.B. Roudabush; Donald a
2 CONC nd Charles, sons of Mr. & Mrs. Cloyd Roudabush, and Eva daughter of Mr. & Mrs. Edward F. Wright. Mr. Roudabush was an officer of the Willow St. United Evangelical Church for many years. Yesterday was the first church and Sunday Sc
2 CONC hool sessions he had missed since 1912. Benjamin Roudabush was identified with Emory Fisher Post No. 30, G.A.R. and was a charter member of the Johnstown Grocery Company and a director of the Johnstown Baking Company. At one time 
2 CONC he was the director of the Johnstown Grocery Company. Johnstown Daily Tribune, November 11, 1918.
