1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for NORTH CAROLINA.  [http://services.dar.org/Public/DAR_Research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A204939  DAR Ancestor #  A204939]
2 CONT
2 CONT Did not move to North Carolina until around 1770, so NOT the father of [https://www.geni.com/people/Susannah-Shores/6000000003266932978 Susanna M. Pipes Shores], born in 1728 in North Carolina.
2 CONT ---------------
2 CONT From http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/PIPES/2001-06/0993604506 :
2 CONT
2 CONT The earliest confirmed ancestor is John Pipes Sr., born 1710-15, place unknown. He married Susanna Hathaway, born 16 January 1712, daughter of Abraham Hathaway, on 3 October 1735, in Berkley, Bristol County, Massachusetts. Abraham
2 CONC  sold his property in Massachusetts in 1736, one year after John and Susanna were married, and he shows up in Morristown, N. J., later in the same year. The Hathaway family continues to be associated with the Pipes family in New J
2 CONC ersey and Pennsylvania. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 25 February 1746, William Plumstead offered a reward for the jailing of four men who had run away from the ship, "Westmoreland." One of the men was John Pipes. It seems highly unlikely that this is the John Pipes who appears la
2 CONC ter in New Jersey, but it more than likely someone else, perhaps a British sailor with the same name. The item appeared in Benjamin Franklin's newspaper, "Pennsylvania Gazette." William Plumstead was a wealthy man who dealt in the
2 CONC  slave trade and was at one time Mayor of Philadelphia. There are unverified stories that some of John's and Susanna's children, particularly Windsor, were born in Philadelphia. Windsor was born 16 September 1740.
2 CONT
2 CONT In July 1750 John Pipes Sr. In Morris Co., N. J. was convicted of counterfeiting money. Associated with Pipes in this case were Abraham and Jonathan Hathaway. Others connected were John Allen and Tim Southard. Pipes would be assoc
2 CONC iated with the Allen and Southard families in Surry County, North Carolina. Sylvanus Totted and his son, Abraham Totten are also mentioned in the counterfeiting court records.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 8 September 1750 attached to a will of Stephen Thompson, a blacksmith, of Rockaway township, there is a business firm called Pipes and Brown. The Rockaway township is noted for iron works and the Allen family continued in the t
2 CONC rade after they came to North Carolina. John Pipes may have also been an ironworker and his business firm, Pipes and Brown, may have been related to the iron working industry.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 September 1751, John Pipes Sr., Elizabethtown N. J., was arrested for a felony committed in the house of Edward Thomas on Sunday, September 15, 1751. The offense is not stated. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite some early law breaking, John Pipes must have reformed for some of the next records indicate he was active as a church worker. The records of the original Presbyterian Church of Rockaway, Morris County,  NJ, show that John
2 CONC  Pipes contributed one pound toward founding the church and calling a minister: "March 2d, 1758. We the subscribers do by these mannerfest it to be our deseir to Joyn with Porsipaney to call and settel a minnister, to have the one
2 CONC  half of the preachen at prosipaney and the other  half at rockaway, and each part to be eakwel in payen a minnester. (signed) John pipes, ....John Harriman,...John gobbel,... John Cogswell." A second document was recorded the sam
2 CONC e day: "We the Inhabitants of rockaway, pigen hill and upper inhabitence at the colonial forges and places agesant, being together in order to consult together about a place to set a meeitng hous, and being all well agreed that th
2 CONC e most sutable place for the hol setelments is upon the small plain a letel above bemans forg, which is below the first small brok upon that rode up to Samuel Johnson, and we the subscriers a blig ourselves to pay toward building 
2 CONC a house at that place that sums to our names afixed:...John Pipes, L1...John Harriman, L3...John Gobel...10s...John Cogswell, L1..." In September 1760, John Pipes and adult sons "Winsur" and John Pipes Jr. Were among the signers o
2 CONC f an "Obligation to pay minister by rate." 
2 CONT
2 CONT Lois Thurston of Chelsea, Maine, who is working on a history of the Harriman family furnishes the following information: "Sometime before or around 1770, the Pipes family left New Jersey. Windsor moved to Pennsylvania and John Sr.
2 CONC  and the other three sons moved to Surry County, North Carolina. In 1771,  Windsor sold his land in Pennsylvania and joined the family in North Carolina." A Greene Co., PA., deed shows that Windsor owned land on Smith Creek, south
2 CONC  of Waynesburg, PA, which he sold in 1771.An interesting but unconfirmed story concerns the Pipes, the Hathaways and other families leaving New Jersey and heading to southwest Pennsylvania. They were attacked by Indians along a ri
2 CONC ver and had to turn back. It has been speculated that this is the time when Joseph Pipes, son of John Jr. and Jemima (Harriman) was kidnapped and that perhaps Jemima was killed by the Indians. The next year some of the same group 
2 CONC made another attempt and this time they were successful.In Tryon Co. North Carolina, Land Warrants 1768-74 abstracted by Miles Philbeck, Windsor Pipes claims "...150 acres on Little Broad River beginning at Roaring Shoal, running 
2 CONC up river, and lying between Fisher's and Lusk's land. Warrant 458, 15 December 1769." The completion or disposition of this land has not been traced, but it would seem to indicate that Windsor was the first Pipes in North Carolina
2 CONC , and he was there before going to Pennsylvania.Abner Pipes was listed on the 1771 Surry County, N. C., tax list, the only time he appears. John Pipes Jr. appears in 1771, but does not appear in the Surry County records again unti
2 CONC l 1780. Phillip appears for the first time in  1772.
2 CONT
2 CONT In September 1771, George Soelle, early Moravian minister, made a trip to "Pipes house" in "Allen's settlement." He remarked: "The settlers here are all Irish, a robbed and plundered people, where proverty makes itself at home....
2 CONC My host received me gladly and cared for me as well as he could; milk and cornbread was the fare practically all the time I was there, and not enough of that." [From the Moravian Records.] Recorded in Pascal's History of North Car
2 CONC olina Baptists : ""John Pipes invited Moravian missionary, George  Soelle, to visit Baptists east of Yadkin...found Pipes forty miles distant in new settlement on Deep Creek."  the name of John Pipes appears in the Surry County re
2 CONC cords from 1771 until about 1797. Sometimes the designation Senior or Junior follows and sometimes the name stands alone. John Pipes Sr. may have left Surry County in November 1783 and moved to South Carolina at that time. There m
2 CONC ay have been some  family difficulty as none of the witnesses to the 25 November 1783 deed to Humphrey Cockran are members of the Pipes family. A speculation as to the reason could be that John Sr. had married a much younger woman
2 CONC , Priscilla. [Susannah (Hathaway) Pipes must have died prior to this time.] This is evidenced by their having four females in the household in the 1790 Spartanburg County, South Carolina, census, possibly a wife and three daughter
2 CONC s. By 1800 there is only one female aged 16-26 in the household other than the two adults assumed to be John and Priscilla. In 1790, John would have been approximately 80 years of age. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 23 January 1793, John Pipes witnessed a deed in Spartanburg Co., SC, between John Cobbs and Willliam Garrett. The other two witnesses are Abraham Cantrell and Elijah Thompson. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 14 June 1793, in Spartanburg Co., SC, John and Priscilla made a gift deed as follows: "We do grant to our dear children Priscilla, Ruth, and Sarah Pipes in equal porportion 11 head of cattle, three head of horses, seven head of
2 CONC  sheep, one loom, two feather beds with furniture thereunto belonging, pewter and all household furniture, etc." The deed was witnessed by Thomas Williamson and William Poole. This deed may have been to insure that John's children
2 CONC  by his previous marriage did not inherit his property. The deed was  recorded 16 July 1793. John Pipes acquired land on 6 April 1795 from John Boley in Spartanburg County for one Spanish milled dollar, 200 acres on Sholey Creek. 
2 CONC Since 200 acres would be worth more than one Spanish milled dollar, there is a possibility that John Pipes is the son-in-law of John Boley, and that the deed is a gift deed. There is a John Boling in Surry County only a few miles 
2 CONC from John Pipes Sr., and the said John Boling disappears from Surry County in the 1780's. Perhaps he and John Pipes moved from Surry County to South Carolina at the same time. The name is sometimes spelled Bowling. John Pipes Sr. 
2 CONC wrote his will on 18 November 1804 leaving all his moveable and unmovable property to his wife, Priscilla, and after her death to his daughter, Sally Pipes. On the reverse of the will is the notation "not proven." Thomas Walling a
2 CONC pplied for administration 27 March 1805. Rather than a certification of the citation being recorded on the reverse is the  following: "We the legatees of the deceased John Pipes have met amongst ourselves considering that the cost
2 CONC  would out do the profit and put a poor old widow to suffer in her old days....Thomas Walling, Benjamin Lewis, Sally Pipes." It was signed on 28 March 1805. Priscilla Pipes probably died about 1814. On 14 April 1814, Absalom Roe, 
2 CONC Sally Roe, and Benjamin Lewis, sold land to Edmund Bishop on Skoley Creek, a branch of Lauson's Fork of the Pacolet River, "part of 200 acres deeded to Sally Roe by her father, John Pipes." 
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of John Pipes Sr. and his first wife, Susanna, appear to be:
2 CONT * 1-1 John Pipes Jr., born 1737-38, married (1) Jemima Harriman (2) Mary Morris.
2 CONT * 1-2 Windsor Pipes, born 16 September 1740, married Jane (Jeanett) McAfee.
2 CONT * 1-3 Abner Pipes, born about 1742, married Mary _________.
2 CONT * 1-4 Susanna Pipes, born 1745-55, married Samuel Carter.
2 CONT * 1-5 Phillip Pipes, born 1750-55, married _________________.
2 CONT * 1-6 Sylvanus Pipes, born 1750-55, married _________________.
2 CONT
2 CONT [The births of the last three children are approximate and they are listed in the order they first appear in public records. Susanna is in court in 1771 on a charge of fornication. Phillip first appears on the Surry County tax lis
2 CONC t in 1772. Sylvanus's first appearance in public records is a land warrant in 1778. There may have been other unidentified children.] 
2 CONT
2 CONT The children of John and Priscilla appear to be:
2 CONT * 1-7 Priscilla Pipes
2 CONT * 1-8 Ruth Pipes (One of the daughters married Thomas Walling and the other one married  Benjamin Lewis.)
2 CONT * 1-9 Sarah Pipes who married Absalom Rowe.
