1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biographical Summary===
2 CONT Hendrick (Henry) DeCamp (c. 1682–1771) was an early colonial settler and a foundational figure in the New Jersey branch of the DeCamp family. He was the son of the Huguenot immigrant Laurens Janson DeCamp. Throughout his life, Hen
2 CONC drick was active in the colonial communities of New York and New Jersey, eventually establishing his family in the region spanning Woodbridge and Elizabethtown, New Jersey—an area later known as Scotch Plains.
2 CONT He is historically recognized as the father of Lambert DeCamp (baptized 1711). Hendrick’s legacy is tied to the expansion of his family into New Jersey during the early 18th century, a time characterized by land development and th
2 CONC e establishment of local family burial grounds.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parentage===
2 CONT Father: Laurens Janson DeCamp (born 1647)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Elsie
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Vital Statistics===
2 CONT Birth: Circa 1682; New Utrecht, New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: June 1771; Woodbridge, Middlesex County, New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Marriage and Issue===
2 CONT Spouse: Maria (Mary) De Lamers (c. 1685–unknown) m. 1704
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT 1. Laurens DeCamp (1705–1709)
2 CONT 2. Laurens DeCamp (1709–1735)
2 CONT 3. Lambert DeCamp (1711–1784) – Married Mary Boude
2 CONT 4. Johanes (John) DeCamp (1715–1782)
2 CONT 5. Hendrick DeCamp
2 CONT 6. David DeCamp
2 CONT 7. Gideon DeCamp
2 CONT 8. Christina DeCamp (Woodruff)
2 CONT 9. Christoffel DeCamp (c. 1725–1740)
2 CONT 10. Benjamin DeCamp
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Factual Information and Relevance===
2 CONT Hendrick DeCamp was a descendant of a French Huguenot family that fled to Holland before emigrating to New Amsterdam (now New York) in 1664. His relocation from New York to the border of Woodbridge and Elizabethtown, New Jersey, i
2 CONC n 1740, placed him among the early settlers of the region. He is a critical ancestral link in the DAR-verified lineage of his grandson, Ensign James DeCamp (A031214), through his son Lambert.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Verifiable Sources and Records===
2 CONT Morrison, George Austin, Jr. De Camp Genealogy. 1900. (The primary scholarly compilation for the family lineage).
2 CONT
2 CONT New Jersey Colonial Records: Wills and probate inventories (New Jersey State Archives).
2 CONT
2 CONT Dutch Reformed Church Records: Baptismal and marriage registers for Staten Island and surrounding regions, which document the family’s early vital statistics.
2 CONT
2 CONT DAR Genealogical Research System (GRS): Verified patriot ancestor records for descendants like Ensign James DeCamp.
2 CONT
