1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Biographical Summary===
2 CONT Lambert DeCamp (1711–1779) was a colonial-era resident of New Jersey and a patriarch of the DeCamp family lineage. He is historically recognized as the father of Ensign James DeCamp (DAR Ancestor A031214), a patriot of the America
2 CONC n Revolutionary War. His family line is among those documented in early genealogical compilations concerning the settlement of the Passaic Valley and surrounding regions.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Parentage===
2 CONT Father: Hendrick (Henry) DeCamp
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Marie De Lamers (also known as Maria De Lamars)
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Vital Statistics===
2 CONT Birth: Baptized April 17, 1711; Staten Island, New York.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: 1779; New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Marriage and Issue===Spouse: Mary Boude (married circa 1740s).
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT 1. James DeCamp (1749–1814): Ensign, 3rd Regiment, New Jersey Militia.
2 CONT
2 CONT -Note: Genealogical records (such as those by George Austin Morrison, Jr.) identify Lambert as having several children; however, James DeCamp is the only one tied directly to verified Revolutionary War patriot lineage records.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Historical Documentation and Sources===
2 CONT Primary/Genealogical Source: Morrison, George Austin, Jr. De Camp Genealogy. New York: 1900. (Documents the baptism of Lambert DeCamp, son of Hendrick and Marie, on April 17, 1711).
2 CONT
2 CONT DAR Verification: The National Society Daughters of the American Revolution (DAR) validates the connection between Lambert DeCamp and his son, Ensign James DeCamp, through Patriot Ancestor Record A031214.
2 CONT
2 CONT Contextual Reference: Littell, John. Family Records; or, Genealogies of the First Settlers of Passaic Valley and Vicinity Above Chatham. 1851. (Provides early regional context for the DeCamp family).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Factual Information and Relevance===
2 CONT The identification of Lambert DeCamp’s parents—Hendrick DeCamp and Marie De Lamers—is derived from 18th-century baptismal registers on Staten Island. These records serve as the definitive link for his lineage. As with many familie
2 CONC s in the New Jersey/New York region during the 1700s, specific details regarding his land holdings and death are sometimes difficult to pin down due to the loss of local colonial archives during the Revolutionary War era. However,
2 CONC  his status as the progenitor of the line leading to Ensign James DeCamp is confirmed via the established DAR patriot files.
