1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Susan Hammond Ashby 
2 CONT
2 CONT In Marblehead, Massachusetts lived the family of Edward and Rebecca Flack Hammond. One of the children of this fine family was a daughter named Susan. This lovely lady grew to womanhood in the town of her birth. The ancestors of S
2 CONC usan had been among the earliest settlers of Maine. Her family had given to Susan a fine heritage. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Susan met and married Nathaniel Ashby on November 30, 1826 in Salem, Massachusetts and became parents of a large worthy family. They had eleven children: Benjamin Ashby, Susan Ann Ashby, Elizabeth Rebecca Ashby, Martha Ellen Ashby
2 CONC , Harriet Maria Ashby, Nathaniel Ashby Jr., Richard Hammond Ashby, William Hardin Ashby, Mary Jane Ashby, John Jefford Ashby and Emma Smith Ashby. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ashbys were converted to Mormonism by Elder Erastus Snow who was then laboring as a missionary in the Eastern States. The Ashby family moved to Nauvoo, Illinois in 1843. During the early 1840's the Nauvoo Temple was being rush
2 CONC ed to completion. The Ashby family gave generously of their substance toward this wonderful cause. Susan had woven two rugs, one with a wreath of roses and one with a basket of flowers. These, together with a beautiful Brussels ca
2 CONC rpet, went into the Nauvoo Temple.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the great western exodus began the Nathaniel Ashby's were among that devoted band that started West. Soon after leaving Nauvoo Nathaniel Ashby died September 23, 1846, three years after their youngest daughter was born on the
2 CONC  plains near Bonaparte, Iowa. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The family of this good man continued on to Winter Quarters where they met Joseph Bates Noble and they were married Mar 3, 1847 at Winter Quarters. Susan and family remained in Winter Quarters during the Winter and Spring of 1847-
2 CONC 48. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In June 1848 Susan and her children started West and traveled in Brigham Young Company to the Salt Lake Valley. On arrival they were met by Joseph B. Noble and taken into one of his homes in the North Fort. Here they were kindly r
2 CONC eceived by Mary Noble and here the Ashby family lived for some time.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Nov 9, 1849, a daughter named Louisa Adeline Noble was born to Susan and Joseph Bates Noble. This child grew to womanhood and later married Rodney Badger of Salt Lake City. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In Feb 1851 when Mary Noble died, Susan Ashby Noble took into her home the three motherless children of Mary and gave to them loving care. But Susan was not to live long herself. She passed away May 16, 1851, in Salt Lake City.
2 CONT
2 CONT The life of this good woman was an example of devotion and sacrifice to her family and to the Gospel she embraced. (Susan and J. B. Noble were married for Time Only).
2 CONT
2 CONT Her history is also on [http://www.biographicalwiki.com/index.php/Susan_Hammond_(1808-1851) BIOGRAPHICAL WIKI]
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: When Susan died, they included her first husband, Nathaniel's name with her own on their gravestone in Salt Lake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Susan and Joseph Bates Noble were married for time only.
2 CONT * Updated from [http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&amp;GRid=21711373 Find A Grave Memorial] via son [http://www.geni.com/profile-34676253267 William Hardin Ashby] by [http://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 Smar
2 CONC tCopy]: ''Jul 27 2015, 13:49:34 UTC''
