1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Susan was born in New York to Eunice Noble and Charles Fairchild, but was adopted as a child by her grand parents, Ezekiel (1787-1856)and Theodocia Bates(1789-1869) Noble. She joined the church as a youngster and c
2 CONC ame to Utah with her uncle Joseph Bates Noble in Jedediah M. Grant's company in 1847. After marrying Jedediah on 11 Feb 1849, she bore him two children, Joseph Hyrum and Susan Vilate, in addition to raising his daughter, Caroline,
2 CONC  by his first marriage and an adopted son, Lewis McKeachie. Following Jedediah's death, she married his brother George D. Grant in 1858 and bore him a son named Franklin Davis Grant. They were later divorced. Susan lived out her l
2 CONC ife in West Bountiful, where she was president of the ward Relief Society (1878-85) and then Stake Relief Society president from 1886 to 1892, when she was called to the General Relief Society Board. 
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: http://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=39461977
2 CONT
2 CONT Susan Fairchild Noble is the granddaughter Of Ezekiel Noble and Theodocia Bates; she was born to their daughter Eunice Noble and Charles Fairchild.  She was raised by her grandparents and so was not called by her name Susan Noble 
2 CONC Fairchild, but was known as Susan Fairchild Noble. She joined the LDS Church and went to Utah along with her uncle, Joseph Bates Noble, who was always referred to as uncle Bates not as her older brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT source: familysearch.org - contributor  LG Porter
2 CONT
2 CONT Susan was almost 15 years old, when she left Winter Quarters - (North Omaha) to come out West. All of the Companies began their journey from the outfitting post on the Elkhorn River, about 27 miles west of Winter Quarters.She left
2 CONC  with the Abraham O Smoot - George B Wallace Company, traveling with the Wallaces. Being a teenager, she spent time with friends from other companies, during the journey, having the exuberance of youth. The wagon companies travele
2 CONC d only a half-mile apart, side by side as a safety precaution against Native Americans and camped not too far, apart. It is believed that she joined up with Jedediah M Grant's Overland Company of 171 pioneers about fifty miles bef
2 CONC ore Sweetwater, Wyoming. She was very close friends with Jedediah Grant's wife, Caroline as were the Wallaces. She likely, wanted to spend time with her and be near her, seeing how ill Caroline was becoming. Susan and Rosetta Robi
2 CONC son, took care of Caroline's children, Margaret and Caddie during that leg of the trip. Susan either stayed with Grant's company or returned back to Salt Lake with the Wallace Company, since the two Companies kept passing each oth
2 CONC er, due to delays.  - contributor  Helen Barton Peterson  (Mormon Pioneer Overland Travel - site)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Susan was born in New York to Eunice Noble and Charles Fairchild, but was adopted as a child by her grand parents, Ezekiel (1787-1856)and Theodocia Bates(1789-1869) Noble. She joined the church as a youngster and came to Utah with
2 CONC  her uncle Joseph Bates Noble in Jedediah M. Grant's company in 1847. After marrying Jedediah on 11 Feb 1849, she bore him two children, Joseph Hyrum and Susan Vilate, in addition to raising his daughter, Caroline, by his first ma
2 CONC rriage and an adopted son, Lewis McKeachie. Following Jedediah's death, she married his brother George D. Grant  in 1858 and bore him a son named Franklin Davis Grant. They were later divorced. Susan lived out her life in West Bou
2 CONC ntiful, where she was president of the ward Relief Society (1878-85) and then Stake Relief Society president from 1886 to 1892, when she was called to the General Relief Society Board.
