2 CONT *   (1) Of Isaac Martin but little is known beyond the fact that he lived at Rehoboth from 1644 to 1646. As there were no Martin descendants of either Abraham or Robert we infer that we owe our parentage to Isaac. 
2 CONT *   The Middlesex Court shows that "John Martin" gave evidence at court, April 15th, 1658, relative to Paul Wilson engaging the affection of his cousin Pricilla Upham. If John and Pricilla were cousins, Elizabeth, the wife of Deac
2 CONC on John Upham, must have been a sister of Robert, Abram and Isaac, and John must have been Isaac's son. 
2 CONT *   Richard was the brother of Robert, Abram and Isaac and came to New England to take possession of the property left him by the will of Robert. 
2 CONT *   The date of his arrival is not known, but it is probable that he, with his son, John, came over about 1663. 
2 CONT *   June 1st, 1689, he was appointed as surveyor of highways for Rehoboth. 
2 CONT *   Richard advanced 1 pound, 5 shillings, 4 pence, to sustain in carrying on the war against the Indians, under King Phillip. 
2 CONT *   He died May 2nd, 1694, leaving his property to his sons. John, Francis and Richard, Jr., and two daughters, Annie and Elanor. 
2 CONT *'''   (2) John Martin''' (son of Isaac Martin) '''immigrant ancestor of this branch of the family, died July 5th, 1687. He was of Dover, New Hampshire, 1648-1666. Piscataway, New Jersey, 1666-1676, and Woodbridge, New Jersey, 167
2 CONC 6-1687. His wife was Hester Roberts''', daughter of Thomas Roberts, who settled in Dover, N. H., in 1823, and was called "Governor" of the state in 1640, the Colonial Government not being very definite or certain. 
2 CONT *'''   John Martin was one of the original grantees of Piscataway, N. J., in 1666. 
2 CONT *   (3) Thomas Martin, born 1659, died 1715, was the '''son of John and Hester (Roberts) Martin of Piscataway, N. J.''' He married, April 28th, 1683, Rebecca Higgins (daughter of Richard and Mary Higgins). Their family:  .... etc.
