2 CONT *'''Lineage Book of Hereditary Order of Descendants of Colonial Governors By Robert Glenn Thurtle
2 CONT *[http://books.google.com/books?id=xUmYgRsSTZUC&pg=PA311&lpg=PA311&dq=John+Martin+1647+Dorothy+Smith&source=bl&ots=rZJo5BYN02&sig=CQJgaiOWhFZQ2iPL7RS3GyZnlFo&hl=en&ei=e4x7Tu3FHbHTiAKtpJy6Bw&sa=X&oi=book_result&ct=result&resnum=4&v
2 CONC ed=0CC8Q6AEwAzgK#v=onepage&q=John%20Martin%201647%20Dorothy%20Smith&f=false/ Lineage Book of Hereditary Order of Descendants of Colonial Governors By Robert Glenn Thurtle]
2 CONT *Pg. 311
2 CONT *1. Thomas Roberts, Governor of New Hampshire 1640-41
2 CONT **2. '''Esther Roberts''' New Hamshire c.1626 Piscataway, NJ 12 Dec 1687 '''m. c.1646 John Martin Durham, Eng. c.1620 Piscataway 5 July 1587
2 CONT ***3. John Martin, Jr. Dover, NH 1647 Piscataway 1703 m. 26 Jun 1677 Dorothy Smith 28 Mar 1698
2 CONT ****4. Patience Martin m. 31 Oct 1704 Daniel Sutton Piscatway 25 Feb 1682 Piscataway 1761 __________
2 CONT *'''Meredith, N.H. : annals and genealogies (1932)
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/details/meredithnhannals00hana
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/meredithnhannals00hana#page/427/mode/1up
2 CONT *   Stearns' History states that Thomas Roberts settled on Dover Neck, about 1623. The land he was on was occupied by Roberts' descendants over two centuries. They were men who were in 
2 CONT *http://www.archive.org/stream/meredithnhannals00hana#page/428/mode/1up 
2 CONT *public offices and occupied high positions. In 1662 he rebuked his sons, who were constables, for enforcing the law against Quaker women. 
2 CONT *    Thomas Roberts had been a member of the church over twenty years. He died about 1673 and was buried on Dover Neck. Tradition, through Charles Roberts of Meredith, states that Thomas Roberts was drawn on a jury as one of the m
2 CONC en to punish the Quaker women for maintaining their religion. He refused and the Dover magistrate took his cow away to punish him. In his will he gave most of his property to his daughter, Sarah, and husband, Richard Rich, but rem
2 CONC embered in his will, John and Thomas; '''Hester, wife of John Martin'''; Anne, wife of James Philbrick of Hampton; and Elizabeth, wife of Benjamin Heard. 
2 CONT *   John Roberts, born in 1629, his son, was prominent on Dover Neck; he married Abigail, daughter of Elder Hate-evil Nutter. They had children: Joseph, Hatevil, and probably Thomas and Abigail Nutter Roberts. In 1679 John was in 
2 CONC Portsmouth, served as marshall of the Province, but resigned in 1681. 
2 CONT *   Thomas Roberts, born in 1633, married Mary, daughter of Thomas Leighton of Dover. 
2 CONT *   Nathaniel lived on Dover Neck; married Elizabeth Mason.  _______________________________
2 CONT *'''Documents Relating to the Colonial History of the State ..., Volume 1; Volume 23
2 CONT *https://books.google.com/books?id=LQsbAAAAYAAJ
2 CONT *Pg.307
2 CONT *SAME AS Documents Relating to the Colonial, Revolutionary and Post ..., Volume 23 _________________
