2 CONT === Life Sketch ===
2 CONT ** DOCUMENTARY & DNA EVIDENCE SHOW FRANCIS IS NOT ROBERT'S SON. 1) Francis isn't in Robert's will. Some have argued that's because Francis became a Baptist, which led to an estrangement between father and son, which led to Robert 
2 CONC refusing to include Francis in his will, but the only evidence of this estrangement is Francis's absence from Robert's will, which can be explained quite simply: he wasn't Robert's son. 2) DNA testing shows Francis's known descend
2 CONC ants aren't related to Robert's known descendants, which means Robert and Francis weren't related. See extended discussion with multiple sources: https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Drake-65 
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis Drake was an early inhabitant of Dover Township in the Province of New Hampshire. He and Mary married there in about 1650. Her surname is not known with any certainty, but is believed to have been Walker. Francis and his f
2 CONC amily moved to Middlesex County in the New Jersey Province and were early settlers of Piscataway Township. Drake was a Baptist, and it's likely he moved to New Jersey because it was more tolerant of religious difference than Massa
2 CONC chusetts. From 1673 and afterward, Francis Drake appears in numerous civil records of New Jersey. In particular, he was granted a license to keep a tavern in 1673, as a surveyor in 1674, and commissioned as captain of the local mi
2 CONC litia in 1675. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary died in 1685. Francis Drake died September 24, 1687 in Middlesex County. No burial place is known for either of them, but the Stelton Baptist Church Cemetery would seem likely.
2 CONT
2 CONT Francis & Mary had THREE children: George, John, and Elizabeth.
2 CONT
