1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Hezekiah was a lineal descendant (grand son) of Mayflower passenger Edward Fuller.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT ==Who Were Hezekiah Bonham's Wives?==
2 CONT See http://web.pdx.edu/~davide/gene/Bonham_Hezekiah_2.htm for an excellent discussion of whether Ann Hunt was Hezekiah Bonham's second wife, with references to other research about this family.  The researchers have concluded that
2 CONC  Hezekiah Bonham was married to (1) Mary Dunn, who he married in 1690 and who died in November1699 and (2) Mary Bishop, who he married soon after the death of his first wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was believed, based on the will of Samuel Hunt, that Hezekiah's second wife was Ann Hunt.  However Ann Hunt would have been too young to be the second wife.  Research and a gravestone "Mary Bonham d. May 3, 1634 age 51," sugges
2 CONC t that the second wife was named Mary and was Mary Bishop, daughter of David and Mary Alger Bishop.
2 CONT
2 CONT So children born up to November 1699 should be attached to Mary Dunn and those born 1700 and later should be attached to Mary Bishop.   
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Hezekiah Bonham==
2 CONT * Birth:	May 6 1667 Sergeant Nicholas Bonham was born about 1630 and may have been the son of George Bonham and his first wife. Some genealogists believe he was George’s brother, rather than his son. He married Hannah Fuller on Ja
2 CONC nuary 1, 1658 in Barnstable, Massachusetts. Their children and life together are described in detail in the section on Nicholas and Hannah Bonham. He signed the Oath of Allegiance in 1672 to Holland. Part of New Jersey was settled
2 CONC  by the Dutch as New Netherland. In 1664 the entire area was seized by the British, and given the name New Jersey, but it was not until the Treaty of Westminster in 1674 that they gained control. He was approved Sergeant in 1681. 
2 CONC Nicholas named the town of Bonhamtown in Woodbridge Township, Middlesex County, New Jersey. Bonhamtown, five miles northeast from New Brunswick, was named for Nicholas Bonham, who was one of the first settlers, having become a fre
2 CONC eholder in 1682, and so recorded as owner of lands by allotment.  During the Revolution five British regiments were stationed at Bonhamtown, and robbed and harried the residents for a considerable period, burning the houses and ba
2 CONC rns, and committing other outrages. The people were very loyal to the patriot cause. (from History of Middlesex County, New Jersey, John P. Wall and Harold E. Pickersgill) He died on July 20, 1684 in Piscataway, Middlesex County, 
2 CONC New Jersey. His will was proved on December 18, 1684. Children of Nicholas Bonham and Hannah Fuller  Hannah Bonham Lippington Mary Bonham Dunham Elizabeth Bonham Slater Sarah Bonham Fitz Randolph Nicholas Bonham Elijah Bonham Heze
2 CONC kiah Bonham Samuel Bonham Jane Bonham Priscilla Bonham Langstaff Piscataway Township in New Jersey was first settled in 1666 by Quakers and Baptists who had left the Puritan colony in New Hampshire. A freeholder is the owner of a 
2 CONC freehold estate which is an interest in land that is not fixed by a specified period of time, but which may last during the lifetime of a person. Old Style Calendar Before 1752 the year began on Lady Day, March 25th,. Dates betwee
2 CONC n January 1st and March 24th were at the end of the year. Old Style (O.S.) and New Style (N.S.) are used to indicate whether the year has been adjusted. Often both dates are used.  Barnstable, Massachusetts was settled in 1639 whe
2 CONC n Parson Joseph Hull came to Cape Cod with and his congregation from Weymouth. A little later in the year, the Reverend John Lothrop brought his Congregationalists. They incorporated as the Town of Barnstable.
2 CONT
2 CONT * Death:	Jan 27 1733 - Maidenhead, Hunterdon, New Jersey
2 CONT * Parents:	Nicholas Bonham, Hannah Fuller
2 CONT * Married:	Mary Dunn, Mary Bishop
2 CONT * Children:	Mary Randolph (born Bonham), Samuel Bonham, Hannah Stout (born Bonham), Sarah Bonham, Zedekiah Bonham, Amaziah Bonham, Jeremiah Bonham, Ephraim Bonham, Josiah Bonham, Isaiah Bonham, Obadiah Bonham, Uriah Bonham, Hezeki
2 CONC ah Bonham, Zephaniah Bonham, Zachariah Bonham, Malachiah Bonham, Nehemiah Bonham, Amariah Bonham
2 CONT
2 CONT Hezekiah Bonham is listed among freeholders who took up land in 1683 in "History of Union and Middlesex Counties, NJ", page 587. His father's will (Nicholas Bonham) was written 06 Feb 1683/4 and he was called "...of ye town of Pis
2 CONC cataway in County of Middlesex in East New Jersey. Hezekiah was named as an executor with his mother Hannah; with Edward Slater and Isaac Smalley named as overseers.  A land patent to Ezekiah (Hezekiah) Bonham of Piscataway for 10
2 CONC 0 acres was recorded 02 Oct 1695 (NJ Archives, Vol. XXI, pages 238 239). Several other land listings can be found in this archive. In 1698 he purchased land in Maidenhead, Hunterdon Co., NJ. By 1710 he had moved his family to west
2 CONC ern NJ after his first wife died and he had remarried Mary/Maria Bishop c1700. From "First Settlers of Ye Plantations of Piscataway and Woodsridge Olde East New Jersey part 4", (on-line book at FTM's Genealogy Library): The death,
2 CONC  July 20, 1684, and will of Nicholas Bonham, 1683-4, has been treated elsewhere; showing survival of widow, Hannah Bonham, 1686. (ARCH., Vol. XXIII, p. 4??). (Vide, ante, PART THREE, pp. 408 and 409). Of the sons, only HEZEKIAH BO
2 CONC NHAM survived or remained in Piscataway, to either perpetuate the name or family, and he soon removed to Maidenhead. He received his patent for land, May 1, 1697, in right of his late father, Ezekiah Bonham (error for Nicholas Bon
2 CONC ham) etc. (ARCH. Vol. XXI, pp. 268 and 281.) He m. ca. 1690, Mary Dunn, (dau. of Hugh, Sr.) (Vide, ante, PART THREE, p. 416), b. Jan. 19, 1671-2 and d. Nov. 7, 1699, who had four children recorded at Piscataway, (foll.) as per (vi
2 CONC de, ante, PART TWO, p. 242.) In 1698-9 HEZEKIAH BONHAM, SR, joined with others in the purchase at Maidenhead, he removing there soon, altho this may not have occurred before 1712, (vide, ante, PART ONE, p. 113 and BURLING, pp. 844
2 CONC , 849 and 817). As he became the father of a further large family of children, he soon m. (2) Ann Hunt, (dau. of either Ralph Hunt, Sr., or dau. Ann, of Samuel Hunt, son of the first RALPH; by certain inference, (ARCH., Vol. XXIII
2 CONC , p. 249), of Maidenhead. By this last marriage there were fifteen children added to the first four (supra). HEZEKIAH BONHAM, SR. was living in 1732 although probably deceased before 1738, as indic., (ARCH., Vol. XXX, pp. 437 and 
2 CONC 232). He was a judge in Hunterdon County, 1723, as "Justice Hezekiah Bonham." (HUNTERDON, Snell, pp. 203 and 258). Soon after the death of his first wife, 1699, HEZEKIAH BONHAM removed to Maidenhead. Many records and intermarriage
2 CONC s, directly and indirectly prove the children, parentage and relationship. He gave the name Hezekiah into the Drake family, and they gave him Jeremiah and Ephraim.  Children of Judge Hezekiah Bonham, Sr.:  I. Mary Bonham, daughter
2 CONC  of Hezekiah Bonham, Senior, b. Oct. 4, 1691, married about 1712, Daniel Howell of Trenton, Hunterdon Co., New Jersey, Blacksmith, who died in 1732, leaving a will dated August 30, 1725. It names wife, Mary. (ARCH., Vol. XXX, p. 2
2 CONC 48).  II. Samuel Bonham, son of Hezekiah Bonham, Senior, b. Feb. 6, 1693, was presented in "Jersey Genealogy," (see ante, p. 581). Samuel Bonham was a creditor in the estate of John Heath of Amwell, Hunterdon Co., along with John 
2 CONC Severns, Philip Ringo, et al in the years, 1724-1729, (N. J. Arch., Vol. XXIII, p. 220). He was a debtor, 1732, to the estate of John Severns of Trenton, Hunterdon Co., together with Zedekiah Bonham, Jeremiah Bonham, and Nehemiah 
2 CONC Bonham, his brothers. Did he m. a Stout and have a son, Jacob Bonham? Yes, I think so.  III. Hannah Bonham, daughter of Hezekiah Bonham, Senior, b. Mar. 14, 1695, married Benjamin Stout, b. 1696, son of Jonathan 2 (Richard 1) Stou
2 CONC t and wife, Anne Bollen, (Stout Gen., p. 9).  Page 588 IV. Sarah Bonham, daughter of Hezekiah Bonham, Senior, b. Feb. 14, 1698, was intermarried with Thomas Runyon, Jr., lived in Hopewell, Hunterdon Co., adjoining the Bonham Famil
2 CONC y there. A son, Bonham Runyon joined the Hopewell Baptist Church Oct. 20, 1764, and was dismissed by letter 4-20-1794.  V. Hezekiah Bonham, Junior, son of Hezekiah Bonham, Senior, b. about 1701, the first son of his second wife an
2 CONC d number one of the "iah" Bonhams, was a citizen of Hopewell Township, Mercer County where in 1722, he is plainly listed as a Hopewell taxpayer. He m. Martha Runyon, dau. of Thomas Runyon, Sr.  VI. Nehemiah Bonham, son of Hezekiah
2 CONC  Bonham senior, b. about 1703, the second son of his second wife, and number two of the "iah" Bonhams was like his brother, Hezekiah Bonham, Junior, a citizen of Hopewell Township, Mercer County, where in 1722 he was plainly liste
2 CONC d as a Hopewell tax payer:  The first wife of Nehemiah Bonham was Ann Stout, b. 1704, who was the third daughter of Jonathan2 (Richard1) Stout, and wife, Anne Bollen (Stout Gen., p. 14). (See Hist. and Gen. Misc. by Dr. John E. St
2 CONC illwell, 1916, p. 332). However, Dr. Stillwell is in error in thinking that Anne (Stout) Bonham had a child when 60 years of age. He is confused because Nehemiah Bonham had a second wife named Elizabeth Martin, (dau. of Benjamin),
2 CONC  who bore him that daughter, Anne.  VII. Zachariah Bonham, son of Hezekiah, Senior (Bonham Genealogy) b. about 1709, married about 1729, Patience Ayers, (sister of Nathan Ayers), daughter of James and Phoebe Ayers.  Zachariah Bonh
2 CONC am married (2) to a daughter of Thomas Marlett, or else Zachariah Bonham married a daughter of Isaac Smalley, as a second wife.  VIII. Zedekiah Bonham, son of Hezekiah Bonham, Senior, born about 1707, was first established as to h
2 CONC is maturity in his relationship to the estate of Jonathan Compton of Woodbridge, who died in 1747, and left a will dated 1745, Sept. 3, naming a granddaughter Sarah Bonham. His first wife was Ann Martin, sister of Elizabeth, who m
2 CONC . his brother, Nehemiah Bonham. (2) Sara Compton.  IX. Amariah Bonham, son of Hezekiah Bonham Senior b. about 1709, was first at Piscataway, and then, later, of Amwell, Hunterdon County, m. prob. Drake.  X. Temperance Bonham, daug
2 CONC hter of Hezekiah Bonham Senior, was born at Maidenhead, in 1710, and married 1728, Zebulon Ayres, born 8-4-1708, son of James and Phebe Ayers, James Ayers lived in Woodbridge. (O. B. Leonard).  XI. Rev. Malachiah Bonham, son of He
2 CONC zekiah Bonham Senior, name broadened from the Biblical Malachi so as to add final syllable "ah," was definitely born 1713, for luckily a short account of him has been kept when a resident of Kingwood Township, Hunterdon County. Re
2 CONC ferring to the Baptist Church there--His successor was Malachi Bonham, who was ordained in 1749. He continued until Feb. 17, 1757. He died in 1789, aged seventy-six, ("hence born in 1713.") (Hist. of Hunterdon and Somerset Countie
2 CONC s, by James P. Snell, 1881, p. 397.)  His first wife was Jemima Harker (said by O. B. Leonard to have been Deborah) and by her, he had a daughter Zerviah, and two sons, Daniel and Absalom.  His second wife was the widow, Hannah Bu
2 CONC ckingham Heath; and the third, Mary Fox, either widow or daughter, Mary, (of GEORGE).  XII. Amaziah (or Amos), son of Hezekiah Bonham, Senior, born in 1713 at Maidenhead, "called Amos," m. in 1734, Rebecca Rittenhouse in Randolph 
2 CONC Township, Morris County, New Jersey.  XIII. Jeremiah Bonham, son of Hezekiah Bonham, Senior, b. in 1715, undoubtedly at Maidenhead, soon removed and became a well known settler at Shrewsbury.  XIV. Ephraim Bonham, son of Hezekiah 
2 CONC Bonham, Senior, was born in 1716, and died in 1785. He married Susannah Jones. (He also had a wife, Elizabeth Morris), and they had thirteen children, who drifted westward. (Bonham Genealogy).  i. Jane, b. Sept. 16, 1743.  ii. Wil
2 CONC liam, b. Feb. 4, 1745.  iii. Susannah, b. Dec. 4, 1746.  iv. Jane, b. Nov. 27, 1748.  v. William, b. Oct. 1, 1750, d. Oct. 3, 1828.  vi. Ephraim, b. Feb. 22, 1752.  vii. Mary, b. Jan. 25, 1754.  viii. Humphrey, b. April 25, 1755. 
2 CONC  ix. Sarah, b. June, 21, 1756.  x. Rebecca, b. Aug. 8, 1757.  xi. Susannah, b. Dec. 30, 1758.  xii. Mary, b. June 18, 1760.  xiii. Blethwaite, b. Nov. 16, 1762.  XV. Josiah Bonham, son of Hezekiah Bonham, Senior, (Bonham Genealogy
2 CONC .)  XVI. Isaiah Bonham, son of Hezekiah Bonham, Senior, b. about 1720, belongs here, without certainty as to his being identical with Obadiah (post).  XVII. Zephaniah, b. about 1722. Family went westward.  XVIII. Uriah Bonham, son
2 CONC  of Hezekiah Bonham, Senior, and probably the youngest child appears on the scene in a record of the estate of John Ruckman of Hunterdon Co., who left a will dated Dec. 24, 1749, naming wife Susannah and William Allen as executors
2 CONC ; sons and daughters mentioned but not named. To this will, Uriah Bonham was a witness.  XIX. Obadiah (Isaiah) Bonham, son of Hezekiah Bonham senior. (Bonham Genealogy) born in 1723 (?)  Alan R. Hunt who wrote "The Hunt Connection
2 CONC " said that Hezekiah Bonham, Sr., bought land in Maidenhead about 1712. He was a judge in Hunterdon Co., NJ during 1723. "Bonham and Related Family Lines" by H. E. Bonham 1996, says on page 229, that Hezekiah's second wife was Mar
2 CONC y Bishop, daughter of David Mary (Alger) Bishop according to the will of David Bishop 07 Jan 1684. Ann Hunt, daughter of Ralph O. Hunt listed by some as a wife to Hezekiah, was not a wife to Hezekiah Bonham, Sr. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes on Mary/Maria BISHOP who d. May 03, 1734: "BONHAM, and Related Family Lines" by Howard E. Bonham does not list these children: Josiah, Amaziah, Isaiah, Zephania, or Obadiah . Mr. Bonham, on page 81 of his book, says that the
2 CONC y are named as children by Smith, Monnette and Hazie in their books. Her parents are named in his book however. He gives her birth and death dates which were taken from a grave marker found exposed in a house foundation in Princet
2 CONC on some 20 years ago. On page 230, he says that Hezekiah married (2) in 1700, and in 1721, Samuel Hunt (father of Ann) willed to his daughter Ann Hunt a mare which indicated she was unmarried at that time. By 1721 Hezekiah had sev
2 CONC eral children born with his second wife. Page 117 in the Bonham book says that the probability of Mary Bishop, aged 51 in 1734, being the second wife of Hezekiah Bonham, Sr., is based upon study of Mary Alger who married David Bis
2 CONC hop 24 Mar 1679.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT 1683 - Mentioned in his father's Will.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1684 - Hezekiah was co-exector with his mother of his father's Will.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1691 - Named in Hugh Dunn's Will (New Jersey Archives, Vol XXIII, p. 145).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1695 - Received patent for 100 acres of land in Piscataway.  He also had other land transactions.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Piscataway Seventh Day Baptist Church was founded by Hezekiah as a result of a dispute with his sister Mary's husband, Rev. Edmund Dunham.
2 CONT
