2 CONT
2 CONT According to First Settlers of ye Plantations of Piscataway and Woodsridge Olde East New Jersey by Orra Monnette, Hezekiah Bonham, Sr. married Mary Dunn about 1690 in Piscataway, Middlesex County, New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT #Mary Bonham was born on October 4, 1691. 
2 CONT #Samuel Bonham was born on February 6, 1693. 
2 CONT #Hannah Bonham Stout was born on March 14, 1694/95. 
2 CONT #Sarah Bonham Runyan was born on February 14, 1697/98.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hezekiah received his patent for land in Piscataway on May 1, 1697.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1697 May 22. Deed. Hezekiah Bonham of Piscataway to Benjamen Fitsrendalph of the same place, for 12 acres, No. 1 of preceding confirmatory patent, S. grantee, W. and N. John Martine and town land, E. a small run. 377 (from Calenda
2 CONC r of Records in the Office of the Secretary of State. 1614-1703 by Berthold Fernow) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary Dunn Bonham died in 1699. 
2 CONT In 1698/99 Hezekiah joined with others in the purchase of Maidenhead (now Lawrenceville, Mercer County, New Jersey) and the family moved there soon after. About 1700 Hezekiah Bonham and a number of the members of the  Piscataway B
2 CONC aptist Church withdrew from that church and formed the Seventh Day Baptist Church, where they observed the seventh day of the week as the Sabbath. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Their plantation Hezekiah built was described in the November 8, 1764 issue of the Pennsylvania Gazette when Malakiah Bonham was attempting to sell it.
2 CONT
2 CONT A Valuable Plantation, lying in Kingwood, a very flourishing Part of Hunterdon County, West-Jersey, containing 200 Acres, 100 of Plough-land, and 15 Acres of Meadow cleared, and in good Repair; 
2 CONT
2 CONT two good bearing Orchards, containing upwards of 300 Trees, chiefly grafted, with excellent Fruit, Peach and other Fruit Trees, well timbered and watered, 
2 CONT
2 CONT 20 or 25 Acres more of good Meadow may be made, the Land good for Grain and English Pasture;
2 CONT
2 CONT there is a good new built Stone House, two Stories high, 31 by 24 Feet, an excellent Cellar, fit for any public Business, 
2 CONT
2 CONT a good framed and Log house,
2 CONT
2 CONT a good Stone Granary, with a Cellar under it, 
2 CONT
2 CONT a good Frame Barn, 36 by 24 Feet, 
2 CONT
2 CONT within one Mile and a Half of two good Grist-mills, lying in a very public Place, on a great Road leading to Philadelphia, about two miles from a Landing and excellent Fishery on the River Delaware, near divers Place of divine Wor
2 CONC ship.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Hezekiah, Sr. married again. His second wife's name is disputed by scholars. It could have been Ann Hunt, Mary Hune, or Mary Bishop. Mary is the most likely.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ann Hunt was the daughter of Samuel Hunt and Marry Burroughs. 
2 CONT Several sources have Ann Hunt as Hezekiah Bonham’s second wife. 
2 CONT The sources include Sons & Daughters of Pilgrims, Volume III, An Ancestral Chart and Handbook, and The Abridged Compendium of American Genealogy. 
2 CONT The Mayflower Society believes that Ann was not his wife and that the idea may have originated in a misinterpretation of the will of Samuel Hunt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Genealogists have said that Hezekiah and his second wife had as many as fifteen children. 
2 CONT Their children were all born in Maidenhead. 
2 CONT #Hezekiah Bonham, Jr. was born about 1701. 
2 CONT #Nehemiah Bonham was born about 1703. 
2 CONT #Zachariah Bonham was born about 1707. 
2 CONT #Zedekiah Bonham was born about 1707 
2 CONT #Amariah Bonham was born about 1709. 
2 CONT #Temperance Bonham Ayers was born about 1710. 
2 CONT #The Reverend Malachiah Bonham was born about 1713. 
2 CONT #Amaziah Bonham was born about 1713. 
2 CONT #Jeremiah Bonham was born about 1715. 
2 CONT #Ephriam Bonham was born about 1716. 
2 CONT #Josiah Bonham was born about 1717
2 CONT #Isaiah Bonham was born about 1720. 
2 CONT #Zephaniah Bonham was born about 1722. 
2 CONT #Uriah Bonham was born about 1724. 
2 CONT #Obadiah Bonham was born about 1725.
2 CONT
2 CONT The early New Jersey families were tightly linked and intermarried for several generations. 
2 CONT The Bonham family was closely associated with the Runyan family. 
2 CONT They were all members of the First Baptist Church of Piscataway. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two of Hezekiah's children married into the Jonathan Stout family. 
2 CONT Nehemiah married Ann Stout and Hannah married Benjamin Stout. Ethel Stroupe wrote that "The first white man in Hopewell was Jonathan Stout who in 1685 explored the wilderness from his parents' home in Middletown, lived several yea
2 CONC rs at Wissamonson with the Indians, then returned home."
2 CONT
2 CONT Two of Hezekiah's son married into the Benjamin Martin family. 
2 CONT Zedekiah and Nehemiah married Ann and Elizabeth Martin. 
2 CONT The Seventh Day Baptist Church first gathered at the Martin home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two of Hezekiah's children married into the Ayers family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Two of Hezekiah's descendants married into the George Fox family. 
2 CONT Absalom's son Amariah's daughter, Christian married Absalom Fox and Uriah married George's daughter, Anchor Fox. Nehemiah was a witness to George Fox’s will in 1754.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hezekiah was living in 1732 and probably died before 1738.
2 CONT Burial site unknown. Should be buried with his 2nd wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT ALL NOTES FROM AN AMERICAN FAMILY HISTORY
2 CONT Kingwood Township is on the western border of Hunterdon County, New Jersey. It was founded in 1798.
2 CONT
2 CONT A land patent is an exclusive land grant made by the government. The certificate that grants the land rights is also called first-title deed and final certificate. In the United States, all land can be traced back to the original 
2 CONC land patent.
2 CONT
2 CONT The First Baptist Church of Piscataway is in Edison, New Jersey and was formed in 1689. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hunterdon County originally included all or parts of Mercer, Morris, Warren, and Sussex Counties.
2 CONT
2 CONT Baptist churches were found in early colonial settlements and grew out of the English Separatist movement and the doctrine of John Smyth who rejected infant baptism.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hopewell is currently in Mercer (formerly Hunterdon) County, New Jersey. Mercer County was formed in 1838 from portions of other counties including Hunterdon. 
2 CONT Early settlers found that their deeds were worthless and they were forced to repurchase their land or relocate. 
2 CONT On April 23, 1715 the settlers who stayed organized Hopewell Baptist Church.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/129065784/hezekiah-bonham Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartC
