1 NOTE John left Piscataway, N.J. in 1715 for the vicinity of Rocky Hill, Somerset
2 CONT Co., N.J. In 1745 interstate of John Runyon of Somerset Co., N.J. Freeman
2 CONT Runyon & Mary Runyon were both mentioned. First Settler Families says that
2 CONT in some vital statistics, he is recorded as "John Vincent, alias Runyon."
2 CONT John & Ann Elizabeth's children are uncertain. First Settler Families P 627
2 CONT says he died 1745 & called "Of Rocky Hill, Somerset Co., yeoman". He &
2 CONT his father John are confussing. Marie Wright also says he d. Oct Or Nov 1745.
1 NOTE John left Piscataway, N.J. in 1715 for the vicinity of Rocky Hill, Somerset Co., N.J. In 1745 interstate of John Runyon of Somerset Co., N.J. Freeman Runyon & Mary Runyon were both mentioned. First Settler Families says that in so
2 CONC me vital statistics, he is recorded as "John Vincent, alias Runyon." John & Ann Elizabeth's children are uncertain. First Settler Families P 627 says he died 1745 & called "Of Rocky Hill, Somerset Co., yeoman". He & his father Joh
2 CONC n are confussing. Marie Wright also says he d. Oct Or Nov 1745.
1 NOTE !SOURCE:  BONHAM, a family Genealogy by Howard E. Bonham, p. 237-239.
1 NOTE In 1722, John & his brother Thomas settled near Hopewell, in East Amwell, Hunterdon Co., N.J. M.C. Shepherd says John & Elizabeth (Dunn) left Piscataway about 1715 for the vicinity of Rock Hill, Somerset Co., N.J., but I think it 
2 CONC may have been his son John & Elizabeth (Freeman). John owned the Glen Moore Mills. The mills had been owned for some time by the Blackwell family. N J Colonial Documents say John & Elizabeth married 20 Jan 1693. The Beginnings of 
2 CONC Three New Jersey Family Lines says John md. age 23 in '92/3.
1 NOTE !SOURCE:  BONHAM, a family Genealogy by Howard E. Bonham, p. 540.
