1 NOTE !SOURCE:  BONHAM, a family Genealogy by Howard E. Bonham, p. 237-239.
1 NOTE Taken from:
2 CONT
2 CONT Family History 
2 CONT (As related to the Shephard's of Hunterdon County, NJ) 
2 CONT By: Russell S. Pickett
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.russpickett.com/family/runyan.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT START:
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas, (4) was born the 3rd child & 3rd son of Vincent (1) and Ann (Boutcher) Runyon in ca. 1673 in Elizabeth, NJ. He married "Martha Dunn" prior to 1698 and it is believed that they were married in Piscataway, NJ. Martha was bor
2 CONC n July 13, 1681 in Piscataway, NJ. the daughter of Hugh and Elizabeth (Drake) Dunn (See the "Drake" Family History by Russ Pickett). Martha's sister Elizabeth married Thomas' (4) brother John (2). 
2 CONT In 1698 Thomas (4) & his brother John (2) signed a compact with many others to build a church above the falls of the Delaware (Scudders Falls at Trenton). The land was traded for land at Maidenhead, now Lawrenceville, & a church w
2 CONC as built there. It was a Presbyterian Church & it is not known at this time whether or not it still stands. Thomas (4) & his wife Martha, however, joined the Hopewell Baptist Church prior to 1730 & that is the only record of them 
2 CONC joining any church. The above compact is in the Colonial Archives in Trenton. Both Thomas (4) & his brother John (2) signed with their marks so evidently neither could write. 
2 CONT By 1700 they had moved to Hopewell, NJ. There they purchased 150 acres of land from Daniel Coxe on March 5, 1712 for 50 pounds (English money). The land is on the Titus Mill Road just below Hopewell & was described in the deed as 
2 CONC being next to William Hixson, by Stoney Brook, & next to Captain Hunt. It was witnessed by J. Basse, Edward Kemp & Alex Lockhart. The record of this transaction is in the Burlington County (Hunterdon Cty was part of Burlington Cty
2 CONC  in 1712) Book AAA, page 445 in the State Archives in Trenton. (Note: The original copy of this deed was found in Pennington, NJ in a mans personal effects after his death during 1991. It was given to Marvin C. Shephard ( see "Mar
2 CONC vin Shepherd" in the "Shepherd Family History" ) who presented it to the Museum in Hopewell, NJ. He also sent this author (Russ Pickett) a copy of the original). 
2 CONT They built their house, which is still standing, in 1713. Thomas (4) died in 1753 & is probably buried in the Hopewell Baptist Church Cemetery (no marker has been found). There is proof that his wife Martha was still alive at that
2 CONC  time, however, no records have been found to establish the date of her death. They had 7 children. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Family Notes:   
2 CONT
2 CONT All the children were born in or near Hopewell, NJ.   Vincent (9) married 1st "Alice Curtiss" & then 2nd "Mercy Bonham".   He had only one child, Vincent, Jr.   Vincent (9) died in Hopewell.   His son, Vincent, Jr. married "Elizab
2 CONC eth Woolverton" & moved to New York in 1800 after selling the remainder of the lands of Thomas I (4).   Catherine (11) married "Cornelius Anderson" & had many children.   A record of her descendants is available as part of the And
2 CONC erson genealogy records in the Hopewell, Museum.   Martha (12) married "Hezekiah Bonham" & the record of Martha's descendants can be found in the Bonham genealogy records which are available in Hunterdon & Mercer County.   Ephraim
2 CONC  (13) married "A____ Houghton" & was a logger & was killed in a wagon accident in 1772.   He & his wife had three daughters, of which I have no record.   Joseph (15) Lived in Amwell Cty after his marriage (unknown to who) & had tw
2 CONC o sons, Peter & John.   John, it is known, remained a bachelor. 
2 CONT
2 CONT END:
2 CONT
2 CONT Following taken from:
2 CONT
2 CONT RUNYAN - RUNYON - RUNNION - RUNYEON
2 CONT Entries: 868    Updated: Sat Apr 27 23:55:02 2002    Contact: Hazel M. Sheehan  
2 CONT
2 CONT START:
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: In 1698, John and Thomas' signatures appear on a documentsetting aside land for a church and burial ground in what is todayLawrenceville, NJ.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: A copy of Thomas Runyan's original deed to land in Hopewell,Hunterdon County, NJ is dated 5 Mar.1712. The land was purchased fromDaniel Coxe and consisted of 140-150 acres of Daniel Coxe's original30,000 acre land grant in W
2 CONC est Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth: ABT 1675 in Elizabethtown, Middlesex County, NJ 
2 CONT Note: 
2 CONT Note: In the Hopewell Town records:
2 CONT 31 Oct.1722 - Township meeting at house of Thomas Runion.
2 CONT Mar.1728 - Thomas Rugnion, Overseer of Rogers Road. (A road betweenHopewell and Trenton.)
2 CONT
2 CONT APR 1753 in Hopewell Township, Hunterdon County, NJ 
2 CONT Event: From Daniel Coxe land grant Deeds 1713 Hopewell Twp., Hunterdon County, NJ 
2 CONT Note: NOTE: In possesson of copy of original deed. 
2 CONT Event: Account of Thomas Runyan's estate Adm. 1753 Hopewell Township, Hunterdon County, NJ 
2 CONT Note: Note: The administration of Thomas Runyan's estate names his children.Copy in folder. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: In the Hopewell Baptist Church Records:
2 CONT 1730 - added to baptism - Thomas Runyan
2 CONT Between 1715-1728 - Martha Runyan, new member.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: It is believed that Thomas and Martha are probably buried inHunt's Burial ground in Hopewell. Wilson Hunt was the richest man inHopewell at that time. He established a private burial ground andgave his close friends and thei
2 CONC r families, an invitation to be buriedthere. He helped inventory the estate of Thomas Runyan, so it isconsidered probable that Thomas and Martha are buried there. Only tenpercent of the tombstones can be read today. Thomas and Mar
2 CONC tha are notamong those that are legible.
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTE: His probate account is dated April 1753. His will names his wifeMartha, and two sons,
2 CONT Vinson (sic) and Thomas. The estate inventory names all of his sonsand the husbands of his two daughters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Children's data from Kephart.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT END:
