2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Joseph Runyon 
2 CONT *   b: ~1704 - Hopewell Twp., Burlington Co., NJ 
2 CONT *   d: 1780
2 CONT * Father: Thomas Runyon 
2 CONT * Mother: Martha Dunn
2 CONT * Spouse: unknown 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Children 
2 CONT * 1: Isaac?  https://www.geni.com/people/Isaac-Runyon/6000000006110701135
2 CONT *           2: Peter - b: 5/Nov/1740                 d: 1793 - Hunterdon Co., NJ                m: Catherine Quick  (Peter should not be here, he was son of other Joseph whose father was named peter)  
2 CONT *           3: John - b: 4/Apr/1747 
2 CONT *           4: Henry - b: 1749 
2 CONT *           5: William - b: 1751 
2 CONT *           6: Elijah - b: 1757
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Biography 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://web.pdx.edu/~davide/gene/Runyon_Joseph_2.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT >Joseph Runyon was a son of Thomas and Martha Dunn Runyon and was born about 1704 in Hopewell Township, Hunterdon (later Mercer) County, New Jersey.  It is not known who Joseph's wife was although her first name has been reported 
2 CONC as "Rachel".  Alternatively, some researcers believe that she was Elizabeth Trembley.  However, Howard E. Bonham presents rather convincing evidence that this is not so and that Elizabeth Trembley was married to Joseph's cousin, a
2 CONC lso named Joseph Runyon, son of Peter and Providence Blackford Runyon, who was born April 1, 1710, and died March 16, 1760, leaving underage children.1,2  Concomitantly, only two children, Peter and John, can be reliably attribute
2 CONC d to Joseph, son of Thomas.  Nevertheless, a number of researchers believe that he may have had as many as four more sons, viz., Isaac, Henry, William, and Elijah (as well as having additonal likely spurious children attributed to
2 CONC  him); however, there is no definite confirmation of this.3  It is also reported that Joseph Runyon served in the Continental Army and was taken prisoner by the British.  Again, there is no known documentary support of this assert
2 CONC ion and, moreover, if Joseph was born in 1704, he would have been more than seventy years old at the time of the Revolutionary War; hence, it seems quite unlikely that he would have served.  Likewise, Joseph Runyon reportedly move
2 CONC d to Frederick County, Virginia, where he died in 1780.  Indeed, individuals having the surname "Runyon" were numerous in the Shenandoah Valley during the late eighteenth century and afterward; however, family lineages remain uncl
2 CONC ear and it is likely that Joseph's identity has been conflated with someone else (although, perhaps, a relative).  Concomitantly, it is known that Joseph's putative son, Isaac, moved from New Jersey to Frederick County, Maryland. 
2 CONC  Accordingly, it would seem more likely that Joseph would have also moved to Maryland instead of Virginia; however, it is perhaps even more likely that he never left New Jersey."
2 CONT
2 CONT http://web.pdx.edu/~davide/gene/Runyon_Joseph_2.htm
2 CONT
