1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *Not a spouse of https://www.geni.com/people/Elizabeth-Fitzrandolph/6000000009543990724?through=6000000000462278237
2 CONT
2 CONT -There are no sources documenting that his middle name was Benjamin, or if he had a middle name.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.historyispersonal.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/12/Salyers-Family-History-PDF.pdf
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT Zachariah Benjamin Salyer died when a "mountain cat" dropped from a tree onto him and his horse in the Mountains of Chowan County, NC. He owned large tracts of land in western North Carolina, including King's Mountain, where the a
2 CONC  Battle of the Revolution War was fought October 1780. He was a shoemaker, trader, and a farmer.
2 CONT —-
2 CONT Zachariah Benjamin Salyer, Sr. was born January 24, 1730 in New Jersey to Charles Salyer (Sallier), Jr. (1697-1757) and Rebecca Bloomfield (1686-1770).
2 CONT
2 CONT Zachariah married Katherine Howard before 1750 and had a son, Thomas Howard who was born in 1750.
2 CONT
2 CONT Zachariah married Elizabeth Dunn (1735-1799) in 1751 in Edenton, Burke, North Caroliina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Known children are: Isaiah Benjamin (Still) Salyer, Sr. (1752-1818)(Note, at one point in history, Isaiah dropped the name Salyer and called himself Isaiah &quot;Still&quot; and he is listed this way in some historical documents.)
2 CONC , Martin Dunn Salyer (1756-1843), John Salyer (1760-1843), Benjamin Salyer, Sr. (1762-1825), Zachariah Benjamin Salyer, Jr. (1765-1845), Elizabeth Salyer (1768-1831), Sarah Sally Salyer (1770-1855), Rebecca Salyer Kilgore (1770-18
2 CONC 60) and Temparence Tempy Salyer (1773-1860).
2 CONT
2 CONT Zachariah and his children owned much land in Russell County, Virginia. More information on him can be obtained in the book, &quot;The Salyer Family&quot; by Elisabeth L. W. Salyer which is located in the Historical Section in the
2 CONC  Russell County Library in Lebanon, Virginia. Bless her heart, Elisabeth did an awesome job on documenting her ancestors.
2 CONT —-
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT The Chowan County Court Records (Pre-1755)The earliest legal records of Zachariah Salyer in North Carolina are court dockets from Chowan County.The Record: He was taken to court for the non-payment of debts.The Context: Court tran
2 CONC scripts show he was sentenced to six months in prison for failing to pay, but he fled the county before serving the sentence. Records from this time note his occupation as a "cordwainer" (shoemaker) and mention he lived on a sloop
2 CONC  named Peggy.
2 CONT The North Carolina General Assembly Petitions (1779)This is the most critical civil document linking Zachariah to his specific geographic location during the Revolutionary War.The Record: Series: General Assembly; Box: Oct - Nov 1
2 CONC 779, held at the North Carolina State Archives (Folder: Petitions; Page 2; Family No. 7).The Context: Zachariah Salyer signed a regional legislative petition in Burke County/Tryon County, North Carolina.
2 CONT Land Deeds and the "Martin Dunn" ConnectionIn genealogy, when a formal marriage certificate is lost, a mother's maiden name is legally proven through land deeds, wills, and naming patterns.The Legal Naming Proof: Zachariah and Eli
2 CONC zabeth named their second son Martin Dunn Salyer (born c. 1757). In the 18th century, using a spouse's maiden name as a child's middle name was a binding legal tradition used to preserve family bloodlines and protect future proper
2 CONC ty inheritances from the maternal grandfather's estate.The Land Records: Later deeds in Russell County, Virginia, explicitly link Zachariah’s children (Isaiah, Martin Dunn, John, and Zachariah Jr.) back to Zachariah Sr.’s estate a
2 CONC nd land holdings.
