2 DATE 25 DEC 1630
2 PLAC Stickford, Lincolnshire, England
2 ADDR
1 OCCU He was approved Sergeant in 1681. Nicholas named the town of Bonhamtown in Woodbridge Township, Middlesex County, New Jersey.    Bonhamtown, five miles northeast from New Brunswick, was named for Nicholas Bonham, who was one of th
2 CONC e first settlers, having b
2 DATE 1681
1 DEAT
2 DATE 20 JUL 1684
2 ADDR
3 CITY Piscataway
3 STAE Province of East Jersey
1 BURI
2 DATE 23 JUL 1684
2 PLAC Piscataway, Middlesex, New Jersey, United States
2 ADDR
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Disputed as son of https://www.geni.com/people/George-Bonham-the-Immigrant/6000000012944460009
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Nicholas Bonham===
2 CONT * b.1630 England, or  Barnstable, Barnstable, Massachusetts
2 CONT * d.20 Jul 1684 Piscataway, Middlesex, New Jersey
2 CONT * Wife: Hannah Fuller (daughter of Samuel Fuller & Jane Lathrop)
2 CONT * 10 children: Hannah, Mary, Sarah, Elizabeth, Nicholas, Elijah, Hezekiah, Samuel, Jane, and Priscilla Bonham.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===First Settler of Piscataway===
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a first settler of Piscataway. He signed "Oath of Allegiance," 1672, and was approved Sergeant, 1681. Dutch Schepen, 1673. He received his patent for land, May 1, 1697, in right of his late father, Ezekiah Bonham (error for
2 CONC  Nicholas Bonham) etc. (ARCH. Vol. XXI, pp. 268 and 281). He gave the name to Bonhamtown, vidoe of Bonhamtown, 5/11/2014   http://youtu.be/QnNdRYifKc8  Woodbridge Township, Middlesex County, New Jersey, which is five miles northea
2 CONC st from New Brunswick and is so preserved to this day. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Events:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Will; 6 Feb 1682/83. His will was proved 4 Jan. 1684/5, but witnesses testified on 18 Dec. 1694. His wife Hannah and son Hezekiah were named executors; mention was made of other children ( not named); witnesses included Edward 
2 CONC Slater and Isaac Smalley who were also overseers and took the estate inventory on 28 July 1684.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Information:
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas married Hannah FULLER, daughter of Samuel FULLER and Jane LOTHROP, on 1 Jan 1658/59 in Barnstable, Barnstable Co., MA 1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8 9. (Hannah FULLER was born about 1636 in Scituate, Plymouth Co., MA 10 and died about 2
2 CONC 3 Aug 1686 in Piscataway, Middlesex Co., NJ 10.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Notes:
2 CONT
2 CONT Of the sons, only Hezekiah Bonham survived or remained in Piscataway, to either perpetuate the name or family, and he soon removed to Maidenhead.
2 CONT
2 CONT They moved to Piscataway, New Jersey, thus bringing the Edward Fuller line there at an early date.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===comments===
2 CONT
2 CONT PLEASE DO NOT MERGE THIS PROFILE
2 CONT
2 CONT Update 24/9/2011  Nicholas Bonham was admitted citizen on 3 Oct 1662 because he was "son of a present inhabitant" and no other Bonhams are on record except George. 
2 CONT
2 CONT No age has been given for No Plymouth scholar or "authority" has found proof he was George's son. Seems unlikely but they may not be related. Nicholas would have been only 5 years old without a mother. This would pose the question
2 CONC ; did his mother die on the voyage? What happened to Miss Bishop his supposed mother. No mention of her in English or Plymouth records have been presented. Only that she was gone before  his marriage to Morton
2 CONT
2 CONT Date and place of birth have also been (erroneously?) reported to be:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1630/1631 in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barnstable,_Massachusetts Barnstable], [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barnstable_County,_Massachusetts Barnstable], Massachusetts
2 CONT * 1632 in [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scituate,_Massachusetts Scituate], [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plymouth_County,_Massachusetts Plymouth], Massachusetts
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources===
2 CONT
