1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Elizabeth Bonham
2 CONT * Birthdate: abt 1665  of Barnstable, Barnstable, Massachusetts (no record found)
2 CONT * Death:	after 1716 at Piscataway, Middlesex, New Jersey
2 CONT * Daughter of Nicholas Bonham and Hannah Bonham 
2 CONT * Wife of 1) Edward Slater (married 9-8-1684/5 at Piscataway) 2) Rev. John Drake about 1703
2 CONT * Mother of Philoreta Martin; Phoebe Doty; Caleb Slater; Aleeshia Skibbo; Elizabeth Hull
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes
2 CONT *Elizabeth was a lineal descendant (grand daughter) of Mayflower passenger Edward Fuller.
2 CONT * She was the second wife of https://www.geni.com/people/Rev-John-Drake-of-Piscataway/6000000003180896504 Her children were named in his 1740 will.
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.anamericanfamilyhistory.com/Bonham%20Family/BonhamElizabethSlater.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Bonham Slater was born about 1665, Barnstable, Massachusetts. Her parents were Nicholas Bonham and Hannah Fuller.
2 CONT
2 CONT She married Edward Slater in Piscataway. Edward was born about 1644.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward had been put in prison in 1681 for speaking out against the governor. The History of Middlesex County, New Jersey states that Edward Slater was a freeholder in the township in 1683, and owned a large number of acres. He was
2 CONC  the first clerk of the courts of the county.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth and Edward's children included:
2 CONT # Filoretta Slater (1691, married Benjamin Martin), 
2 CONT # Phebe Slater (1693, married James Doty), 
2 CONT # Caleb Slater (1695), 
2 CONT # Elizabeth Slater (1697), 
2 CONT # Abraham Slater (1700), and 
2 CONT # Aleeshia Slater (1702, married Henry Skibbo).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1699/1700, Elizabeth's sister Priscilla's husband, John Langstaff, and Edward Slater were involved in anti-English, pre-revolutionary activities. The court wanted to hold proceedings in the Piscataway Public house. The townspeo
2 CONC ple were opposed to this and nailed the door shut. The sheriff attempted to break the door open and John and Edward stopped him forcibly. One account of this incident is below and another is on the Priscilla and John Langstaff pag
2 CONC e.
2 CONT
2 CONT The inventory of the estate of Edward Slater was made on February 11, 1702/03 was made by George Drake and Thomas Grub. It amounted to £94.6.10.''' On may 29, 1704 administration was granted to his widow, Elizabeth Slater.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT John Drake married, in 1677, Rebecca Trotter, as his first wife. She was the daughter of one of the original associates of Elizabethtown, N.J. who came originally from Newbury, Massachusetts. Pastor Drake had, by this marriage, fo
2 CONC urteen children. Rebecca (Trotter) Drake died about 1700, probably following the birth of Mary, her last child. He married second, about 1703/4, Elizabeth (Bonham) Slater, widow of Edward Slater, and daughter of Nicholas & Hannah 
2 CONC (Fuller) Bonham.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT 1740, April 7. DRAKE, JOHN SEN'R, of Essex Co., yeoman; will of. Children: Benjamin, Isaac, Abraham, Samuel and Sarah Fulson. Grandchildren: Abraham, John, Philip, and Gershom Drake; Samuel, Thomas and Mary Davis, children of dtr 
2 CONC Mary dec'd. Dau-in-law Patience Drake. Edward Slater, Allizhiah Skebbo, Elizabeth (wife of Benjamin Hull, Esq), Filiratea (wife of Benjamin Martain), Moses FitzRandolph, Christian Rebout, the poor of Piscataqua. Exrs: grandsons Sa
2 CONC muel and Jonas Drake, and their mother, Hannah Drake. Wit: James Manning, Grace Manning, David Drake. Pr. Sept. 29, 1741 Lib. C p. 442
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
