2 CONT ----
2 CONT Benajah Dunham was the next to the last child of eight children born to Deacon John Dunham and his second wife Abigail Ballou.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Plymouth, Mass. 1640, Benajah Dunham was a freeman in 1664, a court officer at Eastham, Mass., in 1669, emigrated to East Jersey about 1671; in 1672 purchased 100 acres of land at Piscataway, N. J., and became a planter an
2 CONC d in 1673 a captain of the Piscataway Militia Squad in New Jersey under the Dutch.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benajah married October 25, 1660, Elizabeth Tilson, of Scituate MA. Shortly after Benajah & Elizabeth’s marriage, the young couple moved from Plymouth to Eastham Massachusetts (on Cape Cod). They stayed here for eight or nine year
2 CONC s but by 1671 they had relocated to Piscataway, New Jersey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Between 1661-1671, Benajah 2 and Elizabeth had 3 sons and 4 daughters. Their first born son Edmund Dunham moved to the new town of Piscataway along with several other Eastham families.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benajah died in Piscataway, New Jersey 24 December 1680.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==family==
2 CONT
2 CONT From [http://www.anamericanfamilyhistory.com/Bonham%20Family/BonhamMaryDunham.html#BenjahDunham An American Family History] citing "Dunham Genealogy: English and American Branches of the Dunham Family by Isaac Watson Dunham"
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjah [Dunham] b. Plymouth, 1640; m. Oct. 25, 1660. Elizabeth Tilson, of Scituate, dau. of Edmund, of Plymouth. He bought, in 1672, one hundred acres of land in Piscataway, N. J., and became a planter; 1664, a freeman; 1669. a co
2 CONC urt officer at Eastham; 1673. militia captain; May 10, 1679, willed to son. Edmund, seventy acres of land on Raritan river; to Mary, house and meadow on Bonham Creek, and one-third of cattle, movable goods and books; to his wife, 
2 CONC one-third of cattle, movable goods and books; to Elizabeth, forty acres of land, and one-third of cattle, movable goods and books: d. Dec. 24. 1680, at Piscataway, N. J.
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue:
2 CONT * I.—Edmund, b. July 25, 1661; m. July 15, 1681, Mary Bonham; b. Oct. 4, 1661; d. March 17, 1734. 
2 CONT * II.— John, b. Aug. 28, 1663; d. Sept. 6, 1663. 
2 CONT * III.—Elizabeth, b. Nov. 20, 1664; d. Dec. 31, 1667. 
2 CONT * IV.—Mary, b. New Jersey, 1669; m. Mr. Thompson. Had son, John. 
2 CONT * V.—Elizabeth, b. 1670, New Jersey; m. July 15, 1681, Jonas Wood. 
2 CONT * VI.—Hannah, b. June 4, 1666; d. Dec. 25, 1667. 
2 CONT * VII.—Benjamin, b. Oct. 28, 1667; baptized at Eastham, by Pastor Russell; d. young.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==notes==
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1672 emigrated with his family & bought 100 acres of land in Piscataway, N.J. where he established his family. The first mention of him in Plymouth Colony records was of a complaint brought against him on March 5, 1656 for 'foo
2 CONC lish & provoking carriages, in drawing his knife upon sundry person at Taunton, which might have proved of ill consequences.' He denied the charges & it was dismissed due to lack of evidence. Made a freeman on June 8, 1664 (Eastha
2 CONC m, MA.) & served on inquest panels on Sept. 20, 1667 & Dec. 24, 1667. Commisioned Captain of Militia May 10, 1679.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT He m. Elizabeth Tilson, of Scituate, Oct. 25, 1660. His occupation was similar to that of his father, a linen weaver. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He accompanied his brother, Daniel, to Eastham, in 1668. He became a court officer in 1669. Here they separated and in 1672 followed Jonathan, the son of Thomas his brother, and settled in Piscataway, New Jersey, south of Woodbrid
2 CONC ge, the home of Jonathan Dunham. He purchased 100 acres of land and became a planter. He took an active interest in the affairs of the colony and in 1673 was appointed captain of the militia, and as such 
2 CONT was one of the committee of safety and council of war. It was under this board that Jonathan Singletary, from Haverhill, who had assumed the name of Jonathan Dunham, was condemned for his vagrancy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT This Jonathan (Singletary) Dunham is an ancestor of President Barack Obama.
2 CONT
