1 NAME Sarah /Budd/
2 GIVN Sarah
2 SURN Budd
2 _MARNM Dunham
1 SEX F
1 BURI
2 PLAC Andrew Cemetery, Andrew, Jackson County, Iowa, USA
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1 JAN 1816
2 PLAC Brookfield Township, Trumbull County, Ohio, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 10 JUL 1889
2 PLAC Fairfield Township, Jackson County, Iowa, USA
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Sarah was born just west of Mercer Co., PA., where her family was originally from.  On Sept. 26, 1833, she married James Clark DUNHAM, a cousin of Justus DUNHAM (FindAGrave memorial #104227111), husband of her olde
2 CONC r sister Eliza (Budd) DUNHAM (FindAGrave memorial #106030368).  Sarah's paternal ancestry is:
2 CONT
2 CONT John BUDD (1785 – 1863, m’d Eunice Riggs [1785 – 1853]), son of
2 CONT William BUDD (1755 – 1805, m’d Sichy Scofield), son of 
2 CONT Daniel BUDD (1722 – 1806, m’d Mary Purdy [1728 – 1801]), son of
2 CONT John BUDD (1697 – 1756, m’d Mary Prudence Strang [1695/99 – 1745/54]), son of
2 CONT Capt. Joseph BUDD (1669 – 1722, m’d Sarah Underhill [1678 – 1755]), son of
2 CONT John BUDD (1625 – 1684, m’d Mary Horton), son of
2 CONT Lt. John BUDD (1599 – 1669) & Katherine Browne (1591 – 1674), from England
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah's maternal ancestry is:
2 CONT Eunice Riggs (1785-1853, m’d John BUDD [1785-1863]), daughter of
2 CONT Jonathan RIGGS (1745-1836, m’d Mary Hampton [1745-1791]), son of
2 CONT Thomas RIGGS (1692-1789, m’d Eunice Morris [1703-1789]), son of
2 CONT Edward RIGGS (1640-1696, m’d Mary Munn [1640-1685]), son of
2 CONT Edward RIGGS (1618-1669, m’d Elizabeth Roosa [1617-1680]), son of
2 CONT Edward RIGGS (1589-1671) & Elizabeth Holmes (1592-1635), from England
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah (Budd) DUNHAM would bear seven children (five surviving).  The family lived for several years (c.1834-48) in Hickory Township (today’s Town of Hermitage), Mercer Co., PA., before moving to nearby Sharon, seat of Mercer Count
2 CONC y (c.1848-49).  In about 1850 – not long after her sister Eliza & family had moved 1,100 miles west to northeastern Iowa – James & Sarah joined them with their growing family.  Their daughter (Cordelia, b. Dec. 29, 1838), then 11 
2 CONC years old, later recalled the tedious, 3-week journey by wagon team.  
2 CONT
2 CONT James & Sarah’s youngest child was born in 1852 in Jackson County, Iowa, where James had bought 200 acres of “wild land” in Fairfield Township, that he “improved” (turned into a farm) near today’s hamlet of Spragueville.  An 1879 
2 CONC history of Jackson County notes that, for 17 years after he came to the area, James “ran the ferry over the Maquoketa River, known all over the county as Dunham’s Ferry.  He served his township for many years in different capaciti
2 CONC es” including – starting in 1859 – 26 years as Justice of the Peace, and was elected several times as Township Trustee, School Director and Road Supervisor.  Honored by locals with the title “Squire”, he was respected as “a man of
2 CONC  sterling worth” and known as  a “staunch old Jacksonian Democrat” in politics, and a member of the local Baptist church. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Three of his & Sarah’s sons would serve in the Civil War (with one dying at Vicksburg), while their only daughter Cordelia graduated from Normal School (teacher-training academy) at age 15, then taught school for 7 years, before s
2 CONC he was married in Aug. 1860 in Jackson Township to the future-Captain John DOWNING.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the Civil War, Sarah and James moved to the town of Andrew, Jackson Co., Iowa, where Cordelia’s parents also moved and lived out the rest of their lives.  Sarah probably died and James is known to have died ("of general debi
2 CONC lity”) at the home of their daughter & son-in-law John & Cordelia DOWNING in Jackson Co., Iowa.* Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/15733153/sarah-dunham Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCo
2 CONC py/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Mar 17 2022, 23:43:21 UTC''
