2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald MECHLING was born about 1701 in Germany.
2 CONT
2 CONT He emigrated on 11 Sept 1728 to Philadelphia, Pennsylvania. He died in Apr., 1765 in Northampton Co., Pennsylvania. He was buried in Apr 1765 in old graveyard near Dillingersville, Pennsylvania. He has Ancestral File number LJ27-Q
2 CONC N.
2 CONT
2 CONT BIOGRAPHY:  MECHLING MECHLIN MACKLIN 1728-1989 Volume I COMPILER:Dorothy Hallary Mercy
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald Mechling and his brother Jacob arrived in Philadelphia on 11Sept., 1728 aboard the s hip "James Goodwill" David Crockett master.Since they were numbered among the 90 some Palatin es aboard it haswidely been believed that 
2 CONC the Mechling brothers came from thePalatinate whic h is the southwestern part of Germany. Agreat-great-grandson gives credence to this theory li sting their homeas being in Rheinfels Bavaria which today is part of Baden-Wurtenberg
2 CONC on the R hine River.  However, since Dewalt, eldest son of Theobald,named two of his properties in Wes tmoreland Co., Pennsylvania"Mechlinburg" and "Strelitz" respectively - place names in theprov ince of Mecklenburg in the northe
2 CONC astern part of Germany, doubt hasbeen cast upon the Palatina te origin.  Also the Palatinates weremostly Catholic while Mechlinburg was mostly Luthera n - the religionof the Mechlings.  Only further research will determine their c
2 CONC orrectorigin .  The "James Goodwill" sailed from Rotterdam, Holland to Deal,England where on 15 June 172 8 it left for America.  Aboard were 42 menabove the age of 16 comprising 37 families.  Theoba ld's name isspelled as Teobald 
2 CONC Mekeling on the ship's manifest.  Upon arrival inPhiladelphi a they went before the Provincial Council to declare theirintent to settle peacefully in thi s country.  They then signed adeclaration of allegiance to King George II of
2 CONC  England. Theoba ldsigned his name with the letter "D" for Deobald or Dewalt - the Germanspelling.  Theobald a nd Jacob went to Germantown (which was at thattime a separate city from Philadelphia) where t hey engaged in thetannery
2 CONC  business for a few years.  It is quite likely that an oldermember o f the family was already living in Germantown then, and also,buried in Germantown is a Mari a Mechlin wife of Samuel who died 18June 1759 aged 61 years 2 months 
2 CONC and 4 days.  As there i s no record ofa Samuel Mechlin in Germantown at that time one can suppose that thisSamuel ha d preceded the Mechling brothers to Germantown probablyestablishing the tannery business whic h may have attracte
2 CONC d them toGermantown in the first place.  The name Samuel is carried down i n theMechling family.  Nothing definite is known about Theobald for thenext few years but app arently he married some time during that periodto Anna Elizab
2 CONC eth Lauer, daughter of John Pete r and Anna MargaretLauer.  In 1731 his eldest son, Dewalt, was born presumably inGermantown .  In 1734 he obtained a grant of 125 acres in Bucks Countyfrom Thomas and Richard Penn, son s of William
2 CONC  Penn and proprietors ofthe province of Pennsylvania.  According to the record o f land patentswarrants and purchases filed in Bucks County, Theobald secured land asfollows:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT June 29, 1734 - warrant for 125 acres (an estimate)
2 CONT
2 CONT June 12, 1737 - 144 acres and 120 perches surveyed
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan. 22, 1744 - warrant for 20 acres
2 CONT
2 CONT July 17, 1744 - 23 acres and 18 perches surveyed
2 CONT
2 CONT Apr. 29, 1747 - warrant for 11 acres
2 CONT
2 CONT May 5, 1747 - 11 acres and 18 perches surveyed
2 CONT
2 CONT Sept. 16, 1757 - 17 acres and 116 perches surveyed
2 CONT
2 CONT Oct.  6, 1757 - warrant for 25 acres
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Theobald moved to this land in Bucks County and built a log cabinwhich is still standing no w incorporated into the body of a muchlarger house on the property near Zionsville.  The orig inal log cabinis now the middle room in this
2 CONC  house with the original fireplace. Thehouse ha s been placed on the Pennsylvania Inventory of HistoricalPlaces as being one of the few pre-R evolutionary houses in that area.The log cabin was located near a stream called Mechling
2 CONC 's Cr eek (laterWalter's Creek).  A dam was erected across the stream and a millbuilt.  In 1735 th e Provincial Council ordered a road built to becalled the King's Highway.  It passed close t o the Mechling cabin. In1737 Theobald 
2 CONC signed a petition to have Upper Milford district inwhi ch his property was located assigned to a separate region which wasfinally done in 1752 whe n Northampton County was formed.  It becamepart of Lehigh County in 1812.  He was o
2 CONC ne of th e founders of theUpper Milford Lutheran Church donating 30 acres of land for the siteof the c hurch and a burial ground.  His donation of fifteen shillingstowards the pastor's salary in t he years 1756, 1757, 1758 and 175
2 CONC 9made him one of the three largest contributors. But by 1757 differences of opinion arose in the congregation and several membersincluding Theobald brok e away and started a new congregation some twomiles away in the vicinity of p
2 CONC resent day Zions ville. Nothing isknown concerning any active military participation on the part ofTheobald b ut there is one brief item in Pennsylvania Archives, Series5,  Volume 1, page 222, which i s a report of the constable s
2 CONC ent topatrol the frontier during the French and Indian War and w hichmentions that Dewalt Mechlin of Upper Milford township had a horsereturned.  The report i s dated June 10, 1758.  He died in April 1765leaving a will which was s
2 CONC igned Davold Mechlin a nd his mark "D". Heis buried along with his wife Anna Elizabeth (the death date notknown) i n the old graveyard near Dillingersville.  The graveyard isstill preserved but the Mechling s tones have long since
2 CONC  vanished.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT REFERENCES: History of Lehigh County Pennsylvania by Roberts;Pennsylvania Archives - Pennsylv ania German Society; Church Records ofUpper Milford
2 CONT
