2 DATE 15 OCT 1909
2 PLAC Bethesda, Montgomery County, Maryland, USA
2 ADDR
1 TITL Brev. Brig. Gen. (USA)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Richard Coulter''' Drum (May 28, 1825 – October 15, 1909) was Adjutant General of the United States Army from 1880 to 1889. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Early life''' <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Greensburg, Pennsylvania, and graduated from Jefferson College before entering the printing business.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the outbreak of the Mexican–American War in 1846, he enlisted as a private in the 1st Pennsylvania Volunteers, and, the next year, was commissioned a second lieutenant in the 9th Infantry of the Regular Army. He took part in ma
2 CONC ny of the battles of the war, including Chapultepec and Mexico City.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the war, he was transferred to the Fourth Artillery and was promoted to first lieutenant. He served as an aide-de-camp for General William S. Harney during his expedition against the Sioux, and participated in the Battle
2 CONC  of Ash Hollow.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Civil War''' <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1861 he was appointed as an assistant adjutant general for the far-flung Department of the Pacific and promoted to captain. He was quickly promoted to major and lieutenant colonel, and after the end of the American Civil War he
2 CONC  was brevetted Colonel and brigadier general, for faithful and meritorious services in the Adjutant-General's Department during the war.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Drum was a companion of the District of Columbia Commandery of the Military Order of the Loyal Legion of the United States - a prestigious military society composed of officers of the Union armed forces and their descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Among other honors, Camp Drum, a 60-acre army base in Wilmington, CA, was established in 1862 and named after him.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Later service''' <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT Drum was Assistant Adjutant General for the Department of California from June 1865 to October 1, 1866. He was appointed Assistant Adjutant General for the Department of the East, with Headquarters in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania fr
2 CONC om December 27, 1866, to January 6, 1868. He was appointed Assistant Adjutant-General, for the 3rd Military District to August 1, 1868, and Department of the South, headquarters Atlanta, Georgia from March 20, 1868 to March 20, 18
2 CONC 69,[2] and from April 3, 1869 to November 26, 1873 for the Military Department of the Atlantic.
2 CONT
2 CONT Drum was promoted to Colonel in February 22, 1869. He was again made Assistant Adjutant-General, for the Military Division of the Missouri, in Chicago from November 28, 1873 to May 2, 1878, where he commanded the Federal troops in
2 CONC  the Great Railroad Strike of 1877. He then served until retirement in the Adjutant Generals office in Washington, D.C.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT On June 16, 1880, he became Adjutant General of the United States Army and was promoted to brigadier general. He retired on May 20, 1889.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Death''' <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT General Drum died in Drummond, Maryland in 1909. He is buried in Arlington National Cemetery.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Memberships''' <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1871 he became a member of the Aztec Club of 1847, a military society for officers who served in the Mexican War and their descendants. In 1896 he became a Veteran Companion of the Military Order of Foreign Wars by right of his
2 CONC  service in the Mexican–American War. He was also a Companion of the District of Columbia Commandery of the Military Order of the Loyal Legion of the United States. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_C._Drum
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT Civil War Union Brevet Brigadier General. He enlisted in the United States Army on December 8, 1846, to fight in the Mexican War, and was mustered in as a Private in the 1st Pennsylvania Volunteers. Two months later in February 18
2 CONC 47 he was commissioned as as 2nd Lieutenant in the 9th United States Regular Infantry. He was engaged at the March 1847 Siege of Vera Cruz, and in the march to Mexico City that ended the war. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In March 1848 he was transferred to the 4th United States Regular artillery. In September 1855 he was serving as an aide to Brigadier General William S. Harney when United States forces attacked a Sioux Indiana settlement at Ash H
2 CONC ollow, Nebraska, and fought an hours long battle that resulted in 86 Sioux casualties, half of which were women and children (other Army officers who were present at the fight were future Civil War generals Henry Heth, Gouverneur 
2 CONC K. Warren, Philip S. Cooke, William S. Steele, Albemarle Cady and Beverly Robertson). 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1856 he was appointed as acting Assistant Adjutant General at the headquarters of the Army Department of the West. At the beginning of the Civil War, he was appointed Assistant Adjutant General of the United States Army's Depar
2 CONC tment of the Pacific. He was promoted Captain on May 14, 1861, Major on August 3, 1861 and Lieutenant Colonel on July 17, 1862. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was brevetted Brigadier General, United States Regular Army on March 13, 1865 for "faithful and meritorious services in the Adjutant General's Department during the war". He continued service in the Army after the war, rising t
2 CONC o Brigadier General in 18880. He served as Adjutant General of the Army until his retirement on May 28, 1889.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/9731624/richard-coulter-drum Find A Grave Memorial] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Sep 2 2019, 3:04:30 UTC''
2 CONT
