1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Transcribed from: “'''Biographical Record of Linn County Iowa”''', published 1901 by S. J. Clarke Publishing Co. pages 791-793 FLOWER M. PHILLIPS Prominent among the successful agriculturists and highly esteemed ci
2 CONC tizen Jackson Township is numbered the gentleman whose name introduces this sketch.  He is the owner of a valuable and well-improved farm on section 5, where he has made his home for forty-four years.  He was born on the 13th of m
2 CONC arch, 1832, in Richland, Oswego county, New York, and is a son of Moses S. and Charlotte [Ransom] Phillips, both natives of the Green Mountain state.  The father was born in Bennington county, Vermont, and was a son of Elihu Phill
2 CONC ips, who spent the last of his life in that state, engaged in agricultural pursuits. Our subject’s maternal grandfather was Samuel Ransom, who after the marriage of his daughter removed to Richland, New York, where his life was te
2 CONC rminated. About 1825 Moses S. Phillips removed from Vermont to New York, and took up his residence in Richland, Oswego county, where he purchased a tract of timber land and improved a farm.  He erected a good log house and barn, p
2 CONC lanted an orchard and established a comfortable home, but at the end of ten years he sold that place and bought another tract of timber land about one mile from the village of Pulaski, on the margin of a stream called Spring Brook
2 CONC .   This place was remarkably pleasant in its location and surroundings, and in the course of time he transformed it into a good farm.  On disposing of this property in 1845 Mr. Phillips removed to Illinois and settled in Lake cou
2 CONC nty, about five miles from Waukegan.  Upon this land was the old New York House, a hotel located on the Milwaukee road, and he conducted the same until death which occurred in 1848.  He was a very popular landlord, and met with su
2 CONC ccess in this enterprise.  Although his career as such was very brief, he made many friends and became widely known throughout northern Illinois and southern Wisconsin.  His widow subsequently became the wife of Seeley Whitman, an
2 CONC d after his death married E. Townsend, of Cook county, Illinois, where she made home until her death. The subject of this sketch is the third in order of birth in a family of seven children.  The others who reached manhood womanho
2 CONC od were Cyrus S., a resident of Tecumseh, John county, Nebraska; Clarissa P., who first married Lora Tripp and after his death wedded John S. Carrington, and now lives in Johnson county, Nebraska; Elizabeth D., who became the wife
2 CONC  of William Sanders, and died in Chicago, Illinois; Ransom, who was a member of an Illinois regiment in the Civil War and died in the service, his remains being interred in the cemetery at Bloomington, Illinois; and Martha D., wif
2 CONC e of Spencer Trumbull, of Main township, Linn county Iowa. Mr. Phillips, of this review, was thirteen years of age when he accompanied his parents on their removal to Illinois, and he remained at home with his mother until ninetee
2 CONC n, after which he worked for a neighboring farmer.  During the succeeding winter he lived with his mother Walworth county, Wisconsin, and in the spring returned to Lake county, Illinois, remaining there until 1857.  He had been re
2 CONC ared to habits of industry and economy, and from his small earnings he had already saved quite a little sum of money.  Believing he could do better still farther west, he came to Linn county, Iowa in 1857, and took possession of h
2 CONC is farm of one hundred sixty acres of land, eighty acres on section 5, Jackson township, and eighty acres adjoining, but across the line in Delaware county, which he had entered in 1854.  To the improvement and cultivation he at o
2 CONC nce turned his attention, first erecting a house on section 5, in Jackson Township.  From the beginning he met with success here, although the results were necessarily slow and difficult to accomplish.  Acre after acre was placed 
2 CONC under the plow until the entire amount was under a high state of cultivation, and as the years have passed he has added to his landed possessions until he now owns three hundred and twenty acres in his home farm on Section 5, Jack
2 CONC son township, Linn county, and one hundred acres of timber and pasture land on the Nopsu, three miles south.  His first home here was long ago replaced by amore commodious and pretentious dwelling, and the barns and outbuldings ar
2 CONC e in perfect accord with his beautiful home, while everything about the place indicates the thrift and progressive spirit of the owner. On the 23rd of December, 1858, Mr. Phillips was united in marriage with Miss Mary E. Hutchinso
2 CONC n, who was born in Tompkins county, New York, September 14, 1836, a daughter of Alvah and Margaret [Mitchell] Hutchinson.  Her father was a native of Connecticut, from which state he removed to New York, residing first in Tompkins
2 CONC  county and later in Steuben county.  Subsequently he went to Illinois, and died in Lake county, that state, in September, 1857.  After his death his widow made her home with Mr. and Mrs. Phillips in this county.  Five children we
2 CONC re born to our subject and wife, namely:  (1) Holmes died at the age of two years, (2) Margaret is the wife of William G. Andrews, of Smith county, Kansas and they have seven children.  (3) Mary is the wife H.I. Patten, a hardware
2 CONC  merchant of Fullerton, Nebraska, and they have one child, Alva P. (4) Carrie is the wife of M.F. Hollingsworth, who is teaching in the public schools of Palo, Iowa, and they have two children, Esther and Francis.  (5) Flower S. m
2 CONC arried Annie J. King and resides on the old homestead with our subject. In political affiliations of Mr. Phillips has been a Republican since casting his first presidential vote for John C. Fremont in 1856, and has always taken a 
2 CONC very active interest in politics.  He is what the world terms a self-made man, -- one whose success in due entirely to his own unaided efforts.  Thoroughness and persistency have characterized his entire business career, and have 
2 CONC been supplemented by a careful attention to details and by honorable, straightforward efforts, that have gained for him a most excellent and enviable reputation, as well as a handsome competence.  He and his wife receive and merit
2 CONC  the high regard of the entire community, and no citizen s in the township are more honored or respected.
