0 @N6000000000337357782@ NOTE Ibid:
1 CONT
1 CONT It will be of interest to learn a little as to the brothers and sisters of Mistress Thomas Stanton nee Lord. They were all born across the sea, and as follows: 
1 CONT
1 CONT Richard, b. 1611; Thomas, Jr., b. 1619 (Ann, who m. Thos. Stanton, b. 1621); William, b. 1623; John, b. 1624; Robert, b. 1626; Aymie, b. 1629; Dorothy, b. 1630. 
1 CONT
1 CONT As this book is not a Lord Genealogy, I have made no effort to follow the fortunes of these brothers and sisters. A few facts, however, as to the senior brother Richard have an historic value. He was a merchant in Hartford, and wa
1 CONC s one of the body corporate to whom King Charles granted the Connecticut charter, April 20, 1662. He seemed to be of rather a pugnacious disposition, for on Oct. 12, 1643, he engaged his brother-in-law, Thomas Stanton, in a quarre
1 CONC l about trading for Indian corn. He used very threatening language, and drew his sword, but before he could use it he was arrested and fined five pounds by the Hartford Court. It would be interesting to know how Mr. Stanton conduc
1 CONC ted himself in this little broil, but we only know that the Court Record makes no case against him. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1658 the first troop of horsemen in Conn. was organized, and Richard Lord was chosen captain. He died in New London, May 17, 1662, and was buried in the old burial-place there. A table of red sandstone covers his grave, cracked
1 CONC  and sunken below the surrounding soil; it is, nevertheless, still there; probably the oldest inscribed tombstone east of the Connecticut river. The inscription is legible, and reads thus: 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT AN EPITAPH ON CAPTAINE RICHARD LORD, DECEASED.
1 CONT MAY 17, 1662, ’TATIS SV. ’ 51.
1 CONT            "The bright starre of our cavallrie lyes here. 
1 CONT             Unto the state a counselore full deare 
1 CONT             And to ye truth a friend of sweete content. 
1 CONT             To Hartford Towne a silver ornament. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT            "Who can deny to Poore he was releife 
1 CONT             And in composing paroxysmes was cheife. 
1 CONT             To Marchants as a patterne he might stand 
1 CONT             Adventring dangers new by sea and land." 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT After the death of Mr. Stanton his widow lived with the family of her daughter, Dorothy, who married Rev. James Noyes of Stonington. She lived eleven years longer and died in 1688, aged 67 years. In this day people are not old unt
1 CONC il they are 70, but in those days, after 42 years of a pioneer's hardships, we may presume that this worthy couple were regarded as having lived to a good old age. They were husband and wife for 40 years, and the parents of ten ch
1 CONC ildren, all of whom lived to have children of their own. 
1 CONT
1 CONT  It will be of interest to members of the family having the historie spirit, and who may at any time be in Hartford, Conn., to know that the site now occupied by Jewell's Leather Belting Co. and covered with their large factory bu
1 CONC ildings on Ford street was in 1639 the location of the first home of Thomas and Ann (Lord) Stanton. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Source #2 IBID Goodwin (see father)
1 CONT
1 CONT ANN LORD was married to Thomas Stanton, first of Hartford, Conn., afterwards of Stonington, in the same State, about 1637. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr. Thomas Stanton, died in 1678. 
1 CONT
1 CONT His widow, Mrs. Ann Stanton, died in 1688. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Children.
1 CONT 72 Thomas,        born about         1638.  Settled in Stonington. Married and 
1 CONT                                               had a family of children. Died April 
1 CONT                                               11, 1718, aged 80. 
1 CONT 73 John,          born about         1641.  Settled in Stonington. Married and 
1 CONT                                               had several children. Was a captain 
1 CONT                                               in Philip's war. Died October 
1 CONT                                               3, 1713, aged 72. 
1 CONT 74 Mary,          born                      Married Samuel Rogers, November 17, 
1 CONT                                               1662. 
1 CONT
1 CONT (*) At a Session of the General Court, this 11th day of October, 1648. Present, Mr. Hopkins, 
1 CONT Governor, Mr. Ludlow, Deputy,--several Magistrates, and Deputies. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "Thomas Lord was called upon for selling lead to an Indian, and he is to answor the next 
1 CONT Courte." 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT At a Session of the General Court in Hartford, the 30th of June, 1652. Present, John 
1 CONT Haynes, Esq., Deputy Governor, and the several Magistrates and Deputies. "Thomas 
1 CONT Lord, having ingaged to this Courte to continue his abode in Hartford, for the next ensuing 
1 CONT yeare, and to improve his best skill amongst the inhabitants of the Townes uppon the 
1 CONT River within this Jurissdiction, both for setting of bones and otherwise, as at all times 
1 CONT occasions or necessityes may or shall require; This Court doth graunt that hee shall bee 
1 CONT paid by the Country the sum of fifteene pounds for the said ensuing yeare, and they dee?? 
1 CONT declare that for every visitt or journye that he shall take or make, being sent for to any 
1 CONT house in Hartford, twelve pence is reasonable; to any house in Wyndsor, five shillings; to 
1 CONT any house in Wethersfield, three shillings; to any house in Farmington, six shillings; to 
1 CONT any house in Mattabeseck, eight shillings; (he having promised that he will require no 
1 CONT more;) and that hee?? shall be freed for the time aforesaid from watching, warding and training; 
1 CONT but not from finding armes, according to lawe." 
1 CONT
1 CONT 75 Hannah,        born                      Married Nehemiah Palmer, son of Walter 
1 CONT                                               Palmer, November 20, 166
1 CONT                                               Nehemiah Palmer, Esq., died February 
1 CONT                                               17, 1718. His widow, Mrs. Hannah 
1 CONT                                               Palmer, died October 17, 1727. 
1 CONT                                               They had several children. 
1 CONT 76 Joseph,        bap. March     21, 1646,  at Hartford. Three children are recorded 
1 CONT                                               to him on the Records of Westerly, 
1 CONT                                               R. I. 
1 CONT 77 David,         born                      Named in a list of "Free Inhabitants," 
1 CONT                                               of Westerly, May 18, 1669. Died at 
1 CONT                                               Barbadoes shortly after 1681. 
1 CONT 78 Dorathy,       born               1652.  Married Rev. James Noyes, minister of 
1 CONT                                               Stonington, September 11, 1674. He 
1 CONT                                               was son of Rev. James Noyes, of 
1 CONT                                               Newberry, Mass. Rev. James Noyes 
1 CONT                                               died December 30, 1719, in his 80th 
1 CONT                                               year. His widow, Mrs. Dorathy 
1 CONT                                               Noyes, died January 19, 1743, in her 
1 CONT                                               91st year. A number of children 
1 CONT                                               are recorded to them on Stonington 
1 CONT                                               records. 
1 CONT 79 Robert,        born               1653.  Settled in Stonington. Married Joanna 
1 CONT                                               Gardner, September 12, 1677. He 
1 CONT                                               died October 25, 1724, aged 71. Nine 
1 CONT                                               children are recorded to them on 
1 CONT                                               Stonington records. 
1 CONT 80 Samuel,        born                      Married Boradell Denison, daughter of 
1 CONT                                               Capt. George Denison, and of Anna 
1 CONT                                               [Boradell] Denison, his wife, June 
1 CONT                                               15, 1680. Children are recorded to 
1 CONT                                               them. 
1 CONT 81 Sarah,         born about         1654.  Married William Denison. She died 
1 CONT                                               in 1713, aged 59. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
