0 @N6000000001678863117@ NOTE _P_CCINFO 1-4016
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources:  From the internet.
1 CONT Abbrev: Graves, Kenneth Vance
1 CONT Title: Thomas Graves of Hartford, Conneticut and Hatfield, Massach usetts
1 CONT Author: Kenneth Vance Graves
1 CONT
1 CONT Publication: (Wrentham, Massachusetts, Family History Publications, 198
1 CONT Abbrev: Graves, John Card
1 CONT Title: Genealogy of the Family of Thomas Graves of Hartford, Conne ctic ut andHatfield,Massachusetts
1 CONT Author: John Card Graves
1 CONT
1 CONT Publication: (Buffalo, New York, Baker, Jones & Company, 1896)
1 CONT Abbrev: History of Hatfield Massachusetts
1 CONT Title: A History of Hatfield Massachusetts
1 CONT Author: Wells, Daniel White and Wells, Reuben Field
1 CONT
1 CONT Publication: (Springfield, Massachusetts, F. C. H. Gibbons, 1910)
0 @N6000000003927581858@ NOTE Thomas Graves was born 6 June 1605 at Ratcliff, England, near London (or at Stepney, England), was baptized 16 June 1605 at Stepney, part of the city of London, and died 31 July 1653 (31st day, 5th month, 1653, old style calendar)
1 CONC  in a sea battle with the Dutch in the English Channel. (A number of sources say he died in Charlestown, MA, but this definitely appears incorrect; e.g., Tracy Genealogy, p. 177, and NEHGS Register.) His will was dated 13 June 165
1 CONC 2 and probated 1 Oct. 1653. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Graves sprang from a prominent shipbuilding and mariner family of London, and came to America as early as 1628. He was a skillful shipmaster, and was mate of the Talbot, in which Higginson came to Salem, Mass. in 1629. He w
1 CONC as mate of the Arabella, master of the Plantation in 1630, master of the Plough in 1631, of the Whale in 1632, of the Reformation and Elizabeth Bonadventura in 1633 and 1634, and of the James in 1635. He was master of the first Am
1 CONC erican built ship, the Tryall, on her second voyage in June 1643, and followed the sea more or less until his death. He brought his family (consisting of his wife and two children) to America about 1636 or 1637 and settled in Char
1 CONC lestown, Mass., probably at the same time that his wife's mother came. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He married Katherine (or Katharine or Catharine) Gray before 1635 (probably by 1630) in England. She was born about 1606 at Harwich, England, died 21 Feb. 1682 (21,12, 1681 in old style calendar), and was buried 23 Feb. 1681/2. Sh
1 CONC e was a daughter of Thomas Gray and Katherine Myles (daughter and co-heir of Robert Myles, of Sutton, in County Suffolk). Thomas Gray died in 1607, and his widow married on 23 Dec. 1610 at Harwick, England, Rowland Coytemore, of W
1 CONC apping (next to London, in Middlesex), a widower, who died in 1626; she came to New England in 1636 or 1637 and settled in Charlestown, Mass.; was admitted to the church in 1638, and died 28 Nov. 1659. Her will, dated 28 or 30 Apr
1 CONC il 1658 named 14 grandchildren, including the 5 children of her daughter Katherine. An article in the NEHGS Register gives much more information on the Myles family, and also says that Katherine (Myles) Gray had 6 children. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Katherine Coytemore, mother of Thomas Graves' wife, with her son Thomas and wife and her daughter Elizabeth, also came to America, probably at the same time as the family of Thomas Graves. The son, Thomas Coytemore, was a mariner 
1 CONC and commanded the Tryall on her first trip. He was lost on the coast of Spain 27 Dec. 1644, and his widow Martha, daughter of Captain William Rainsborough, a mariner of England, married 4 Dec. 1647 at Boston as her second husband 
1 CONC Governor John Withrop who died 26 March 1649. She married thirdly John Coggan of Boston on 10 March 1652. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Graves was admitted to the First Church, Charlestown, with his wife, 7 Oct. 1639, and was made freeman at general court, Boston, on 13 May 1640. He became possessed of a large amount of land in Charlestown and Woburn. (Wobu
1 CONC rn is next to and just south of Wilmington.) He continued to follow the sea, and while in command of a merchantman captured a Dutch "man-of-war" vessel in the British Channel, for which gallant act he was appointed by Parliament c
1 CONC aptain of the frigate President in the Royal Navy on 30 May 1652. The following year he was appointed rear admiral of the White, and on board the St. Andrew (360 men and 56 guns) he participated in the naval battles with the Dutch
1 CONC , and was killed 31 July 1653. His body was landed from the fleet in Aldborough Bay, Co. Suffolk, England, 8 Aug. 1653, and buried the same day, his wife's family being from that county. Parliament granted his family 1000 pounds. 
1 CONC His will dated 13 June 1652, deposited with his brother Abraham in England, was forwarded to America. It was presented for probate by his widow and proved on the first of the tenth month of 1653 (1 Dec. 1653), several months after
1 CONC  his death. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas is said to have been commissioned Rear Admiral in the British Navy by Oliver Cromwell, after the overthrow of King Charles I. Previous to that event he had been in America and laid plans for his future abode here, by securi
1 CONC ng, for some service rendered the colony, a grant of 250 acres of land located in that part of what was then Charlestown, now near Wilmington, called the "land of nod". (This seems strange, since Wilmington is nowhere near present
1 CONC  day Charlestown. It is much farther north of Boston, next to Billerica.) For some years he commanded a vessel running between Boston and the ports of the mother country. By this means he kept up his acquaintance with men and affa
1 CONC irs in England. He was on familiar terms with the principal men of the Mass. Colony, and was held by them in high esteem, being pronounced by Gov. Winthrop after his death in 1653, "an able and godly man". Winthrop wrote his name 
1 CONC Greaves. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Will of Thomas Graves of Charlestown: This my last will and testament is that I do bequeath unto my beloved wife Katharine Graves, my now dwelling house scittuate in Charlestowne in New England for the terme of her life, and also 
1 CONC the one-third part of all my goods, shiping & moneys and plate, I give and bequeath unto my daughter Rebecca five pound, & to her child three pound, I do bequeath unto my sonne John my house scittuate in Limehouse neere Dickes sho
1 CONC are, he paying One hundred Pound, to be payd the one third part of it to his mother my beloved wife, the other two thirds to be divided unto my sonne Thomas, Nathaniel, Susanna & Joseph, my will is that my Land at Oburne in New En
1 CONC gland, and Land in Charlestowne neck, and what goods, household stuffe, chipping, plate or moneys I have be all vallued, with one hundred Pound, I Appoynt my sonne John to pay of all with my will is that my wife shall have the one
1 CONC  third part of, And the other two thirds to be Equally divided between my sonne Thomas, Nathaniel, Susanna & Joseph, but my desire is, that if the house at Limehouse which I do bequeath unto my sonne John he paying one hundred pou
1 CONC nd be not vallued to be worth as much as double what wilbe of my estate dividable to my sonne Thomas, Nathaniel, Susanna or Joseph, that then my sonne John shall pay lesse to be dividable as above mentioned, for my desire is that 
1 CONC my son John should have twice as much as the rest of my children. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Further I do bequeath unto my sonne Thomas after his mother's decease, my now dwelling house, with all the garden & orchard there unto belonging, which is scittuate in Charlestowne in New England, provided that my sonne Thomas pay
1 CONC  unto his sister Rebecca ten pound, Nathaniell Ten pound, Susanna ten pound, Joseph Ten pound. I do Appoynt my beloved wife and my sonne John to be my Executors and do appoint them to pay to the church at Charlestowne forty shilli
1 CONC ngs And I do intreate my Brother Nowell & my Brother Lindes of Charlestown to be overseers of my will, and do give to them Twenty shillings apeece, witnes my hand this 13th of June 1652. Thomas Graves 
1 CONT
1 CONT This last will of me Thomas Graves being left by himselfe sealed up, and delivered to his Brother Abraham Graves and presented by his relict widow before the Deputy Governer, and the rest of the magistrates, it was by them allowed
1 CONC  & Approved the first of the 10th month 1653. Thomas Danforth, Recorder 
1 CONT
1 CONT Entred & Recorded 25 10 mo. 1655. Thomas Danforth, Recorder. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Rear Admiral Thomas Graves has sometimes been confused with the engineer of the same name. Thomas Graves, the engineer was of Gravesend, County of Kent, England, signed a contract with the Massachusetts Bay Company in March 1629, 
1 CONC arrived in New England in July 1629, and laid out Charlestown. Thomas Graves, the engineer, was clearly not the same as Rear Admiral Thomas Graves. In 1629 the engineer already had an impressive reputation for engineering, had tra
1 CONC veled extensively, and had a wife and five children. He was older than the 24 years of age that the Rear Admiral was at that time. In addition, the signature of the Rear Admiral from his will, and that of the engineer from his con
1 CONC tract, as shown below, are different (from History of Charlestown, page 140, by.Richard Frothingham). 
0 @N6000000004548336263@ NOTE From Ancestry.com World Tree Project - Ancestry of Bob and Mary Beth Wheeler - Updated:  Aug 19, 2001    Contact: Mary Beth Wheeler <mb@thewheelers.com>:
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas Graves came to America with his wife and 5 children before 1645. The youngest son was about 16 yrs. of age. He first appears in Hartford, CT 1645 where he owned 3 pieces of land, one described as being one "whereupon his ho
1 CONC use standeth." Thomas was freed from training, watching and warding 1 Dec. 1645, likely meaning that he had reached age 60.
1 CONT
1 CONT As a result of religious dissension within the community, Thomas and others, known as "Withdrawers," religious conservatives, decided to leave Hartford and move to the wilderness up the Connecticut Valley to what became Hadley, MA
1 CONC . Thomas, his aged wife, sons Isaac & John and their families of 5 children each, arrived there about October, 1661. The family of Nathaniel Dickinson from Wethersfield was also among this group.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas died at Hadley in November, 1662, (in an area which became incorporated as Hatfield) a little over a year after making this move; he was then past his 76th birthday. He and his wife had lived with son Isaac during his time 
1 CONC in Hatfield. Son Isaac was appointed administer of his estate in MA, while son John performed the same service for his CT property which he had not sold before his departure. His wife survived him for about 4 years.
