0 @N6000000006348228254@ NOTE Edward Aylsworth Perry was a general in the Confederate Army during the Civil War.
1 CONT
1 CONT Pre-War Profession Lawyer
1 CONT War Service 1861 Capt. in 2nd Florida, May 1862 Col., Frayser's Farm (w), August 1862 Brig. Gen., commanded Perry’s (Florida) Brigade/Anderson's Divn at Chancellorsville, Gettysburg, Wilderness (w), reserve duty in Alabama.
1 CONT Post War Career Lawyer, governor of Florida.
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward Perry was born in Richmond, Mass., on March 15, 1831. He attended Yale University; taught briefly in Alabama; and took up residence in Pensacola, where he was admitted to the practice of law in 1853. He fought with distinct
1 CONC ion in the Confederate army. Perry was twice wounded and rose in rank from private to brigadier general.
1 CONT
1 CONT During Governor Perry’s administration, Florida adopted a new constitution and established the state board of education to support public schools. At the end of his term, he returned to Pensacola, where he died on October 15, 1889
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Massachusetts Town Birth Records
1 CONT Name: Edward Aylsworth Perry
1 CONT Birth Date: 3 Jul 1831
1 CONT Birth Place: Richmond
1 CONT Father's First Name: Asa
1 CONT Gender: Male
1 CONT Source: Vital Records of Richmond
1 CONT
1 CONT Brigadier-General Edward Aylesworth Perry
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Brigadier-General Edward Aylesworth Perry was born in Richmond, Berkshire county, Mass., March 15, 1833.  He entered Yale college, but before the completion of his course removed to Alabama, where he studied law.
1 CONT
1 CONT After admission to the bar, in 1857, he moved to Pensacola, Fla., where he began the practice of his chosen profession. He fully shared the sentiments of the people of his adopted State, and when the civil war commenced he raised 
1 CONC a company, of which he was elected captain.
1 CONT
1 CONT His command became a part of the Second Florida regiment, of which Captain Perry became colonel in May, 1862.  The regiment was sent to Virginia and was attached to the division of Gen. James Longstreet.  Colonel Perry commanded t
1 CONC he regiment at Seven Pines and in the Seven Days' battles around Richmond, and from the first the regiment and its commander were conspicuous for valor and efficiency.
1 CONT
1 CONT At Frayser's Farm he was severely wounded.  General Longstreet mentions him among others as distinguished for gallantry and skill.  He was commissioned brigadier-general on August 28, 1862, and upon his recovery was put in command
1 CONC  of the newly organized Florida brigade, which he led at Chancellorsville.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the battle of Gettysburg Perry's brigade, with Wright's and Wilcox's, pressed close up to the Federal lines, and at one time broke through; but for lack of support had to be withdrawn from the advanced position.  It is claimed 
1 CONC by Perry's brigade that its losses at Gettysburg were heavier than those of any other brigade of the Confederate army.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the battle of the Wilderness General Perry was a second time severely wounded.
1 CONT
1 CONT After the close of the war he returned to the practice of law in the city of Pensacola.  During the gloomy period of reconstruction he remained true to the interests of the people of Florida.  The result of the war had not changed
1 CONC  his views of the constitutional rights of the States, and not even by silence did he give seeming approval to the corrupt methods or usurpations of that epoch.
1 CONT
1 CONT He took an active interest in political affairs, and in 1884 he was elected governor of Florida on the Democratic ticket. This office he held for four years, and within a year after the expiration of his term he died, October 15, 
1 CONC 1889.
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:  Confederate Military History, vol. XVI, p. 207
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Fourteenth governor
1 CONT January 7, 1885 to January 8, 1889
1 CONT
1 CONT Edward Perry was born in Richmond, Mass., on March 15, 1831. He attended Yale University; taught briefly in Alabama; and took up residence in Pensacola, where he was admitted to the practice of law in 1853. He fought with distinct
1 CONC ion in the Confederate army. Perry was twice wounded and rose in rank from private to brigadier general.
1 CONT
1 CONT During Governor Perry?s administration, Florida adopted a new constitution and established the state board of education to support public schools. At the end of his term, he returned to Pensacola, where he died on October 15, 1889
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Source:
1 CONT http://www.flheritage.com/museum/collections/governors/about.cfm?id=21
