0 @N6000000002339097580@ NOTE When just a mere boy, Frederick left Maine and went to California withthe gold rush to work and seek his fortune in the gold mines.  He worked in a sawmill.  He and a partner got some money together and handled horses for the Army
1 CONC .  He was also a poney express rider.  
1 CONT
1 CONT He started back on his way to Maine, but his partner wound up with all the money.    He got as far as Iowa and went to work for a farmer named Phelps who had two daughters.  "As the story goes", one daughter fell in love with him,
1 CONC  but he didn't care for her and eloped with her younger sister Lucy, who was only 14.  The older sister became so upset that she went insane.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick started a small general store.  While hauling green sawed lumber, a bridge (English Bridge) collapsed under the strain of his four-horse team.  The wagon fell on him and crushed both of his legs.  One did not heal proper
1 CONC ly and it was evntually amputated.  He died May 1, 1889 in Caloma, Iowa of blood poisoning which developed after the amputation.[CRONE Family-1.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT [CRONE Family-1.FTW.GED.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT When just a mere boy, Frederick left Maine and went to California withthe gold rush to work and seek his fortune in the gold mines.  He worked in a sawmill.  He and a partner got some money together and handled horses for the Army
1 CONC .  He was also a poney express rider.  
1 CONT
1 CONT He started back on his way to Maine, but his partner wound up with all the money.    He got as far as Iowa and went to work for a farmer named Phelps who had two daughters.  "As the story goes", one daughter fell in love with him,
1 CONC  but he didn't care for her and eloped with her younger sister Lucy, who was only 14.  The older sister became so upset that she went insane.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frederick started a small general store.  While hauling green sawed lumber, a bridge (English Bridge) collapsed under the strain of his four-horse team.  The wagon fell on him and crushed both of his legs.  One did not heal proper
1 CONC ly and it was evntually amputated.  He died May 1, 1889 in Caloma, Iowa of blood poisoning which developed after the amputation.
