0 @N6000000000337357768@ NOTE Source #1:  William A. Stanton, " A RECORD, Genealogical, Biographical, Statistical, OF THOMAS STANTON, OF CONNECTICUT, AND HIS DESCENDANTS: 1635-1891" Joel Munsell's Sons: Albany,  New York (1891)
1 CONT
1 CONT From the New England Historical and Genealogical Register (vol. ii, p. 113), we learn that January 2, 1635, Thomas Stanton took passage for Virginia in the merchantman Bonaventura, and that he recorded himself as being twenty year
1 CONC s old. The ship's record shows no other passenger named Stanton. It is certain, therefore, that he came unattended by any relative bearing the same name. There was a John Stanton in Virginia prior to 1635; from 1652 to 1688 there 
1 CONC are records of a Robert Stanton of Dorchester, Mass., and another Robert Stanton, a Quaker, was a resident of Newport, R. I., prior to 1645. This Robert of Newport died in 1672, aged 73 years. His descendants are now very numerous
1 CONC  in the United States, and many of them are still Friends or Quakers. As Edwin M. Stanton, Lincoln's great Secretary of War, has been thought by many to be a descendant of Thomas, I will state here that he is in direct line from R
1 CONC obert of Newport. Savage and many of the older New England genealogists guessed that Robert was an older brother to Thomas, but there is not the slightest evidence to that effect, nor, so far as records go, do they seem to have ev
1 CONC en known each other or ever inter-visited. 
1 CONT
1 CONT There is no proof that Thomas was related in any way to any person in America, or that any relatives ever followed him to this country. There is a family tradition that before his death he received a legacy from his former home ac
1 CONC ross the sea, but there are no records to prove even this. Undoubtedly there were on record in New London, Conn., many records and documents that would have been invaluable witnesses as to Mr. Stanton's origin, ancestry and youthf
1 CONC ul life. His will was probably recorded there and no other copy of it has as yet been found. But these New London county records were destroyed in 1781 by the traitor Benedict Arnold when he sacked and burnt New London town.
1 CONT
1 CONT  Evidently Mr. Stanton's stay in Virginia was very short. In 1636 he appears on record in Boston, Mass., serving as a magistrate. He may have traded and mingled a little with the Indians in Virginia, acquired some knowledge of the
1 CONC ir language, and then sailed for New England.
1 CONT
1 CONT The next we know of Mr. Stanton is in connection with the Pequoit War. Just before the war he had been serving Winthrop as Indian interpreter, while the latter was in command at Saybrook. July 4, 1636, Mr. Fenwick, Mr. Hugh Peters
1 CONC  and Mr. Thomas Stanton bore instructions from Boston to Winthrop at Fort Saybrook, Conn. (going overland), as to the Pequoits. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The war followed immediately. From Caulkins' Hist of New London, Conn. (p. 296), I copy the following tribute: "The services of Mr. Stanton, as interpreter during the Pequoit War, were invaluable. He was moreover a man of trust an
1 CONC d intelligence, and his knowledge of the country and of the natives made him a useful pioneer and counselor in all land questions, as well as difficulties with the Indians." In DeForest's Hist. of the Indians of Connecticut (p. ii
1 CONC i) is another record of a second visit to Fort Saybrook, near the end of the war, when its commander was the historic soldier, Capt. Lion Gardner. DeForest says: "Sometime in April (1637) a small vessel arrived at the fort, having
1 CONC  on board Thos. Stanton, a man well acquainted with the Indian language, and long useful to the colonial authorities as interpreter." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Mr. Stanton served through this war. Special mention is made of his bravery in the battle of Fairfield Swamp, where he well nigh lost his life. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He must have returned to Boston very soon after peace was secured, for Drake, in his History of Boston, names him as one of the magistrates in the trial of John Wheelwright, which took place Oct. 3, 1637. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The next seventeen months of Mr. Stanton's life were eventful ones, for in them occurred his marriage and settlement in Hartford, Conn. In June, 1636, Rev. Thomas Hooker and 100 other persons came from Newtown (Cambridge), Mass., 
1 CONC and founded Hartford, Conn. In February, 1639, there were 127 property-holders in Hartford, and among them were Dr. Thomas Lord and his son-in-law, Thomas Stanton. It is not known whether Dr. Lord and his family came to Hartford w
1 CONC ith Hooker or at a later date. The time of Mr. Stanton's arrival and marriage is not known, but we do know that his wife was Ann, eldest daughter and third child of Dr. Thomas and Dorothy Lord. 
1 CONT
1 CONT A fac-simile of the signature of Thos. Stanton of Hartford, 
1 CONT Conn., to the Constitution of the Colony of Connecticut. Signed by him during the session of 
1 CONT "A genrall Cort at Harteford. 5th Apr 1638."  At said term of court the following record was made: 
1 CONT
1 CONT "It is ordered that Thomas Staunton shal be a publicke officer for to attend the Corte uppon all occasions, either Genorall or prticuler, as alsoe any meetinge of the Magistrates to interprett betweene them and the Indians, as als
1 CONC o is to have for it 101. pr Annum." 
1 CONT
1 CONT At this same session of Court he, with others, was sent on a mission to the Warranocke Indians. The same year (1638) Mr. Stanton was a delegate to an English and Indian Council-meeting at Hartford. Among other delegates were Roger
1 CONC  Williams of Providence, Uncas of the Mohegans and Miantinomo of the Narragansetts. He also received ten pounds for past services to his country, and was given authority to represent Hartford in all trade for beavers at 1s. per sk
1 CONC in. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Nov. 24, 1638, we find him at New Haven acting as interpreter for the Yorkshire colonists of Quinnipiac (New Haven) in their purchase of the land, where the city now stands, from the Indians. To the deed of said land is added the 
1 CONC following declaration: 
1 CONT
1 CONT "I, Thomas Stanton, being interpreter in this treaty, do hereby profess in the presence of God that I have fully acquainted the Indians with the substance of every article and truly returned their answer and consent to the same, a
1 CONC ccording to the tenor of the foregoing writing, the truth of which, if lawfully called, I shall readily confirm at any time. 
1 CONT
1 CONT                              (Signed) THOMAS STANTON." 
1 CONT
1 CONT This year Thos. Stanton, Jr., was born in Hartford. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1641, the second son, John, was born. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In April, 1642, Mr. Stanton and his brother-in-law, Mr. Richard Lord, make a voyage to Long Island to trade and collect old debts. It would seem that these two were then in business partnership which may have been dissolved the ne
1 CONC xt year by the quarrel already referred to. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1643, the eldest daughter, Mary, was born in Hartford, and in 1644 Hannah was born. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The third son and fifth child, Joseph, was born in 1646. That same year Mr. Stanton violates his license and sells some lead beyond the jurisdiction of Hartford. For this he pays a fine of five pounds. Because of his trading trips
1 CONC  or for some other reason he is so long absent from Court as an official that a successor to him is appointed for one year, but in 1648 Mr. Stanton is re-engaged as Court Interpreter at a salary of five pounds per year. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The same year his sixth child, Daniel, is born in Hartford. In connection with that birth it is inferred that some trouble arose, for shortly after we find Mr. Stanton entering suit for 200 pounds in an action of slander against o
1 CONC ne Joane Sipperance, "to the utter undoing of his wife's good name, and almost taking away her life." Since he got judgment for only 20s. we conclude that while he had cause for complaint it was not quite so serious a matter as he
1 CONC  thought. 
1 CONT
1 CONT We now find a possible explanation for the long absence from Court of Thomas in 1646. In 1648 he has decided to remove his business from Hartford to the south-eastern corner of the Colony, and the following record is a result of h
1 CONC is decision. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "A Session of the Generall Courte, 6th of Febr. 1649.
1 CONT This Courte, taking into consideracon the petition of Thos. Staunton presented to them, have graunted to him and doe order, that hee shall have libberty to erect a trading house at Pawcatuck, with six acres of planting ground, and
1 CONC  libberty of feed and mowing, according to his present occasions, and that none within this jurisdiction shall trade within that River for the space of three years next ensuing; provided hee submitt himselfe to such other exceptio
1 CONC ns and cautions as the Governor and Deputy shall judge meett." 
1 CONT
1 CONT As soon as possible after receiving permission to do so Mr. Stanton built and occupied his trading-house on the Pawcatuck river, but for the time his family remained in Hartford. When they did move they went first to Pequot and th
1 CONC en to what is now Stonington, Conn. 
1 CONT
1 CONT He probably went to Pequot with his family in 1651, and to his permanent residence at Stonington in 1658. If these dates are correct, his seventh child, Dorothy (b. 1651), was born either just before leaving Hartford or just after
1 CONC  reaching Pequot. The three other children were born in Pequot, Robert in 1653, Sarah in 1655, Samuel in 1657. 
1 CONT
1 CONT About the time of the removal from Hartford, an interesting act of the General Court was recorded, which I now copy. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "Code of Laws, May, 1650. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "This Courte, judging it necessary that some meanes should bee used to convey the lighte and knowledge of God and of his Worde to the Indians and Natives amongst us, doe order that one of the teaching Elders of the Churches in thi
1 CONC s jurisdiction, with the helpe of Thomas Stanton, shall bee desired, twise at leaste in every yeare, to goe amongst the neighbouring Indians and indeavor to make knowne to them the Councells of the Lord, and thereby to draw and st
1 CONC irr them up to direct and order all theire wayes and conversations according to the rule of his Worde. And Mr. Governor and Mr. Deputy, and the other Magistrates are desired to take care to see the thinge attended, and with theire
1 CONC  owne presence so farr as may be convenient incourage the same." 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT THE STANTONS IN STONINGTON.
1 CONT
1 CONT Every student of words will be struck with the fact that Stanton and Stonington have the same etymology and meaning. When the final name was given to Stonington (for it was first known as Southertowne), did the prominence and numb
1 CONC ers of the Stantons there decide the name, or is it a mere coincidence? 
1 CONT
1 CONT This question is respectfully referred to the family debating club for decision. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Miss Caulkins, New London county's painstaking historian, says that in 1656, Mr. Stanton sold to George Tongue the grant of 6 acres of planting ground, etc., made to him in 1649. 
1 CONT
1 CONT She records in her County History this very pleasant reading for the descendants of Mr. Stanton: 
1 CONT
1 CONT "On the Pawkatuck River, the first white inhabitant was Thomas Stanton. His trading-house was probably coeval with the farming operations of Chesebrough (at Wequetequock Cove, 2 1/2 miles east of Stonington), but as a fixed reside
1 CONC nt, with a fireside and family, he was later upon the ground. He himself appears to have been always upon the wing, yet always within call. He was required to be present wherever a court, conference, or treaty was to be held. Neve
1 CONC r, perhaps, did the acquisition of a barbarous language give to a man such immediate, wide-spread, and lasting importance. From the year 1636, when he was Winthrop's interpreter with the Nahantic sachem, to 1670, when Uncas visite
1 CONC d him with a train of warriors and captains to get him to write his will, his name is connected with almost every Indian transaction on record." 
1 CONT
1 CONT In March of 1652, Mr. Stanton received a grant of 300 acres, laid out in a square upon the river, next to his former grant from the Court of 6 acres. Looking forward a few years, it may be said here that in 1659 Chief Cassawashitt
1 CONC  deeded to Thomas Stanton the whole of Pawkatuck Neck and the small islands that lay near to it, known as "The Hommocks." This deed or grant was confirmed by the Court in 1671. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Soon after Mr. Stanton went to his new trading-house, the General Court of Connecticut elected him its Deputy Magistrate. This occurred May 15, 1651, and on Oct. 6 he was commissioned "to make a rigid inquest whether any of those 
1 CONC murtherers of the English before the Pequett warres" could be found that they might be "brought to condign punishment." 
1 CONT
1 CONT He was also to demand œ40 of Ninigrett for payment of Eltwood Pomrye's mare that had been killed by the Indians. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The next we know of him is under date of June 20, 1656, when he with Capt. Denison, Mr. Brewster and Hugh Calkin are to make a list of the state of the towne and the inhabitants, and to make the County rate of Twenty pounds. The s
1 CONC ame year the Commissioners of the United Colonies advised the preparation of a catechism in the Narragansett or Pequot language, and appointed Rev. Mr. Pierson of New Haven, and Mr. Thomas Stanton to do the work. Mr. Pierson's rem
1 CONC oval prevented its execution howeve
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1657 Mr. Stanton did something or said something that greatly roused the wrath of the General Court, and led to a warrant for his arrest. The cause of offense is not clear to me, because I have been unable to find out what is m
1 CONC eant by the "cast of Uncas." It may be that the record was intended to read that the Commissioners had cast off Uncas, and that Mr. Stanton had proof in his pocket of some such insult to his noted friend, the Indian sachem. Howeve
1 CONC r, here is the record of the Court verbatim: 
1 CONT
1 CONT                "COURT AT HARTFORD, 12th of August, 1657. 
1 CONT "James Morgan, ages about 50 years, testifyed upon oath before this Court that he being last winter at Jacob Waterhouse his dwelling house, in company wth Mrs. Brewster, Goody Waterhouse and Capt. Denison, Thomas Staunton also bei
1 CONC ng prsent, hee heard the said Staunton say that the Comissionrs had cast of Uncus, and hee had it in his pockett to shew. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "This Court orders and appoints that Leiftent Wm. Lewis should tomorrow goe to Gilford, & if upon inquiry there hee understand that Thomas Staunton is gone to Branford and not returned, hee is to goe to Mr. Leet, and desire he wou
1 CONC ld take order that Thomas Staunton, in that jurisdiction, at Branford or elsewhere, shall be forthwth sent to Mr. Wells, Deputy Governor, or else take security for 40 l. that hee will appeare before the Gen: Court at Hartford, on 
1 CONC Tuesday next, & abide the judgmt of the Court, in reference to such things as shall be testifyd against him by Major John Mason. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [84]                      Copia. 
1 CONT "You are to bring or cause Thomas Staunton to bee brought before the Gen: Court in Hartford, on Tuesday next, then & there to answere vnto such things as shall bee alleadged against him by Major John Mason & for so doeing this sha
1 CONC ll be yor wart." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Lieutenant Lewis does not seem to have been successful in his effort to bring Mr. Stanton before the Court, and the following records give all the further light to be had upon the subject. 
1 CONT
1 CONT            "BY ORDER OF THE GEN: COURT IN HARTFOR
1 CONT                          J: C: SECrY.               | 
1 CONT To Leiftent Wm. Lewis: 
1 CONT "This Court ordered the Secrt to write to the Comissionrs in refference to Thomas Staunton, wch letter was read & approved, & the Seer ordered to send the same. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [86] This Court orders that if Tho: Staunton comes in the vacancy of this Court, hee shall bee required to put in security for his appearance at the Court in Hartford in October next. 
1 CONT
1 CONT [88] Tho: Staunton, for contempt of the last Gen: Court, in not appearing when sumoned thereunto, for wch offence the Court fines the said Thomas ten pounds." 
1 CONT
1 CONT It would be interesting to know how Mr. Stanton appeased the wrath of the Court and the Commissioners, but adding this to other impossible things we learn that the next year he and his family moved to Wequetequock Cove, two and a 
1 CONC half miles east of Stonington.
1 CONT
1 CONT This was in 1658. The first settler upon the shores of that cove was William Chesebrough, in 1649; the next was Walter Palmer, who came to Salem, Mass., from Nottinghamshire, Eng., in 1629. The third settler was Mr. Stanton. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In October, 1658, this territory belonged to the Massachusetts Plantation, what is now Stonington, New London Co., Conn., was then Southington, Suffolk Co., Mass. The court ordered it managed by Capt. George Denison, Robert Park, 
1 CONC William Chesebrough, Thomas Stanton and Walter Palmer. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1662, Charles II gave Connecticut a new charter that included Southington, in 1665 the name was changed to Mystic, and in 1667 the final change was made to Stonington. The first meeting-house was built in 1661. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Here, then, Mr. Stanton settled down as a permanent resident, and in Drake's "Boston" is found the following account as to the location of his house: "From Wekapauge to Mr. Stanton's is 3 miles, 300 rods; from Mr. Stanton's to Wm.
1 CONC  Chesebrough's is 2 miles, 123 rods; from thence to Misticke river, by Capt. Denison's house, is 4 miles." Mr. Stanton's farm lay on the east side of Pawcatuck river, near its mouth. He also owned another farm at Quanacontaug, whe
1 CONC re Major Tallcott had his headquarters in 1676. Quanacontaug was a neck of land five miles east of Pawcatuck river. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The following record gives an idea of some of the official duties that fell to Mr. Stanton's lot. At a Commissioners' meeting, September 6-17, 1660, this action was taken: 
1 CONT
1 CONT The Commissioners, upon consideration of premises, and of similar complaints preferred by the English residents of the new plantation at Mohegan, resolved "to require and force the Narrogansetts to a just satisfaction," and for th
1 CONC at end commissioned Capt. George Denison, Thomas Stanton, Thomas Mynor and others to repair to Ninigret and the Narragansett sachems, and require of them the punishment of the offenders, and full reparation of injuries done to the
1 CONC  English; that "at least foure of the chiefe of them that shot into the English house at Mouhegin should be proceeded with and punished according to justice, and in case they cannot be drawn thereunto," that five hundred fathoms o
1 CONC f wampum should be exacted in expiation of the offense, and that speedy payment should be made of a quantity of wampum which the Commissioners, the year before, had required of the Narragansetts, "for insolencies committed at Mr. 
1 CONC Brewster's, in killing an Indian servant at Mrs. Brewster's feet, to her great affrightment, and stealing corne, and other affronts." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Three years after this we find Mr. Stanton appointed to apportion 8,000 acres of land to the Pawcatucks, and in 1664 he is appointed to magisterial office by the General Court. The record reads as follows: 
1 CONT
1 CONT                       COURT AT HARTFORD, Octobr 13th, 1664. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [202] This Court doth hereby impower Mr. Wm. Cheesebrooke, Mr. Thomas Stanton and Mr. Thomas Minor to issue and determine all cases that shall be brought before them, to the value of forty shillings, to grant summons according to 
1 CONC lawe to any that desire it, to summon men to appeare before them or at any Court in this Colony, and to marry persons, and punish for criminall matters to the value of forty shillings, or by stocks. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1665 he becomes a Commissioner of Stonington, and has authority to hold a semi-annual court at New London, the county seat. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The first court ever thus held in said county assembled Sept., 20, 1666. The commissioners or judges upon the bench were Major Mason, Thomas Stanton and Lieutenant Pratt of Saybrook, the clerk was Obadiah Bruen. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Among other court duties that year, one that fell to Mr. Stanton was the administration of the freeman's oath to his two sons, Thomas, Jr., and John. Honors now came to his thick and fast. In 1666 he was re-elected Commissioner or
1 CONC  County Judge, he was also appointed Overseer-General of the Coassatuck Indians; a Commission of Appeals on Indian Affairs was created this year, and Mr. Stanton was made a member thereof. As a County Commissioner he evidently was
1 CONC  a great success. He was annually re-elected to the office until his death in 1677, a continuous term of twelve years. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The last honor to come to him in 1666 was his election as a member of the General Assembly of Connecticut. With all this, however, he does not neglect his private business, for we find him entering suit (Oct. 11, 1666), against Ch
1 CONC ief Cuskatome and the chief's brother-in-law for "stealing a considerable estate" from him. Mr. Stanton got judgment in this case for œ27 12s. 2d., to be paid within fourteen days. If not so paid, the Court authorized him "to disp
1 CONC ose of these Indians by selling or sending them to Barbadoes or any other English Island." 
1 CONT
1 CONT Whether he secured his money, or whether he sold the debtors, is unrecorded history. It is to be hoped that the red men paid the cash and kept their freedom. 
1 CONT
1 CONT For many years one of Mr. Stanton's warmest friends was Mr. Walter Palmer of Stonington. Their children intermarried, and in many ways they had much in common. In 1662 Mr. Palmer died, and Mr. Stanton was appointed by the court to
1 CONC  take an inventory of his "goods and chatels." 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1667 Mr. Stanton was again elected a member of the General Assembly of Connecticut, and he continued uninterruptedly to be elected to said Assembly until 1674. 
1 CONT
1 CONT For his services to the Colony the General Court granted him, in October of 1667, 250 acres of land on Pachaug river, in what became the town of Preston. It was specified that 30 acres of the 250 should be in meadow. 
1 CONT
1 CONT As Indian Commissioner he was called upon this year to settle some difficulties that were threatening trouble between Uncas, chief of the Mohegans, and the Niantic tribe. The following year Mr. Stanton was reappointed Commissioner
1 CONC  of Indian Affairs. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In those Colonial days serious differences often arose between rival towns and between towns and the General Court or Assembly. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Some such trouble existed between Stonington and the General Court at Hartford. Thomas Stanton's second son, John Stanton, was then Stonington's Town Recorder. On May 4th, 1668, Thomas drew up a petition on behalf of the town and 
1 CONC addressed it to the Court. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The petition was signed by John as recorder, but his father was the author. It seems that the dignity of the General Court was greatly injured by this appeal, and, either for this presentation of it or for presenting another one j
1 CONC ust like it in 1670, two or three of Stonington's leading officials got into serious trouble. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas seemed to escape, but John was fined ten pounds, which, however, was revoked (it being yet unpaid) at the close of King Philip's War on account of John's service in that war. The settlers along the Connecticut and Rhode Isl
1 CONC and line had a good deal of disputing, and the same month this petition was drawn up, just ten days after it, Thomas Stanton and John Allyn were authorized to go to Rhode Island, confer with the Governor and Council, and demand sa
1 CONC tisfaction of them for Rhode Island's aggressions and insults. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The 3rd of June these two men undertook their commission, but their demands were not met. They were reappointed Sept. 8th, but with what results the records do not show. The petition of Stonington to the court reads as follows: 
1 CONT
1 CONT                                           "May 4, 1668. 
1 CONT "PETITION OF THE TOWN OF STONINGTON TO THE GENERAL 
1 CONT  COURT AT HARTFORD FOR PROTECTION AGAINST THE ENCROACHMENTS 
1 CONT  OF THOSE OF R. ISLAND. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT "A Companie of personnes from Road Island, whoe threatned the east side of Paquatuck River (wch seemed at first to vs but as rediculos) yet have built, fenced, improved & forsibly settled & ar growing numerovs, & doe noe less thre
1 CONC aten the west side of Paquatuck River and wee know not but they may as well take all as a part, nay, our houses over our heades, by as much right as wt thay at present injoye, for any thing the Pattent priveledges more to one side
1 CONC  of the River then the other. 
1 CONT
1 CONT "Least multitude of busines might overwhelme you, & our beeing remoat & as ovt of sight might too much burie us in oblivion, or want of information might render you the les sensible of our condition, wee make bold to remind you, &
1 CONC  if it maye bee to add a litell breath to the saylls and fethers to the winges of your solicitous indeavours in our behalfe." 
1 CONT
1 CONT In all probability it was this touch of irony and facetiousness that was more than the gravity of the old Puritan legislators could stand, and that induced them to punish their Stonington brethren. But to continue with the petitio
1 CONC n: 
1 CONT
1 CONT "They complain of the conduct of their neighbours, the intruders east of the River, `neither can any true harted & fellowfeeling Christians choose but mourne to see & heare of our neighbouring disorders, & acknoledg our condition 
1 CONC is trulie deplorable to have personnes of such corrupt principalls & praktises to live so near vs, and on our owne. 
1 CONT "'Tis not of small concernment, the bad example yt is given to the Indians. 'Tis to the griefe of parents & others, observed how these fire brands too much inflame youth. 
1 CONT "May not parents heartes bleed when about to leave the world to thinke how thay leave their deer children in the mouth of the Lion & paw of the Beare, and worse, as being daylie tempted by exampells to folow after & imbrace lies, 
1 CONC to live as riotous, wanton, luxsurious, and even no better than to bee said vnto, Serve other Gods or no God? 
1 CONT "Surelie if this case be made as your owne, you will not spare for cost or paynes or bee unwilling to run hassard in the indeavour of our speedie redres." 
1 CONT
1 CONT The petitioners recapitulate the grounds on which the Conn. right of jurisdiction is based,--the priority of Charter, the decision of the Royal Commissioner, first possession, &c.; in conclusion they refer to several recent acts o
1 CONC f the Rhode Islanders.-- Mr. Thos. Stanton had been prohibited from mowing land granted to Mr. Willis, and threatened with the cost of œ1000 if he persisted; "they take in newcomers contrarie, vis. Goodwife Seager, James Walklie, 
1 CONC and the tinker &c. 
1 CONT
1 CONT   "Signed by order of the Town, by 
1 CONT                          "JOHN STANTO
1 CONT                                    "Town Recorder." 
1 CONT
1 CONT These inter-colonial fends continued for a long time. Three years after this petition while Mr. Stanton and Mr. Thos. Miner were one day laying out lands east of Pawcatuck river they were met and opposed by Tobias Saunders, John C
1 CONC randall and some other Rhode Island men armed with clubs. Afterward Crandall was arrested, tried and imprisoned at New London for the above assault. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1669 Mr. Stanton's son Joseph, was appointed Assistant Magistrate of Stonington, and he held court with his father in New London. On the 8th of July, 1669, we find Thomas Stanton and his son John reporting to Major Mason at Nor
1 CONC wich, their suspicions of a plot against the English, led by Uncas, Hermon Garrett and a daughter of Ninigret. Not long after, Thomas goes to Robinstown where the Indians are having a great dance and arrests Ninigret. In 1672 he i
1 CONC s sent to the Niantic Indians to demand the surrender of Mowweam for a double murder, that of an English girl and of a squaw. In 1675 Mr. Stanton and Major Winthrop are authorized to effect the ransom or exchange of a son of James
1 CONC  Rockwell, who was held captive by the Indians. 
1 CONT
1 CONT This year the Indians became very aggressive, and in June, Mr. Stanton sends word to the Governor at Hartford, of the first act of hostility in King Philip's war. This act was the burning of houses and killing of men at Swansey. W
1 CONC ar was immediately declared. Mr. Stanton and his sons bore no unimportant part in this conflict. Under the biographies of John and of Joseph will be found further details as to their experiences. It may be here said of John, howev
1 CONC er, in addition to what is otherwise recorded, that in 1676, he made an exchange with Abachickwood and other "Narrogancetts" for some Indians, giving for them other Indians that he had captured in war. He then sold to a Major Palm
1 CONC es the Indians he got in exchange. This was undoubtedly in harmony with the code of warfare in that century, but it illustrates the fact that the world is getting better. Capt. John Stanton's descendants would not now do what he c
1 CONC onscientiously did in 1676.  That was in February; later in the season he is ordered to distribute among his men the spoils taken in war, and in November he goes to the Indians and instructs them as to the laws passed for their co
1 CONC nduct. All of the sons of Thomas Stanton seem to have inherited their father's ability to speak the Indian dialects, and they were in much demand as interpreters. Joseph Stanton served in this capacity in the treaty made between t
1 CONC he English and the Narragansetts at the close of King Philip's war. The father was then too old to engage in much active service, but we find him acting with Major Winthrop and Hon. Samuel Willys as a commission to negotiate peace
1 CONC , if possible, between the English and Indians.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the year of his death we learn that he returns to the Indian, Robbin Cassacinamon, all the wampum that he holds in trust for him from Ninicroft. We also know that his is the first name on the roll of the First Congregational Ch
1 CONC urch of Stonington, which he was instrumental in organizing June 3, 1674. Mr. Stanton's son-in-law, Rev. James Noyes, was ordained as the first pastor of this church Sept. 10, 1674, and remained its pastor for more than 55 years. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The cause of Mr. Stanton's death is unknown. He died Dec. 2, 1677, and his will was probated in June, 1678.  As to his burial place, the following from Caulkins' History of New London (p. 285) is full of interest. On the east side
1 CONC  of Wicketequack Cove, half way between Stonington, Conn., and Westerly, R. I., is the old family burying-ground, and Miss Caulkins says: "Here undoubtedly Mr. Chesebrough and all his sons were buried. Here lies Walter Palmer in t
1 CONC he midst of a throng of descendants. Here Thomas Stanton lies near the stones bearing the names of his sons, Thomas and Robert."
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
