0 @N6000000004229659147@ NOTE REFN: 9305 
1 CONT Joseph was a Magistrate.
0 @N6000000004229327395@ NOTE REFN: 1010 
1 CONT He was son of Thomas Stanton and Ann Lord
1 CONT He married Hannah Thompson 1664. (daughter of Rev Wm Thompson.  John
1 CONT studied at Harvard and was a Captain in King Phillips War.  Before his
1 CONT death he gave his son Joseph the homestead farm in Stonington, and John
1 CONT and Thomas all the lands in Preston, Conn.Capt John was Captain of one
1 CONT of the four Conn. Regiments in King Phillips' war.  He captured Canocher
1 CONT the chief sachem of the Narragansetts.
1 CONT He studied war at Harvard and was Captain in one of Conn regiments in
1 CONT King Phillips War. He captured Canochetm the chief sachan of the
1 CONT Narragansetts.
0 @N6000000000337357744@ NOTE Source #1:  William A. Stanton, " A RECORD, Genealogical, Biographical, Statistical, OF THOMAS STANTON, OF CONNECTICUT, AND HIS DESCENDANTS: 1635-1891" Joel Munsell's Sons: Albany,  New York (1891)
1 CONT
1 CONT Joseph2 Stanton was b. 1646 in Hartford, Conn.; he was bapt. March 21, 1646. He removed with his parents to Stonington. He settled on a great tract of land which his father had bought of a Narragansett sachem for a half bushel of 
1 CONC wampum. The sachem's child was a captive and was redeemed by the aid of Thomas1, and so the Indian sold the land as part payment of the price. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In ancient Boston records, Joseph is described as "Joseph Stanton of Squanicot, Narraganset county, King's Province;" and in a lease recorded in Boston he describes the land leased as "half the farm or neck named Quannecontague, w
1 CONC here I do now live." This land was where Charlestown, R. I., now is, formerly a part of Westerly. The lease was dated Nov. 20, 1685. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1669 Joseph lived in Stonington and was appointed assistant magistrate to hold court in New London with his father who was magistrate. 
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1714 Ben Uncas and fifty-four other Mohegans signed a paper, dated in May, affirming that Oweneco had wrongfully sold a great part (western part) of their lands, and they then consigned what was left to Joseph Stanton and four 
1 CONC others. This was probably Joseph2, though as he d. in 1714 it may have been another Joseph. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Joseph m. June 19, 1673, to HANNAH, dau. of William MEAD of Roxbury, Mass. William Mead's will was made at Roxbury in 1683; he gave half of his estate to his wife and half to Joseph Stanton; there was also a charge of œ20 against 
1 CONC Joseph for Mead's supply of clothing, etc., to Joseph's daughter Hannah. Joseph's wife d, 1676. They had two children: 
1 CONT
1 CONT  I JOSEPH3, b. 1674; m. Esther Gallup.
1 CONT
1 CONT II  HANNAH3, b. 1676; m. Dr. James York of Westerly, 
1 CONT             R. I., Nov. 13, 1695. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Joseph2 m. (2) Aug. 23, 1677, HANNAH LORD 
1 CONT             of Hartford, Conn. She was buried May 6, 1681, 
1 CONT             leaving children: 
1 CONT      III   THOMAS3, b. Dec. 16, 1678; d. young. 
1 CONT      IV   REBECCA3, b. April    , 1681; m. Joseph Babcock; 
1 CONT             had issue. 
1 CONT There is in the records of the Stonington church the admission to the church of the wife of Joseph Stanton, March 16, 1683. This must have been a third wife. She seems to have died childless. But he had three children born after 1
1 CONC 690, and both Dr. Savage and Hon. John D. Baldwin think they were the issue by a wife named (???) PRENTICE. Baldwin "conjectures" that "Miss Prentice" was a fourth wife. Savage thinks she was the third wife. The record of their ba
1 CONC ptisms in the Stonington church is as sons of Joseph Stanton of Westerly and reads thus: 
1 CONT
1 CONT     V   THOMAS3, bapt. April 5, 1691. 
1 CONT     VI   DANIEL3, bapt. April 1, 1694. 
1 CONT     VII   SAMUEL3, bapt. July 17, 1698; d. young. 
1 CONT
1 CONT  Joseph d. in 1714, aged 68 years. 
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
