0 @N6000000004538117749@ NOTE Elizur Holyoke
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Captain Elizur Holyoke (c. 1624 – 1676) was the namesake of the mountain, Mount Holyoke, and (indirectly), of the city of Holyoke, Massachusetts.
1 CONT
1 CONT Holyoke arrived in the Massachusetts Bay Colony from the town of Tamworth in Staffordshire, England in 1637 or 1638 at the age of 20. He and his family settled in Chelsea, Massachusetts, where his father, Edward Holyoke Jr., had a
1 CONC  farm. His mother is Prudence Stockton.
1 CONT
1 CONT On September 20, 1640, Elizur Holyoke traveled to Springfield, Massachusetts and married Mary Pynchon, daughter of William Pynchon, the founder of Springfield. The Holyoke and Pynchon families appeared to be close friends, dating 
1 CONC back to their days in England. Elizur and Mary Pynchon marriage was the first for the small community.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1642, Elizur received 10 rods (English measurement of 16.5 linear feet per rod; a square plot of land) just off Chestnut Street in Springfield. In 1643, other desirable lots were given to Elizur by his father-in-law, one being 
1 CONC between what is now Worthington and Bridge Streets.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Land issues
1 CONT In 1647, Elizur was the second largest land owner in Springfield, at 125 acres. William Pynchon had 237 acres. After William Pynchon's problems with the General Court of Boston and his eventual retreat to England, William’s son Jo
1 CONC hn Pynchon and Elizur Holyoke took control of Springfield, and in 1652 Elizur was appointed commissioner to govern the town of Springfield. (2)
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1660, after the founding of Hadley and Northampton, a question arose regarding the exact borders of Springfield. The general court of Springfield sent out two surveying parties to explore the surrounding regions. Elizur Holyoke
1 CONC  served as head of one of these parties, while Rowland Thomas headed the other party. While Holyoke traversed the east side of the Connecticut River, Thomas explored the west side of the river. Lore holds that the two held a conve
1 CONC rsation over a narrow part of the river near Hockanum.
1 CONT
1 CONT Mount Holyoke
1 CONT It is commonly stated that Holyoke and Thomas gave their names to the mountains that stood at the end of their paths. Mount Holyoke was named for Elizur Holyoke, while Rowland Thomas named Mount Tom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Career
1 CONT In 1661, Elizur was chosen as Springfield's deputy to the General Court; 1662, he was appointed associate county judge - recorder of all courts; 1663, made the rank of Captain, and was a selectman and deputy to the general court; 
1 CONC in 1675, the Holyoke home was destroyed during a raid by Indians lead by Wequogan.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the King Philip's War, Capt. Elizur Holyoke was in command of the successful defense of Springfield, until his death in 1676, one account says not due directly to any injury. However, there are other accounts that say he wa
1 CONC s killed by the Wampanoag Indians on 5 Feb 1676.
1 CONT
1 CONT Contrary to some records, both the city of Holyoke and Mount Holyoke Female Seminary (now Mount Holyoke College) were named after the nearby mountain, Mount Holyoke, and not directly after Elizur Holyoke. Elizur Holyoke's grandson
1 CONC  Rev. Edward Holyoke was president of Harvard University from 1737 to 1769. Elizur is the uncle of Thomas Putnam and the great uncle of Ann Putnam, Jr. of the Salem witch trials 
1 CONT
1 CONT References
1 CONT Harper, Wyatt. 'The Story of Holyoke. Holyoke, MA: Holyoke Centennial Committee, 1973. 
1 CONT Ella Merkel DiCarlo. "Holyoke - Chicopee; A Perspective." Transcript-Telegram (Holyoke, MA / 1982) 
1 CONT Josiah Gilbert Holland. "The Mountain Christening." notes circa 1660. 
1 CONT John Gibbs Holyoke. "Holyoke, A North American Family 1637 - 1992." Gateway Press, Inc. (Baltimore, 1993) 
1 CONT Retrieved from "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elizur_Holyoke"
0 @N6000000008562607024@ NOTE Edward Holyoke, a farmer, and his son Elizur Holyoke, abt 20yrs old, came to Chelsea fro Tamworth, Warwickshire, England in 1637 or 1638.  The family and that of William Pynchon, Founder of Springfield had been friendly in England
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizur moved to Springfield and married Mary Pynchon daughter of William. They lived on land in vicinity of Worthington and Bridge streets.  A prominent man in the settlement, Elizur served as selectman, constable and later in 165
1 CONC 2 as one of three commissioners appointed by the general court to govern the town.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1660, after the establishment of settlements at Northampton and Hadley, a question arose as to the exact limits of Springfield.  Holyoke was head of one of two surveying parties sent out to make a survey for presentation to the
1 CONC  general court.  He went up the east side of the river, Rowland Thomas going up the west side, headed the other party.  The tradition is that Mt. Holyoke and Mt. Tom were named by the pair.  Holyoke had served successively as ensi
1 CONC gn, lieutenant, and captain of the Springfield militia and hel the latter rank from 1653 until his death.  His wife Mary Pynchon, died October 26,1657. He then married Editha Stebbins, who had been successively the wife and widow 
1 CONC of Robert Day and John Maynard. Elizur Holyoke died Feb 5 1676, leaving four sons.
1 CONT
1 CONT Information obtained by leter received by Gloria Forrest-Lecompte from Frank H. Wotton of the Holyoke Public Library Oct 1995.
