0 @N6000000000250111160@ NOTE Andrews' Hamblin genealogy says: "farmer: Iowa: Tooele, Santa Clar a and Kanab, Utah.  Democrat; Mormons." (He does not mention Adeline's fi rst marriage.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Census:
1 CONT  1850 Russell, MA, a farmer, living with the Edmin Malley family. [Censu s copy is badly faded and barely legible. ]
1 CONT  1860 Tonaquint, UT: Ensign Riggs, age 28, farmer, real estate $150, per sonal estate $475, b. MA. Head of household including wife Adeline an d 6 children.
1 CONT  1870 Claratown, UT: age 39, farmer, real estate worth $300, personal es tate worth $375, born MA.
1 CONT  1880 Kanab, UT: age 49, farmer, b. MA. (7 houses from son Albert )
1 CONT NOTE: The 1890 census was largely destroyed by fire, so we cannot be sur e where Ensign and Adeline were living then.  Ensign might still have ha d his own farm, or have been living with Albert when he died. If he die d at age 96,
1 CONC  as family stories say, rather than at age 63 as the Hambli n genealogy says, he should appear on the 1900, 1910 and 1920 censuses.
1 CONT
1 CONT      "Ensign Riggs was friendly with the Indians (Paiute?) who spent summ ers in Utah, and returned south for the winter.  He always treated them f airly.  One year when they returned to Utah, they found their stock of se ed corn 
1 CONC had been damaged or destroyed.  Ensign shorted his family's cor n to share with the Indians.  They brought game and goods to pay him fo r the grain.  At another time, shortly after the Indians had left for th e winter, Ensign was 
1 CONC in the woods near his home and heard what sounded li ke a small animal whimpering. He found papoose hanging in its wrapping s on the branch of a tree. The baby was very sick.  Ensign took it home , and Adeline nursed it back to he
1 CONC alth.  The next spring, when the Indian s returned, Ensign went to greet them, and asked about the papoose.  He l earned that it was the first child of a young couple.  The baby had becom e very ill just when the tribe had to leav
1 CONC e for the south, and the elder s had decreed that the child must be left to die, lest caring for it slo w the tribe's journey and cause others to lose their lives when snow clos ed the mountain passes.  Ensign was able to restore 
1 CONC the baby to his grate ful parents.  That summer, Ensign and three of his sons were invited to j oin the Indians at the Feast of the New Corn -- a celebration when boys w ere accepted as men in the tribe.  Ensign and his sons were 
1 CONC made member s of the tribe." -- Daphne (Shulsen) Sanford
0 @N6000000000250111460@ NOTE Her name varies: Malenza, Melinza, Melenza, Melonza.  Melenza is cit ed by Currier and W.J.L. Notes as from brother Beals E. Litchfield's auto biography, so I shall use that form .
1 CONT      Currier adds to the marriage record: "by Isaac King J.P."
1 CONT      Elisha Litchfield Jr.'s pension record shows Hopewell NY was in Onta rio County.
1 CONT      Daughter Melenza Riggs received five dollars in the will of Ensign L itchfield dated 1 October 1840.
1 CONT      She died 5 March 1892, aged 87 years 6 months, and is buried in Hope well, NY, but the death record did not show the name of the cemetery.
1 CONT
