0 @N6000000004089615252@ NOTE Nicholas Wade came from Downham Market in Denver as noted above. Downham Market is in Norfolk , which is a seacoast county, in the East of England. on the shores of the North Sea, close t o the Great Wa sh ; it took its name from 
1 CONC the East English settlers, who called their peopl e in these parts the "North folk," now Norfolk, and in the southern parts of their kingdom "S outh folk," now Suffolk. The co unty is an egg shape, the ends lying east and west; th
1 CONC e north ern half being bound by the Wash and the North Sea, with a small part to the west joining Cam bridgeshire. The boundary between Norfolk an d Suffolk is the river Waveney, from its mouth a t Yarmouth, south-westerly, to nea
1 CONC r its spring, and then by the Thet, or Little Ouse, flowin g north-westerly. From Cambridgeshire, Norfolk is divided b y the Nene. The greatest length i s 67 miles and the greatest breadth 43. The area is 1,315,064 acres, the boun
1 CONC dary having bee n altered by the addition, for administration purposes, of Gorleston, fro m Suffolk, with 2,1 66 acres, and 1,817 from Welney, Cambs., and the transfer of 1,925 acres, part of Central Win gland, to Lincolnshire, an
1 CONC d 23 acres of Brandon to Suffolk; it is the fourth shire in si ze , Yorkshire, Lincolnshire and Devonshire larger.
1 CONT
1 CONT Downham Market probably had its origins as a Saxon settlement, sited around the elevated grou nd on which the Church was built, and achieved Market status by the year 1050. Downham was gr anted to the A bbey of Ramsey as early as 
1 CONC the reign of Edgar (959-975 AD.). Confirmation of t his is recorded during the reigns of Edward the Confessor, William 1 and King John in 1047 A. D., 1078 A.D. and 1200 A.D. ( 1) respectively.
1 CONT
1 CONT The part of East Anglia in which it is situated is known as Hereward the Wake country, it bei ng closely associated with the exploits of that worthy in his struggle against William the Co nqueror. The t errain, known as the Fens, 
1 CONC is a vast flat plain that stretches from Cambridg e to the coast and is considerably different today since the drainage operation to reclaim la nd for agriculture was finall y completed in the nineteenth century, having been start
1 CONC ed in t he first instance, many years before, by the Monks at Crowland Abbey.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, it was not until the 17th century, at the hands of Cornelius Vermuyden, the celebrat ed Dutch engineer of the time who was employed by the Duke of Bedford, that any impact was ma de in this res pect. Charles Kingsley desc
1 CONC ribed it to be similar, in the seventh century, t o the forests at the mouth of the Mississippi on the swampy shores of the Carolinas.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Town lies on the Roman Akeman Street and takes its name from a HAM (dwelling place) o n a DUN or DOWN (hill). In the Doomsday book there are references to Duneham and Dunham, prob ably derived fro m the Anglo-Saxon word Dun or
1 CONC  Dune meaning a hill and Downham has been calle d in times past, Dunharn, Donham, Douneham, Dounham, Dunarne. In a similar vein, two village s close to the Town, Hilgay an d Bexwell, have been known in the past as Helgaye and Beky
1 CONC swel l. Thus Dounham or Douneham, roughly translated, means an estate on a hill. The Roman road i s said to have traversed through Hertfordshir e from Cirencester in the South, to Cambridge , Ely and thence through Downham Market 
1 CONC to King's Lynn. Certainly from Cambridge, the Al0 tru nk road seems to have followed the path of this ancient route.
1 CONT
1 CONT Nicholas was a 19 year old yeoman and came to the US in 1635 aboard the ship Falcon. His shi p first went to Barbados. His brothers Jonathan and Nathaniel were also yeoman and came ove r the next thre e years. Once in the US, Nich
1 CONC olas Wade took the oath of fidelity 1638. His ho use and homestead were on the west side of Brushy Hill, and north-east side of road where Sha drach, his descendant of the s ixth generation, now (1831) resides (in Scituate). In 16
1 CONC 57, h e was licensed to keep an ordinary or tavern. It is reported that his original house is stil l standing at 200 Country Way in Scituate.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1684, after the death of Nicholas, an inventory was taken of the estates and cattle of Nic holas Wade.
1 CONT
1 CONT One dwelling, house and barn, measow lands, purse and apparell, the best bed & bedsteed & fur niture, two flocks of beds and bedsteed and furniture, a piece of woolen cloth, two Ioyne Che sts, sheets an d linen, linnen and cotton 
1 CONC yarn, pewter and tin, brass, iron pots and kettles , Trammell ongs & other iron things, arms and ammunition, books, one long table and stools, t ubs and pails, beef and pork , four cows, one bull, and two young cattle, one sheep, 
1 CONC two mare s, two young swine, lumber belonging to the house, one bridle and saddle collar, rye and indi an corn, saw cahin, crow, and other iron to ols, one hatchet, spinning wheels and cards and e arth ware, one looking glass and 
1 CONC a box iron. The total value given to Nicholas's belongings w as 120 19 00. Debts were owed to Isaak Buck senior, Samul a Clap, also to Mr John Wing of Bos ton, Margrett Collman, Ensigne Allen, and Ephraim Little totaling 20 06 00.
1 CONT
1 CONT Elizabeth Wade, Nicholas's wife, appeared on June 24, 1684 to confirm the estate of Nicholas.
1 CONT [Baulch.ged]
