0 @N6000000004089710866@ NOTE Bigamy, too, was found in Plymouth Colony. Nicholas Wade of Scituate in 1680 complained his d aughter Elizabeth had married Thomas Stevens not knowing of his ealier marriages. It appeare d that at the t ime Stevens married her, he
1 CONC  already had a wife at Boston, a wife and childre n in England, and another wife in Barbadoes. the court agreed this was sufficient reasonto di smiss Elizabeth from the afores aid marriage bond, and it disolved the covenant of mar
1 CONC riage b etween them. Elizabeth was granted the liberty of marrying again, while Steven was sentence d to be severely whipped at the post. - Eugene A ubrey Stratton, FASG. Plymouth Colony: Its H istory and People 1620-1691.
