0 @N6000000002441175039@ NOTE Captain Francis DRAKE is presumed to be the son of Robert Drake and 
1 CONT it is believed that he was estranged from his father because of differences
1 CONT in religious beliefs. He did remove from Hampton, New Hampshire in 1668
1 CONT where he and his wife, Mary Walker, began a huge family that has since
1 CONT spread throughout the whole country.
1 CONT
1 CONT Captain Francis Drake was born in England and came to New Hampshire with his
1 CONT father about 1643. He was married,undoubtedly in New Hampshire about 1650.
1 CONT C.I. Kephart adds, "Francis Drake soon became an outstanding figure in
1 CONT the Piscataway Colony. In addition to being a land-owner, he conducted a
1 CONT tavern. The records show that he was commissioned Captain of Militia of
1 CONT Piscataway Township on July 15, 1675, and was discharged at his own request
1 CONT on May 30, 1678. He was one of the first selectmen of Piscataway, was
1 CONT Constable, Justice of the Peace, and in 1682 was County Judge.
1 CONT Source: "The Drakes in New Hampshire, New Jersey and Kentucky" by
1 CONT Clifford M. Hardin and Martha Hardin
1 CONT C.I. Kephart, writing in the D.A.R. Magazine (Vol. 64, 1930)
1 CONT Mrs. Betsy Errickson of the Hopewell N. J. Historical Museum
1 CONT Ora E. Monette
1 CONT Chambers and Savage
1 CONT
1 CONT ! "THE DRAKES in NEW HAMPSHIRE, NEW JERSEY and KENTUCKY"
1 CONT by Clifford M. Hardin & Mary Wood Hardin
1 CONT
1 CONT ! It has been proved beyond doubt that Captain Francis Drake of New Jersey was the progenitor of a huge family of Drakes in New Jersey. There are differing views as to the parentage and English origins of Captain Francis Drake. Al
1 CONC l do agree, however, that he was living in Portsmouth, New
1 CONT Hampshire in the early 1650's, and that he did move with his wife and children to Piscataway, New Jersey between 1665 and 1670. In the following paragraphs we will summarize some of the published evidence and views.
1 CONT C. I. Kephart, writing in the D.A.R. Magazine (Vol. 64, 1930), writes as follows:
1 CONT "The presumed father of Francis Drake was Robert Drake, who with his
1 CONT children emigrated from Colchester, Essex;, England to New Hampshire in the
1 CONT autumn of probably 1640, and finally settled at Hampton, where he died January 14, 1668, aged 88 years. Thus he was born in 1579. He was a "seargemaker" by occupation at the time of emigrating. According to a deposition executed b
1 CONC y his sons Nathaniel and Abraham on April 27, 1691, which appears in the "New England Historical and Genealogical Register, Vol. 21, page 316, they had resided at Colchester "since childhood"'. The will of immigrant Robert Drake, 
1 CONC on file at Ipswich, Massachusetts, makes no mention of a wife or a son Francis. This circumstance has added to the difficulty of tracing this connection."
1 CONT "The records show that Ann, wife of Robert Drake;, was buried at Epping, Essex, in May of 1640. In all probability this was the wife of the immigrant Robert, and upon her demise the family departed for the New World. Robert was ne
1 CONC ver remarried, and his household apparently was managed by his daughter, Susannah, who seems not to have married."
1 CONT "When Robert Drake immigrated to these shores he was accompanied by the
1 CONT following named children: (They settled first in Exeter and later, during
1 CONT 1650-51, at Hampton, N.H.)
1 CONT (1) Nathaniel, born 1612-13 (he was past 78 years in 27 April 1691).
1 CONT (2) Francis (presumably), born about 1615.
1 CONT (3) Susannah, born about 1617, probably never married.
1 CONT
1 CONT (4) Abraham, born about 1620/21 (he was past 70 years on April 27, 1691)
1 CONT "Francis was granted land near Portsmouth, New Hampshire, on April 17, 1654, and on July 10, 1655. (N.E. Historical and Genealogical Register, Vol. 2, page 126.) Nathaniel and Francis participated in a distribution of public land 
1 CONC near Portsmouth as of January 22, 1660. (See Rambles about Portsmouth, by L.W.Brewster, 1st. series, page 28). During 1660 both Nathaniel and Francis were grand jurors at Portsmouth. In 1663 a road was projected by the fenced fiel
1 CONC d of Francis Drake toward Hampton. During the same year the people of Portsmouth elected Nathaniel and Francis Drake as surveyors of highways. As of February 15, 1664, Nathaniel and Francis were adjacent land owners near Portsmout
1 CONC h (N.E. Hist. and Gen. Reg.l, Vol. 23, page 163). In July 1665, a petition alleging misgovernment and seeking protection of their property and rights was signed by these two men, among others, and filed at Dover, N. H. There undou
1 CONC btedly appears reason that actuated the removal of Francis Drake from New Hampshire to New Jersey. The religious disturbances of the New England Colonies are a part of the history of that region. Francis Drake had espoused the cau
1 CONC se of the Baptists, became estranged from his presumed relations, and in consequence of local conditions joined the Baptist colony established by co-religionists at Piscataway, New Jersey."
1 CONT Alice Thompson in "The DRAKE FAMILY of New Hampshire" 1962 writes:
1 CONT "Later generations of Robert's descendants also knew something about
1 CONT Francis. Francis and his wife, Mary, sold land in Greenland (near Portsmouth) on August 5, 1668, signed and acknowledged that day, but not recorded until August 1721, fifty-three years later. When it ws recorded a memorandum was a
1 CONC dded, signed by Abraham Drake, a possible grand-nephew, that "quiet and peaceable possession" was actually given to John Johnson and Thomas Brackett on September 30, 1668".
1 CONT "Francis at an unknown later date, with his wife and three children, went to Piscataway, New Jersey, as members of a group who wished to rid themselves of religious persecution and bitter disputes over land boundaries. They moved 
1 CONC at the instigation of the governor of New Jersey, who had learned of their difficulties and had promised them complete religious freedom. In New Jersey, Francis' son John was for more than fifty years pastor of a Baptist Church, a
1 CONC  sect not tolerated in Portsmouth, nor in Hampton. If Francis was related to Robert, differences in religious opinion may account for the omission of any legacy in Robert's WILL. Robert's will offers no explanation of the legacies
1 CONC . Son Nathaniel received just half the money given to each of Robert's grandchildren and much less than the legacy given his brother Abraham. Nathaniel's association with Francis may be the explanation".
1 CONT Mrs. Betsy Errickson of Lambertville, N.J., who has made a considerable
1 CONT study of the Drakes is convinced, along with the writers of this document,
1 CONT that Captain Francis Drake was indeed the son of Robert Drake and that he was estranged from his father because of differences in religious beliefs. He did remove from Hampton, New Hampshire in 1668 where he and his wife, Mary Wal
1 CONC ker, began a huge family that has since spread throughout the whole country. Son Nathaniel received just half the money given to each of Robert's
1 CONT grandchildren and much less than the legacy given to his brother Abraham.
0 @N6000000004162114239@ NOTE Capt. FRANCIS DRAKE was not in his father's will, probably due to religion differences. He was Capt. in NJ Foot Colonial Militia in 1675; Justice of Peace and Judge of County Court, Middlesex Co., 1681; First Selectman of Piscataw
1 CONC ay, NJ.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources: Letter Dorothea Schweiger (file); Drake Family of NH; 217 Gen Books
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT http://worldconnect.rootsweb.com/cgi-bin/igm.cgi?op=GET&db=bosley-baylor&i d=I00519
1 CONT ============================================
1 CONT LDS Ancestral file gives Capt Francis a birth date of 1618.
1 CONT Clifford and Martha Hardin quote from C.I. Kephart on page 6:
1 CONT
1 CONT Governor Philip Cartaret, came to New Jersey in 1665 with a commission by the Proprietors as governor of the province. In the Autumn of 1665, he published a document of "Concessions and Agreements of the Lord Proprietors", wherein
1 CONC  were offered liberal inducements to populate the settlements, such as property in estates and liberty in religion. This was the motiviation for the migrations to Jersey.
1 CONT On December 18, 1666, John Martin, Charles Gilman, Hopewell Hull, and Hugh Dunn of Piscataqua New Hampshire, not far from Portsmouth, acquired for colonization 40,000 acres and founded the township and village of Piscataway, named
1 CONC  after their New Hampshire home. Less that two years afterward they were joined by Francis and Mary Drake, who came from the vicinity of Portsmouth. By 1690, land had been surveyed for George Drake, John Drake, Capt. Francis Drake
1 CONC , and Samuel Walker (the brother of Mary Walker, the presumed wife of Captain Frances Drake) "Piscataway NJ planters settled there for relief from the severities of court justice and the intolerance of the Established Church order
1 CONC  found in New Hampshire. From the first, Piscataway, NJ was a plantation of pious people-establishing permanent homes where they might enjoy the liberty of the gospel and the free exercise of their own spiritual convictions."
1 CONT
1 CONT Drake soon became an outstanding figure in the Piscataway colony. In addition to being a land-owner, he conducted a tavern. The records show that he was commissioned Captain of militia of Piscataway Township on 15 July 1675, and w
1 CONC as discharged at his own request on 30 May 1678. He was one of the first selectmen of Piscataway, was constable, justice of the peace, and in 1682 was county judge. He is credited with the ownership of 245 acres of land in 1690. (
1 CONC Monnette, pg 69-77)
1 CONT
1 CONT Captain Francis had at least three children who were born in New Hampshire and who moved with their parents to Piscataway, New Jersey in 1666. There may have been others who stayed behind in New Hampshire. Francis was buried in Po
1 CONC rtsmouth, New Hampshire, at "Olde Strawberrie Bank" (Monnette pg 326)
1 CONT
1 CONT It is said by many that the father of Captain Francis was Robert Drake of Colchester, Essex County, England. S.R. Williams, with references to Alice Smith Thompson, makes a good case for this argument. They postulate that Francis 
1 CONC and Robert were estranged because of Francis' conversion to the Baptist religion, and this is the reason that Francis and his family removed to New Jersey. Francis was not named in Robert's will, apparently due to these religious 
1 CONC differences. If Francis is the son of Robert Drake of Colchester, Essex County, England, his lineage would be: Robert-bap Halstead, Essex, England 23 July 1581, will proved 14 April 1668 at Salisbury,Mass. Recorded in Essex County
1 CONC ,Mass. His father: William Drake born 1553 at Halstead, Essex England, died 3 Nov 1616. Burial at Elmstead, Essex, England. Wife of William- Joan Merrylls, born 1557 at Halstead, Essex, England, died abt. 16 March 1617 at Elmstead
1 CONC , Essex, England. William's father was also a William Drake born abt 1518 at Great Waltham, Halstead, Essex. England; died 23 September 1555 at White Notley, Essex, England. The father of William Drake was Thomas Drake born before
1 CONC  19 May 1465 died in White Notley and buried there 27 Nov 1541, leaving widow Agnes Butler (widow of William Butler). She was buried there 30 September 1550. The father of Thomas Drake was William Drake, birth date unknown--died b
1 CONC efore 19 December 1510. William's parents were Edmund Drake,
1 CONT who died before 17 Oct 1471, and wife, Joan. Edmund's father was William Drake, who died in 1420. William's father was Robert Drake whose wife was Elen. Land transfers and court records seem to indicate that Robert died before 139
1 CONC 2, and Elen probably died in 1376, Nicholas Drake, the son of Nicholas and Agnes Drake was probably born at Great Waltham, Essex, England. Probable birth date between 1275-1290. He was married in 1302 at the time of his father's d
1 CONC eath.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Another theory about the ancestry of Francis Drake is found on page 5 of the manuscript by Clifford and Martha Hardin:
1 CONT Mrs. Betsy Errickson of Hopewell Museum in New Jersey, who is also a Drake descendant, has made a considerable study of the Drakes. She is convinced that Francis is indeed the son of a Robert Drake, and that he was estranged from 
1 CONC his father due to differences in religious beliefs. He did remove from Hampton, N.H. in 1668 with his wife, Mary Walker. But Betsy asserts that the Robert who was the father of Francis was from Devon and not Essex; that his parent
1 CONC s were William Drake of Yardbury who died there in 1625 and Phillippa Denys who died in 1655. William was the son of Robert Drake of Wiscomb who died in 1602. He in turn was the son of John Drake of Ashe who died in 1558 and his w
1 CONC ife, Amye Grenville, who died in 1578. John Drake was the son of John Drake of Exemouth and his wife, Margaret Cole. Their roots were deep in Devon, tracing finally back to John Drake, Esq. of Mount Drake.
1 CONT
1 CONT On Page 4, the Hardins publish a report taken from Monnette, "First Settlers", pg 1116:
1 CONT "Mr. Joseph Brown Turner, genealogist, Newark, Delaware, has visited and made personal researches in Devonshire re. the ancestry of Captain Francis Drake of Piscataway. He presents will records, indicating an entirely new lineage.
1 CONC  The will of John Drake of Wadden, part of Sowlie Co. of Devon, March 14, 1606, son Augustine, et al, a brother-in-law. Probate July 11, 1607."
1 CONT "Will of Augustine Drake, the Elder, of Exeter Co. of Devon, June 21, 1641. Among et al, grandson, Francis Drake. Proved July 20, 1641."
1 CONT "Again, will of Augustine Drake, of Ide Co. of Devon, merchant, Nov. 6, 1644, names sons, Augustine, William, John, Robert, Richard, dau. Agnes Drake, et al. and "my son, Francis Drake" et al. Proved December 31, 1646."
1 CONT "This Francis Drake is possibly, or probably, the Francis who came to New England, was at Portsmouth, N.H. in 1631, (1641) and died in New Jersey 25 September 1687."
1 CONT
1 CONT Still another version of the ancestry of Francis is found in Monnette's "First Settlers", pg 403.
1 CONT Mr. O.B. Leonard examined the records for the administration of the estate of a Francis DUKE of Piscataway. He concluded that the name was transcribed incorrectly, and should be Francis DRAKE. He entered a correction into the NJ A
1 CONC rchives(Vol. VIII, p 82) stating that the First Series, NJ Abstracts of Wills,( Vol XXIII pg 144) contains a typographical error. Francis (DUKE) should be DRAKE. He was the father of Capt. Francis Frake and Rev. John Drake.--O.B.L
1 CONC eonard.
1 CONT
1 CONT O.E. Monnette (Page 403) personally reviewed these same records with a magnifying glass, and said that the will record did, indeed say Francis Duke. But he says that there are no other records or reports for Piscataway that mentio
1 CONC n a Francis Duke. All the documents pertaining to this will were hand copied by the same clerk from four original pages. None of the original pages was to be found, nor was there a signature for this "Francis Duke" to be found. He
1 CONC  states that very frequently there are found spelling variations such as Deake, Drack, etc, and Duke as a variation is easily possible.
1 CONT
1 CONT In his conclusion, he states, "All the evidence, inherent in, and outside of this record,indicate that it was a FRANCIS DRAKE and not a FRANCIS DUKE, whose estate was administered upon in 1683. He was clearly an old man, weaver an
1 CONC d cloth-maker to the community and his account books show as debtors all the names of his neighbors and his contemporaneous well known settlers of Piscataway. The notable DRAKE representatives and their relatives were among them. 
1 CONC He was clearly a single man, perhaps a widower, without any children dependent on him, as each of the DRAKE settlers were then FIRST SETTLERS of the community and themselves heads of families. He could have easily have been the ol
1 CONC d father of CAPTAIN FRANCIS DRAKE, having removed with him from Portsmouth, New Hampshire. He could also, possibly, have been his uncle, a brother of ROBERT DRAKE, of Hampton, N.H. the now reputed father of said Francis Drake. If 
1 CONC the inherent evidence pointed to a younger man, he could have been a son of FRANCIS DRAKE....Many have followed the conclusions of Chambers, the first to assert that ROBERT DRAKE of N.H. was the probable father of CAPTAIN FRANCIS 
1 CONC DRAKE of PISCATAWAY, a First Settler there. Chambers presents reasonable logic and authority to that end. But they must explain FRANCIS DUKE, OR DRAKE, of PISCATAWAY, of the estate of 1683."
1 CONT
1 CONT The true ancestry of Francis Drake of Piscataway may never be proven.
1 CONT
