1 CONT Capt Francis Drake b 1615, probably in Devonshire, England. He served on grand jury in 1663 with Nathwniel in Portsmouth, N.H. Married Mary Walker, dau. of Hannah and Joseph Walker of Portsmouth, N.H. Francis (Capt) Drake son of R
1 CONC obert & ? Drake born 1625 died 9-24-1687 at Portsmouth,N.H. Married 1650 to Mary Walker, born at County Health Ireland. Died 7-29-1688 at Portsmouth N.H. "Their Children" #1.Elizabeth Drake born 1653 m:Hugh Dunn 12-19-1670 Hugh wa
1 CONC s born 1640 died 12-10-169? #2.John Drake born 1655 m. Rebecca Trotter on 7-7-1677. John died 4-7-1740W. Rebecca was born 1655 her parents were Catherine Gibb & William Trotter #3.George Drake born 1658 m:Mary Oliver on 11-13-1677
1 CONC . George died 11-8-17??. Mary was born c1658 parents were Mary Ackerley & William Oliver. #4.Francis Drake Jr. born?
1 CONT
1 CONT Given the name Francis Drake, there have been many who have speculated as to a familial connection between our ancestor and Sir Francis Drake (1540?-1596), the great Elizabethan Admiral. Again, there is absolutely no proof to subs
1 CONC tantiate this claim. It may have been that Francis was the grandson of Sir Francis' brother Thomas. Claims that he was a direct descendant are almost certainly spurious, since Sir Francis had no offspring (at least, no legitimate 
1 CONC offspring).
1 CONT
1 CONT Recent research reveals that Captain Humphrey Drake, brother of Colonel Robert Drake and both killed in Ostend, Nehtnerlands in 1600, wrote a letter to his brother, Nicholas in England asking that money from his will be used to su
1 CONC pport a son born to him by a gentle Dutch woman. Another brother, Henry married Anne Champerowne. Champerowne was a protector of Francis Drake in NH. MY theory is that Humphrey's son was George and he had Francis. The brothers wer
1 CONC e from the family of Robert Drake of Ashe family in Devonshire. The Francis Drake in Piscataway NJ who died about 1683 was a serge maker. The Francis Drake who died in 1687 was an entirely different personality. It is possible tha
1 CONC t the former Francis was a son of Nathaniel in NH because there Robert Drake was a serge maker. Our Francis Drake owned land "at the mouth of a fresh water creek" in NH. A boat owner? Albert N Drake saltpres@ix.netcom.com
1 CONT
1 CONT Many genealogists of note have speculated that Francis was Robert's son and that he was left out of his father's will due to religious differences. (Robert was a known Puritan and Francis a known Baptist.) Of course if there had b
1 CONC een such a familial religious conflict, Francis wouldn't have named any of his sons after his father. Taking the above into consideration, your statement in the fourth paragraph of your biography of Francis that there is evidence 
1 CONC "contrary" to there being a father-son bond between Robert and Francis has no basis in fact whatsoever and is at best speculative. In fact, there is much evidence, (much of which you yourself list in your biography,) which indirec
1 CONC tly links Francis to Robert. We know, for example, that Francis lived next door to and shared many civic duties with a known son of Robert's, Nathaniel.
1 CONT ==========================================
